The ČSSD in Prague knows neither Voldemort nor the pastry
The new ČSSD councillors in the capital are going to have to overcome, it seems, the obvious language barriers for the members of the Board of Prague and the Municipal Council to understand their renewed SDG comrades.
The Aktuálně.cz period "examined" the members of the new ČSSD Council to verify their knowledge of the slang-marked language that has emerged in recent years in the Council, when the current members of the coalition governed Prague.
The codified dictionary that used Prague's political flower and cream during the previous period, by Mayor Pavel Bém, includes some of the most well-known characters, situations and causes of the metropolis.
Surprisingly, it has been proven that the new councillors hardly understand those well-known concepts.
At least that's what they say.
"Who is Voldemort?"
"The truth is, I don't know."
"Rather I'm a rookie in Prague politics," Lukáš Kaucký said during the "collegueo" terminology examination.
And as far as the veteran politician was concerned, Karel Březina reacted.
Maybe you know the concept "golf hole".
"Possibly it's the only expression of all that you asked me that I use from time to time."
"If not, I don't normally use slang."
Del glossario, whose definitions published the newspaper Aktuálně.cz last June before the SDG voting congress, the wording chose six examples, particularly those that are especially related to the Prague municipal authorities.
The Socialists had to explain the concepts "Voldemort", "greater golf hole in Prague", "go to the bakery", "bullship", "treason of the capital" and "blindar".
Three councilors agreed to submit to the test and a fourth, Antonín Weinert, who in the future will be responsible for education in Prague, initially agreed, but did not pick up the phone again.
Fifth Councilman Petr Dolínek directly refused to do so.
"I don't get into these things and I don't want to assess how civic Democrats have fun."
"What matters to me is how we will understand each other in the execution of our programs," he said.
As has been seen, supposedly Councilman Lukáš Kaucký would not have realized that the previous title is an expression about an influential businessman and a friend of former Mayor Bém, Roman Janoušek.
Kaucký the word "Voldemort" doesn't tell him anything; the only term that hit the exam is "the greatest golf hole in Prague".
"It's about the Blanka tunnel," Kaucký says, adding immediately: "But rather I know it from the news."
"I'm going to have to aim for a language course," he jokes.
Karel Březina is not the only one who recognizes the Blanka Tunnal in the golf hole, but also the Councillor for the Environment Lukáš Plachý.
Afterwards, both socio-democrats know who Voldemort really is and Lukáš Plachý even knows where to find that character; in addition, he suspects the meaning of "going to the bakery".
"I think it means going to a company in Prague"; Plachý is soon revealed that it is the headquarters of Janoušek companies.
Like Březina, it indicates that such knowledge was obtained from the news, but does not use such a vocabulary.
According to the participants in the test, among the socio-democrats there is no glossary so familiar with colloquial terms.
"I asked my teammates, but it really seems like we don't have anything like that."
"Rather, among us we use nicknames, sometimes abbreviations or we make puns with the names," says the president of ČSSD Praguense, Petr Hulínský, whose party comrades call Hulda.
"At school they called me Bříza, but I don't know if my classmates also call me like that," confesses his own nickname Karel Březina.
Aktuálně.cz newspaper tested the name and works.
For his party partners, Weinert is Děda (similar to "yayo") or, less friendly, Dědek ("old"), Representative Miroslav Poche, with whom they partnered before the deceit elections during the party's sponsorship, receives the nickname "Igráček" ("playmobil") by some of his companions.
The nicknames are also the only expressions that socio-democrats tolerate in speech within the party.
Hulínský doesn’t like being called Hulda, but the Aktuálně.cz newspaper uncovered even other nicknames for this Praguese president.
"The doctor of baton rights" apparently recalls that Petr Hulínský obtained his doctorate in law at the Bratislava police academy.
The government wants to limit the torture of "witches", as published in a pamphlet
Women regarded as witches in Burkina Faso share certain characteristics.
They are usually elderly, cannot work, for example, for health reasons, are single and have no children.
Women who "become" witches are sent to an isolated area, so that they do not harm the rest of the village.
"Men seldom suffer only."
"It also does not isolate healthy young women, because they help in work and have children," explains a nun who, in the capital of West African state Burkina Faso, occupies a special residence of more than a hundred women expelled from their communities.
The local government has decided to oppose this unusual "social measure".
He issued an informative booklet that should contribute to avoiding witch hunting.
However, this has a hit.
“More than 70% of adults in Burkina Faso can’t read,” the Die Standard newspaper warns about the disadvantages of written sensitization.
Accusations of witchcraft are also common in other African countries.
In Gana, Burkina Faso’s neighbour, Ammy Hemmah, 72, died last week when three women and two men tortured her, covered her with gasoline and burned her.
The news appeared on the BBC.
The authors claim that it was due to an unfortunate cluster of circumstances.
According to her version, the woman caught herself in the oil that is used for ritual anointings.
It caught fire when the group wanted to "expell evil spirits" from the woman.
The isolation of “witches” is supported by people at all educational levels.
The newspaper Der Standard quoted a 42-year-old businessman from Burkina Faso: “Human soul devourers always posed a danger,” he said.
While in Burkina Faso the government tries to appease the witch hunt, in the Gambia last year the president still participated in it.
“Gambian President Jammeh, following the death of his aunt, launched an extensive witch hunt that had supposedly been related to his death and had tried to kill the president himself through black magic.”
“To help him he brought shamans from nearby Guinea who, protected by police, military and the president’s personal escort, forcibly forced men and women randomly chosen among the Gambian peoples to the president’s property in Kanilai,” Amnesty International said in its report.
On the estate, the arrested people had to drink a potion of herbs with hallucinogenic effects that, in addition, caused intestinal and kidney problems.
Two of those arrested did not survive the stay on the estate.
After international pressure, they finally liberated about a thousand detainees.
Bakala Tenant: Heated With Coal Stoves and Signs Higher Rental
The man, old and old, crossed the doors of the customer service center of RPG Byty of "coal mogul" Zdeňek Bakala, the largest private owner of apartments for rent in the Czech Republic.
Under his arm, he carried some documents with the new three-story rental contracts: his, that of his daughter and that of his son, who is serving sentence.
All three just caught the liberalization of rentals.
Instead of 3,500 Czech kroners a month, the son will pay from next year 4,100 kroners for a two-stay apartment with separate second-class cuisine.
"In addition, it does not have hot water and the heating is based on a solid fuel boiler," the man shows with his finger in his son's new contract, about whose signature he has full powers.
"But I signed it," explains half-angry, half-resigned.
The BytyOKD.cz civil association, which called the new rental conditions “incorrect and indecent,” advises tenants not to sign the new contracts and wait until Dec. 7, when it will publish a guide reporting on how to proceed in these cases.
But, in the face of this, the man only makes a gesture.
"Everyone says a thing," he says with a bad face.
Ostrava is becoming a great liberalization testing lab.
After New Year it will occur with the vast majority of the flats that, in the Czech Republic, were so far under a regulated rental.
That is why Aktuálně.cz decided to visit "laboratory" in person.
RPG’s mining waiting room is one of Ostrava’s four and, on Tuesday at nine o’clock, it was already full.
Right next to the doors are the security guards and next to him a machine to take numbers.
"Rent 2011", can be read on a luminous button on your touch screen.
When pressed, a paper with a number comes out of the machine.
It almost doesn’t even give time to read “157” and already the number appears on the large screen located at the top of the room.
In a second, you're in the queue.
Shortly after, an elderly marriage arrives to ask, but the crowd of the room is not here for the new rentals.
Most people come to solve usual problems, such as previously: requests for settlements, insurance issues and similar issues.
Letters with the new rental contracts will arrive in Ostrava to the mailboxes of tenants of most of the 44,000 apartments belonging to RPG around mid-November.
RPG Byty proposes in the letters an increase in rent of between 15 and 38%.
On November 18, a special window is opened in the customer service center for new rentals.
"It's been running for a week, so it's beginning," they say, after briefly hesitating, the workers who are in it when the client introduces himself as a journalist.
Bakala’s liberalization and former mining flats are the most sensitive issues in the area.
Prior to the elections, they have already been treated loud and clear by the Vice-President of Parliament, originally from Ostrava, Lubomír Zaorálek of the ČSSD, and they now appear continuously in the newspapers in the area.
"Twenty years later I want new soil."
"They didn't even change the fucking dish of the bathroom," the man mentioned in the introduction vents.
According to the workers, at the moment they come mostly retired to check if they receive a discount on rent if they sign on time, as the special RPG offer promises.
It will also receive half of the February rental discount the remaining tenants who sign the new contract before December 15.
However, the window of mining "liberalization" undoubtedly fails to dispel the avalanche of doubts.
"Of the 360 customers who were here yesterday, they only asked about forty or fifty people for rent in 2011," estimates the employee of the window.
Meanwhile, RPG proposes on the website and on its planks to hold individual meetings on contracts when necessary.
If the two parties do not reach an agreement on renting, an independent judge must resolve the issue.
"It's not a threat, but information about the only way of proceeding possible and completely in accordance with the law," RPG warns.
"No one will be judged here," says one of the RPG Byty employees and a man on the street in front of the customer service center gives him, at least in part, the reason.
"I receive a pension of 13,000 and I have 5,000 left for food; I have no means to go to court," he says.
But, immediately, the employee sows doubt.
"We'll see."
"Mr. Zaorálek warns them in the media that they don't sign, so maybe people will wait," he says.
Rental regulation will cease to exist in the majority of the Czech Republic at the beginning of 2011 and, according to RPG Byty, its increase is an economic necessity.
“Current rentals don’t yet cover even the amounts necessary for the maintenance of the community fund,” recalls Pavel Klimeš, director of RPG Byty’s residential portfolio, adding that the sloppy state of the community fund that angers tenants is, for landlords, the main reason for increasing rent.
While during the five years between 2005 and 2010 RPG was able to invest in apartments four billion, in the four years that will go from 2011 to 2014 the figure will increase to 5.200 million.
The large screen that hangs from the ceiling, in front of the seats of the waiting room, now repeats some data that stands out in red.
"The increase in rent in 2011 by 15% and less applies to 31,200 apartments, that is, more than 90% of RPG properties."
"In addition, it will always be at most 7.50 Czech kroners per square meter," it is indicated on the screen.
Only 38% increase in the rental of 400 flats from the RPG portfolio.
"In such cases, this is an increase from extremely low amounts of 19.66 kronor per square meter," RPG said.
At 2,600 apartments, the rental will remain unchanged next year.
RPG Byty promises that liberalization won’t suddenly affect rents, but will escalate four to eight years.
In addition, he adds the following commitment: the company will invest all the money of raising the rent (see the graph) again in the flats, mainly in savings in heating: new windows and heating.
Priority will be given to people who sign the rent increase agreement.
The architects have understood that offices also need kindergarten
The Avriopoint administrative building in Brno is the new headquarters of Arch.Design.
No one would say that it also hides inside a small nursery with all the same to such installations: colorful walls, creative furniture and even a miniature park on the outdoor terrace.
Since October last year, children of Arch.Design employees can come to it.
The architectural project company Arch.Design, from whose studio numerous known buildings of Brno came out, thought from the first sketches of the Avriopoint building to include a space for its mini-key shop.
About 90 people work in the company, mainly in the age of the baby boom and wanted to allow architects and delineants to soon return to work after motherhood.
Along with the possibility to enjoy a reduced schedule, the nursery is one of the benefits offered by Arch.Design to its workers within the framework of its social program.
Builders and architects in this way have the possibility to combine a demanding profession with the care of children.
The mini-nursery was an idea of Arch.Designu's own employees and, with the support of management, managed to obtain funding from the European Union and the Ministry of Labour and Social Affairs.
The company’s mini-karestore is financed through the European Social Fund under the Operational Programme on Human Resources and Employment, as well as the State budget of the Czech Republic.
Thanks to the funding granted (an endowment of more than 2.8 million Czech kroners) they were able to purchase the equipment and, after two years, hire qualified personnel and services.
When the financing is exhausted (two years), the company Arch.Design has an obligation to keep the mini-nursery running with its own funds for three years.
The preparation of the project lasted one year and in the submission of the financing application the company Economy Rating contributed.
The care of the children is in the hands of qualified personnel; in addition, a nurse and a teacher from the Ministry of Education are present all the day for their care. The nursery currently welcomes children without an age limit, although most of them are less than three years old.
Since it is a "mini-garderie", it can only simultaneously accommodate a maximum of five children, althus with shifts come more during the day.
The operation of daycare is flexible to adapt to the needs of parents and children of all workers have the same right to use kindergarten, regardless of the hierarchy within the company.
Although it is individual education and care, the contents of the mini-nursery programme are comparable to the regular pre-school education programme.
In it children not only play, but also receive quality teaching: from basic learning to school preparation, they teach to English.
The class has not only toys and board games, but also a computer for children.
The presence of young children in the company itself creates a friendly and positive environment.
Parents can go see their children at any time and avoid the stress of picking up school kids late when they are away from their workplace.
Parents are more productive while working.
It’s not surprising that this naturally generates a loyalty relationship between the worker and their employer.
In this way, Arch.Design is placed among the companies that offer a great advantage for the admission of new workers: they support the family and, in short, this program reinforced personnel marketing.
The bone house continues to fascinate after 100 years. Observe the interiors of Gaudí
The roof looks like the back of a prehistoric lizard, the ladders reminiscent of a dinosaur’s spine, and the balconies look like the mouth of an open fish.
This is the Casa Batlló, in Barcelona, by the Catalan architect Antoni Gaudí.
Due to its skeleton-like shape, the locals nicknamed it "the bone house."
Its organic shapes, its striking colors, the novel use of the stone, the fragments of tiles and mosaics, a façade reminiscent of the scales and the ubiquitous gently curved lines, all this is typical of the secession of Antoni Gaudí.
The cover of the house plays with dream lines, delicate colors and textures taken from nature and fauna.
Paying more attention, you observe a certain geometry, rhythm and a carefully thought order.
At night, the whole facade of the house seems even more impressive, since it is illuminated in such a way that the brightness of the colors is improved.
Coil and the torsion of the shapes continues to surprise us also inside.
Corrugated wood copies window frames, railings, door ornaments, peepholes and signs.
Casa Batlló, which is located in the Eixample neighborhood at number 43 of the lively shopping street of Passeig de Grácia, emerged from the remodeling of an 1877 house by the architect Emilio Sala Cortés.
The reconstruction lasted two years and the house was completed in 1906.
The investor, the rich industrialist Josep Batlló, initially insisted on knocking down the previous building and starting from scratch, but he soon accepted Gaudí’s idea of carrying out a novel and cheaper reconstruction.
However, Casa Batlló, formed by several apartments, was not designed by Antoni Gaudí alone.
The architect Josep María Jujol collaborated in design.
In fact, in his plans of the project arose ornaments for the apartment representative of the owner of the property and the mosaics used on the facade.
Other creators and artists on the façade also workers.
Collaboration in Casa Batlló’s proposals was the beginning of Jujol’s successful collaboration with Gaudí.
Jujol participated in the construction of Casa Milá and Gaudí’s Güell park, in which other pieces of it are located.
Due to its organic form, the locals shortly after its completion nicknamed it "the bone house".
Others, due to the specific mechanism of opening the windows began to call it "the house of yawns".
It is also said that, at the top of the construction, appears the sword of St. George, patron saint of Catalonia.
Other sculptural elements of construction hide religious motives.
Gaudi designed in the house a unique system for the central heating season with ventilation vents and typical fireplaces.
His forty-five-degree inclination may be strange.
During the construction, Gaudí did not at all respect the construction rules of the moment and did not allow himself to be influenced by them.
Even so, the construction obtained the corresponding permit from the beginning, although obviously in it the data indicated on the planned reconstruction were so vague that, two years later, at its end, the city council was so impacted with the result that it asked to be corrected.
It bothered him, for example, that the construction included columns even up to the public sidewalk.
The arrangements were never made, on the contrary: just fifteen days after the request of the city council, Josep Batlló requested (and obtained) permission to rent the top two floors of the building.
On the lower floors the owner himself was installed.
In the gallery of the second floor was the living room and on the third floor the bedrooms and the private part of the apartment.
The ground floor was rented from a jewelry store.
The six-story house is completely traversed by a staircase that leads to the roof terrace.
The basic elements of reformation consisted of breaking all angles and straight lines, covering them with curved walls.
Gaudí also worked with the modern concept of space connection.
The common part can be connected completely by opening the doors of the salons and a larger group can communicate in this way smoothly, without giving up the privacy necessary for more intimate conversations.
The house, with the reconstruction of Gaudí, not only obtained a new façade, but also an unusual roof and two additional floors.
It is also impressive how Gaudí plays with light, white and blue tiles that cover the inner courtyard and skylights.
The game with the two colors provides the maximum light on all the floors of the house.
It was based simply on the principle that white reflects more light than dark colors, which on the contrary absorb the sun rays.
Anyone can visit Casa Batlló, which in 2005 was added to UNESco’s World Heritage list.
On the first floor of the house is the most spacious and more recharged apartment of those accessible to the public.
The owners decided to open the doors to the public in 2002, on the occasion of the 150th anniversary of the birth of the famous creator.
With the visits they confirmed, or discovered, that it is a very beneficial business and extended visits to the common area and attic, including the roof terrace.
The price of admission to the monument is not insignificant, it reaches 16.50 euros, although students, retirees and holders of tourist cards get a 20% discount.
The amount considerably exceeds the price of entry of other Gaudí constructions in the city.
If you want to enjoy the true atmosphere of the bed with its lizard towers, you should quickly befriend a resident of the neighboring houses and visit him at home.
How to choose the mattress and somier: women and men are looking for different things
Nothing of a pompous frame for the bed; what really confers its quality to a good bed is the surface to lie down, i.e. the mattress and somier.
Above all they must be complemented perfectly so that they adapt to specific users.
Numerous elements come into play during the election, including weight, height, state of health and sex of the owner.
It is also important the type of interior in which the mattress and somier are located.
A typical example?
For example, in a weekend cabin that doesn’t usually have heating, latex mattresses shouldn’t be used because they “warm up” more slowly.
In addition, they tend to weigh considerably more than the usual polyurethane mattresses.
Therefore, it is necessary to turn them around more frequently.
If the central channels are not properly aerated and, above, they are on a firm basis, they keep moisture inside. Even mold may appear underneath.
When it comes to choosing a bed, customs also influence.
Anyone who has spent a lot of time abroad often uses a very tall mattress with a soft coat and will have trouble feeling good on a lower, harder surface.
“Almost all of the hockey players who came back from the NHL complained that they didn’t sleep as well here.”
"For their crushed shoulders, heels and hips they needed mattresses with a soft layer of viscoelastic."
"These types of mattresses are also recommended for people with diabetes what time nervous system problems, so they get bad information about the joints and skin," explains Sylva Hadamcziková, who specializes in quilted furniture.
Many hockey players, like other clients, have long preferred viscoelastic mattresses.
"There's no problem today anymore, because they have these suitable Tempur and the Italians Magniflex, but also the Czech brand Tropico."
You don’t have to be an NHL player to appreciate the viscoelastics.
This "miracle" works very simply.
Optimizes its rigidity and shape depending on the heat absorbed or adapts to its body heat.
Nothing exerts pressure; during the night, he won't spin like after a tough game.
It just sleeps better.
Viscoelastics also functions as protection against sores.
When choosing the size of the mattress, add to its height the length of its feet, i.e. between 21 and 34 cm.
A man or woman with a height of about 180 cm will need a bed between 210 and 220 cm.
The minimum width for one person should be, in a double room, 90 cm, in total 180 cm.
In the case of single rooms, for example of singles, it is recommended at least between 120 and 140 cm.
So that a possible visit is also comfortable.
"If not, you can apply a simple standard to calculate the width: when lying on your back, you should have your hands comfortably shrunken towards your elbows," advises engineer Helena Prokopová, an expert from Cechu čalouníků to dekoratérů, who has been dealing with the problems of healthy sleep and the ergonomics of furniture for many years.
In the double rooms, everyone needs their own somier and mattress, so during the purchase you have to pay attention to combine the height of the mattresses.
Otherwise, it will be necessary to mount the box springs at different heights, which is not always possible in all beds.
Foam mattresses should have a minimum height of 14 cm, models with inside springs at least 15 cm, of which at least three centimeters are for the layer that adapts to the shapes and softens.
"When touching it with your hand or settling into the mattress, you certainly shouldn't notice the springs," Helena Prokopová warns.
Each mattress requires a certain type of somier.
Completely padded bedrooms, in which the mattress and the base are like Siamese, make no sense in the bedroom.
Mattresses breathe badly and you can’t wash their cover, which today is often done.
And, since every night we perspire between 0.2 and 0.75 l of sweat, it is clear that after about five or seven years the mattress will begin to remind us of a large (and congested) sieve.
For this reason, specialists reject slogans that talk about a mattress life of up to 25 years or even a lifetime guarantee.
However, everything has a dog.
That’s why vendors give advice: on mattresses with a width of 120 or 140 cm, take careful care of the cleaning and washing of the cover, but better add a mattress protector that can be easily removed and washed.
Although mattress sleeves with zip closure are currently manufactured throughout their perimeter, so when opened they stay in two parts (something very important for the washing and size of the washing machine!), their manipulation in the larger models (and by a single person) when lifting the mattress may seem like a fight between a fly weight and a heavy weight.
In bed, instead of a somier, do you have a firm board?
Never!
At most of the beds designed for you to spend one night the visits.
However, you should make (if you don’t already have them) venting holes in the board and it should be used with a mattress with springs inside and a minimum height of 15 cm with springs in bags (such as German Taschenfederkern) and a soft layer.
Polyurethane mattresses should not be used on such bases.
For non-articulable sheet box springs containing between 14 and 18 sheets, latex mattresses or sandwich mattresses (e.g., a combination of latex and coconut fiber) may be used, although a box spring with a greater number of sheets, 28 or more, in which the sheets of the lumbar area are reinforced and their rigidity may always be regulated.
Articulable sheet box springs should not be used with a spring mattress, since it does not react to reclining.
In addition to the adjustment of the stiffness of the sheets, shoulder protection can also be used.
It is a special fixation of the sheets that allows you to press or even tilt the sheets in such a way that they "surround" perfectly to the shoulders during sleep.
For men who have very wide shoulders, it is something very important.
They are even remembered by mattress makers who, in the "masculines", began to use a softer area precisely around their shoulders.
They also remember women, for whom they put a softer area around their hips.
For the most discerning customers, there are breaches with cross or sleeping structures with so-called multi-torsion damping.
The pressure is distributed between different points and the voltage is lowered.
You should also keep in mind that, even if you use the appropriate mattress and somier, if you use a bad pillow (large and "filful"), most of the advantages will disappear.
The basic standard is simple: the pillow must hold the head and the corners can only reach the shoulders, so they can relax their muscles.
The appropriate recommended size is 50 x 60 cm, something normal for example in Scandinavia.
When lying on the side, the pillow should be at the same level (when combined with the bean with shoulder reinforcement or the softer area of the mattress) than the height of the shoulders.
Modern mattresses have something to do, it seems, with the original mattresses stuffed with straw.
Manufacturers continue to use, more and more often, natural materials or at least mixtures.
In fact, biological mattresses are fashionable.
Petroleum-based raw materials, with which polyurethane foam is manufactured, are filled with natural oils, e.g. castor or soybeans.
In fact, virtually all known manufacturers use soybeans.
"Biological, in the case of mattresses, means that the center is made only of materials of natural origin," explains Petr Vašíček of the company Tropico Plus.
In addition, natural substances are used during the manufacture of the mattress cover, one of the first aloe vera.
But you can also opt for microcapsules, which release a fresh scent to forest or lavender.
There are also differences for men and women.
While men "refresh" with breeze plant extracts, so that the mattress cover absorbs the heat and refreshes the body, women prefer to "warm up" the sheath.
Red pepper extract improves blood circulation, so women feel more warmth.
The favorites currently?
Mattresses made of cold foam, which are more porous, with an adapted cell structure, which makes them more permeable than those of normal polyurethane and offer more permeability.
The combination of various stiffnesses of cold and viscoelastic foam allow to manufacture mattresses of various areas that know perfectly where the head, shoulders and heels go.
A good quality mattress will cost you seven thousand kroners up.
Those who play a therapeutic function usually have a lower VAT, since they are considered as sanitary means.
The first who gained this advantage in the Czech Republic was Gumotex.
It’s tricky to save on mattresses and somieres.
Sometimes you can approve the offers of manufacturers or sellers, who sometimes offer two mattresses for the price of one.
For example, the Magniflex brand offers Duofreeze mattresses with a Memory memory foam attached to the case by 11,990 crowns.
The second one is available free of charge.
The era of viscoelastic with an open cellular structure and a high density began with the "cosmic" Tempur material.
It initially emerged as a material for NASA, because during take-off the astronauts were subjected to an enormous overload.
Tempur softens with the heat and hardens with the cold.
Consequently, the human body, warm, exerts a perfect effect and optimally adapts its pressure.
Therefore, it is ideal to prevent sores from occurring, but also to improve comfort during sleep.
Viscoelastic foam is not only used in mattresses, but also for medical devices, for example for pillows that are placed under the legs and relieve them, as well as their circulation.
Domestic manufacturers, for example, use Eucatherm C or Twinsaver foam and the like.
More and more parents are employing people-tracking devices to monitor their children
Fear for children leads more and more people to think about controlling them by GPS.
The system, initially designed for vehicle monitoring, can also follow the movement of people and thus contribute, for example, in case of the disappearance of nine-year-older Anička Janatková.
At the moment the device has the approximate size of a matchbox, but it will soon be reduced to the size of a watch.
Many people were afraid of the case of the missing Anička and began to report on the means available to better protect their children.
Even at the cost of invading your privacy.
"We all try to tell ourselves that it's not about spying, but about another means to decrease the chances of a tragedy," says Petr Pilin of Macro Weil, one of the providers of tracking devices in the Czech market.
"I recognize that, in certain cases, such devices can be abused, for example if they get into someone's purse without their knowledge, but we have not yet run into such cases."
“Most customers really buy it for security reasons,” Pilin adds.
In his opinion, children should always know what the "cajita" is for and how it is used in case of an emergency.
Child psychologist Dana Bočková warns about the fact that if the child doesn’t want to wear it, she’ll simply throw it away.
"Even younger children show great creativity when it comes to intentionally losing certain things if they want to prevent them from controlling them."
“Before, parents relied heavily on mobile phones, but they could be turned off as easily as the tracking device,” Bočková says.
In his opinion, it is debatable if in the event of an attack such a device is really useful.
"If it's a prepared abduction, the perpetrator will practically always check if the child wears any kind of electronic device that can reveal its location."
"It's the phone now."
"As the use of the tracking devices is extended, it will also look for them, since their sale will undoubtedly be done with a lot of advertising."
"In such cases, criminals are always one step ahead of the police," the psychologist says.
However, he adds that such devices can help in the event of an improvised attack by a pedophile.
According to Robert Čech of the company RC Monitoring, which offers tracking services for both vehicles and people, even in the event that, after an attack, the tracking device is immediately destroyed, its presence can help.
"You can configure the device to regularly send the position after a certain period."
"If you suffer damage, it will at least facilitate the last position of the person in question."
"The most expensive devices have an integrated tilt meter, so if the person simply slips and stays immovable, the device will immediately issue a warning with the position in question," says Čech.
In his opinion, such a service can be obtained even with smart mobile phones with navigation, in which only special software must be installed.
The problem with current tracking units is that at the moment they are devices with a size similar to a matchbox or smaller mobile phones.
Therefore, they can be lost.
According to Pilin de Macro Weil, for that reason tests are currently underway with devices that look like a digital clock.
"In addition, they will have the added advantage that a potential assailant may not identify the device."
"Or identify it after some time, which can cause the police to gain time," he adds.
Although the tracking devices are worn on the wrist or hung, they will continue to use the same technology used for vehicle tracking.
It is the verification of the position with a GPS system (which is used in the usual navigation in the car) combined with the function of a mobile phone that communicates the current position.
The army has enough wicks to encircle all of the Czechy, but they only last two years
The Czech army bought 445 kilometers of wicke to turn on explosives.
Last year the Ministry of Defense added 224 kilometers of fuse to its arsenal.
Therefore, they have stock for 225 years.
The problem is that the wick’s shelf life is two years, four at most.
The army bought the wick for explosives from the STV Group, a. s. for forty million kroners.
General staff responded to MF DNES justifiing the purchase of such a huge amount of wick by saying that soldiers need for training maneuvers 60 kilometers of wick a year.
According to the calculations of the pyrotechnicians of the army, with whom MF DNES contacted, it is decided, however, that, in this case, they should carry out such maneuvers with frequent use of explosives each year also all the generals, high command officers, and even secretaries and cleaners.
They didn’t even want Defense Minister Alexandr Vondr and army captain Vlastimil Picek.
Only in this way could it be justified that the army spends sixty kilometers of fuse.
Such a length equals 25,000 explosions.
Even so, the army would have sufficient stocks for more than ten years.
If on the contrary, as experts say, for the initial training of each new professional arrives with a maximum of two meters of wick, the army would have with the new purchase (even adding the consumption of the units of engineers or special) sufficient stock for the 225 indicated above.
The soldiers of the rapid intervention brigade complained recently to Minister Vondra because they say that they barely have ammunition for various weapons, so even shooting practices are limited for money.
Don’t talk about the lack of underwear.
"I also can't understand that they repeatedly buy us such amounts of wick."
"But it's their decision, and they'll certainly know why they act like this."
“For us, the military is a good customer that, as a company, we try to satisfy,” admits STV Group’s commercial director Jan Děcký.
Defense Ministry spokesman Jan Pejšek defends the hundreds of kilometers of wick, also saying that he will have stocks of this "munition" for at least ten years.
However, Jan Děcký of STV Group indicates that the fuse only has a lifetime of four years and, in the case of pyrotechnic products for the army or police, two years.
“If it’s stored in perfect conditions, it can last up to four,” says one of the soldiers who, for possible criticism from the army, doesn’t want to give his name.
After the end of its useful life, each ammunition must be carefully checked to see if it remains reliable and will not harm the soldiers.
But these tests are not free and require additional investment.
Another argument of the Ministry is that, by buying 445 kilometers of fuse, it obtained a discount.
"They cost us 60% less than last year's 244-kilometre order," Defense Department spokesman Pejšek said.
Just to compare: the annual fuse consumption of police pyrotechnicians, who eliminate World War II ammunition or that left by the Soviet army, has not already exceeded 1,500 meters for years.
In addition, the police always asks only for the right amount to exhaust the ammunition before its useful life ends.
They decided it for safety reasons for pyrotechnicians and, in addition, in the meantime you can get more modern and reliable types of wick.
The Ministry already has clear difficulties to finish tens of thousands of tons of ammunition from the Warsaw Pact era, when the Czechoslovak army of two hundred thousand people was preparing for a campaign in the Rhine.
According to the pyrotechnicians with whom MF DNES spoke, after a couple of years there will be the following problem with the current one: it will be necessary to eliminate hundreds of kilometers of old wick, which is being bought now "cheap".
So far I still live on the contacts I got thanks to the contest
Winning the "Entrepreneur of the Year" contest helped Lukáš Pytloun in the sale of his hotel franchises.
The last edition won the "Entrepreneurs of the Year" contest the 28-year-old entrepreneur Lukáš Pytloun de Liberec.
His success story seems taken from a movie.
Lukáš Pytloun loved to travel.
Abroad he realized that in Liberec you needed quality accommodation at a good price.
With the savings he met with seasonal jobs abroad and a credit from the bank bought a house that was half-sleeted composed of five floors.
Considering that he was stilly studying his third year in college, he was really brave.
"At present, the quality-to-price ratio of accommodation in our area prompted me to do something about it," says Lukáš Pytloun.
He started renting the rebuilt first floor to foreign workers.
All the income he earned was invested again in the building, which he gradually converted into a 54-bedroom boarding house.
Since then, in seven years in the business, he gradually managed to open in Liberec four pensions, two stylish restaurants and two hotels with a total capacity of around 300 in all accommodation categories.
The most comfortable of all was, until recently, the four-star Pytloun design hotel.
However, this year opened another one of the same quality.
“We have just signed a contract with a hotel in Ostrov or Tisé, to which we’ve sold our franchise concept, and other people are showing interest in a Pytloun franchise,” said last year’s number one entrepreneur.
"To this sale of licenses for the opening of hotels greatly helped to win the 'Empressury of the Year' contest," Pytloun adds.
The victory boosted his fame and popularity both in and around Liberec.
"People recognized me on the street, they were still congratulating me often and, I must say, it was very pleasant," he recalls.
Undoubtedly, he advises everyone who wants to try some company that participates in the contest.
But he thinks there is no need to win, but that participation is already beneficial.
He affirms that, so far, he continues to live by contacts and acquaintances that, without the contest, he might not have obtained.
"With some people I met thanks to the contest I've usually started to collaborate."
The fame associated with success, in his opinion, also included in the employees,
in total around seventies.
"I've seen that they show greater loyalty and are proud to participate in something that gained overall recognition."
“It’s not my own personal success, it’s the result of the hard work of the entire team.”
"It's just that it's my name that appears on the front page," Lukáš Pytloun says modestly today.
To my successor I wish him a quieter year than we have had at Farmet
Euphoria for victory soon dissipated over the impact of the crisis.
Thanks to the hard work of the victor, it is possible that Farmet has already had the worst.
The owner of the Farmet company, Karel Žďárský, received the first prize in the last edition of Vodafone's "Company of the Year" contest, last year in the "Eslava House" in Prague.
The jury valued his efforts, with which he managed to turn a small technical workshop into Česká Skalice into a firm with a turnover of hundreds of millions.
"I can say that the award not only encouraged me, but all my collaborators and improved our confidence to fight the competition," says last year's winner.
The history of the Farmet company begins with the desire to do something useful.
For this reason, when the State proposed to Karel Žďárský’s father the rental of a small workshop as compensation for the property embargo during communism, he did not hesitate too much; he resigned his position in the company in which he worked and, with a couple of colleagues, they founded the company with their father of plastic batteries.
Shortly thereafter, they began specializing in vegetable oil presses.
Eight years later they became the largest manufacturer of automatic presses in central Europe.
They also obtained a greater presence among the non-specialized public after achieving success in the competition in the media.
“Not only did our customers begin to consider the Farmet brand as considerably better, but also foreign partners began to value collaboration with a company that achieved greater success in their country,” says Karel Žďárský.
Last year maybe it wasn’t at Farmet just a sign of the euphoria of victory.
It also gained recognition for commercial results until 2008 and, partially, 2009.
And just the year 2009 was the toughest year for the sale of machinery.
The worsening crisis, which had not yet reached so many citizens, caused companies to stop their investment projects and begin to limit the purchase of new machines or technologies.
"We obviously don't get rid of such a situation either."
"Revenues fell by 40%, we had to limit production and we even had a few layoffs," explains Karel Žďárský.
A viable company must sometimes take even unpopular measures.
However, they controlled the situation and maintained the profits of the company, albeit at lower levels than before the crisis.
However, where Farmet didn’t cut expenses was in research and development.
"With the grants we have built a research and training center, reinforced the presence in the international market and obtained great valuations at national fairs," says Žďárský.
Everything indicates that they have already left the crisis behind.
Revenues rise again and, with them, the number of new employees.
"The new achievement expect it to bring a feeling of victory, but in a calmer atmosphere," says Karel Žďárský.
A survey that unveils the ridiculous rules
Absurdity of the Year’s survey is part for the fourth time of the business tournament.
He has already managed to eliminate some ridiculous rules.
The unknown winner of the new Absurdity of the Year survey was the obligation to bring to the administration documents that officials can access online.
The decision was adopted by more than a third of the nearly four thousand voters.
For example, the social security administration requested an excerpt from the commercial registry, although its officials can obtain it with two mouse movements.
Thanks to the survey, this has changed.
"The Czech social security administration will no longer ask for an extract from the commercial register when carrying out a check on the impositions of the insured," ČSSZ spokeswoman Jana Buraňová said.
The objective of the Absurdity Survey of the year is to consult the rules and regulations that must be respected by companies and entrepreneurs in the Czech Republic and to try to eliminate those that are absurd.
This year forms for the fourth time part of the business tournament of the publication Hospodářské noviny and begins to create fans also among the general public.
While last year’s edition had the participation of three thousand voters, this year it practically reached four thousand.
And it is not surprising, since we still have several legal obligations that must be respected simply to trade.
"Perhaps in number of papers necessary for the tax return we are in the first place in the world," says Karel Havlíček of the Association of small and medium-sized companies and entrepreneurs of the Czech Republic.
The simplification of business standards has been a declared objective of all governments for years.
However, compared to the rest of the world, the situation of improvement especially in the Czech Republic.
According to the World Bank, from the point of view of the conditions to have a company, Chequia ranks 74th, that is, eight positions worse than last year.
The Absurdity of the Year competition was held for the first time in 2006 and is repeated in the same way each year.
Anyone can indicate any administrative or bureaucratic absurdity each year between April and the end of September, when the contest is closed.
The proposals can be made on the website www.firmaroku.cz. Then, the organizer of the contest chooses seven candidates for the "anti-premium" and, during October, allows the public to vote on the Internet through www.firmaroku.cz/absurdita and www.ihned.cz.
It wins the absurdity that receives more votes.
For this year’s survey, more than a hundred were presented, in particular 105 rules and regulations that do not make sense.
The examples were submitted by both businessmen and professional associations and journalists.
Among the "finalists" of the contest was, for example, the requirement that vehicles for the maintenance of roads and highways pay the toll or that entrepreneurs with twenty employees, two offices and two bathrooms paint the walls every two years.
This year’s list of seventy proposals is published on the pages of the contest.
The announcement of the contest, which is always made at the beginning of November in Wenceslas Square in Prague, is part of an event in which personalities from the political and social world usually participate.
This year, for example, Eva Svobodová, director of the Association of Small and Medium Companies and Entrepreneurs of the Czech Republic, or Petr Kužel, president of the Chamber of Commerce of the Republic, attended this event.
"Economic prosperity cannot be sustained if the state continuously hinders entrepreneurs, so my objective is to eliminate every year a minimum of thirty paragraphs that currently unnecessarily complicate commerce," announced the Minister of Industry and Commerce Martin Kocourek.
Efforts to eliminate absurd regulations don’t stay in borage water.
In 2007, what bothered people the most was the obligation of the entrepreneur to communicate three days in advance to the business office of the area of permanent operation, the intention to go with a position to a fair or exhibition.
This obligation was eliminated in the new industrial law.
The obligation that ended second in number of votes in 2007, that is, the communication to the work office by its foreign workers of work trips of up to 30 days, was eliminated in the law of the so-called green card.
And the third goes the vanquished.
The Absurdity of 2009 was the regulation for physiotherapists to have in their practice a large mirror, a refrigerator and two floor scales.
For this reason, this year the Ministry of Health issued a communication saying that it had eliminated the obligation to have a refrigerator.
A new category recognizes socially responsible companies
Ecological, humanitarian, educational or sporting projects are valued.
The novelty in this fifth edition of the Hospodářské noviny contest is the category called Company responsible for 2010.
The task of this competition is to support responsible and fair companies.
"In this way we want to support companies that deal with the impact of their activity on the environment and that support socially responsible activities," says Patrik Bartholdy, regional director of the mobile operator of Vodafone, organizer and sponsor of the contest, as in the case of the Rebell contest of the year.
For this new special subcategory of this year’s edition of Vodafone Empresa’s main contest of the year 2010, companies were presented for which commercial success is not the only long-term objective.
The story of each of them is inspiring and would allow us to write an independent article.
Although the type of activities varies completely between one and the other, the common denominator is the support and consideration they offer.
In one case, it is for example disabled people, in others about personal or health problems of employees.
Among the fourteen finalists from all over the Republic was, for example, the Prague software company Et Netera, which supports the formation of informal sports teams among its workers, who later participate in tournaments of various sports.
Another example of company responsible for the Pardubice area is the company Isolit-Bravo, which has already been the main contributor of funds to sponsor disabled children for seven years.
In Poděbrad, there is a stationery that is particularly concerned about the health problems of its workers, since they constitute more than 70% of the workforce; that is why he received the title of Company responsible for the central area of Chequia and in the Vodafone Company of the year he finished in third place in the region.
At Olomouc he won the accounting firm MiRyz Soft, which reduced working hours, even below the average of 35 hours per week, without reducing wages.
The title was also granted because it buys priority from Czech manufacturers, supports community activities in the area and respects the environment.
"We try to recycle as much as possible so that our production and management activities produce the least possible impact on the environment," explains entrepreneur Miluše Ryznarová.
The IT company Enlogit of the Ústí region also thinks about the health of its employees and, with each laptop, offered them an ergonomic chair and keyboards; in addition, it supports working from home and in its business events uses the catering services of a local protected company.
"In addition, we support the Persona scholarship in distress, which specifically helps the local Roma community," says the representative of the company Ondřej Suchý.
They are also placed in a good place with a mention the laundry company Praní to čištění from the Karlovy Vary region, whose employees receive bonuses in a special way that have an impact on their pension funds.
"We give benefits in food, transportation, improvement of qualifications and leisure," says Josef Strapáč, of the Bruntál Czasch construction company, winner in the Moravia-Silesia region.
In the category of Responsible Company of the year, all regional finalists of the Vodafone Company of the year contest were automatically appointed and sent to the organizers before August 15.
Organizers removed forms that were not completed or wrong from the evaluation.
"If the representatives of the competing companies did not answer the questions, they could not participate in the company contest responsible for the year, but they are not disqualified from the "main" Vodafone Company of the year contest."
"Individual nominations for this contest are not allowed, since it is a subcategory of the main company of the year contest," explains Karel Goldman of the Bison & Rose consultancy.
Police made arrests during a public auction at the Agricultural Fund building in Pilsen
Anti-corruption police made various arrests at the Agricultural Fund building in recent days.
Detectives handcuffed a local media entrepreneur and real estate entrepreneur Luboš G.
It appears that it attempted to manipulate an ongoing public auction for the restitution of rights in such a way that the auccuted land is received by a precise interested party.
He had to pay Luboš G. half a million kroners for it.
"At the moment I can only say that the police are looking for a person for bribery during a public auction and public bidding," Pilsen Antonie Zelená's attorney general's head told Pravo, adding that for the time being he can't disclose further details about the ongoing investigation.
After twenty-four hours, the police released the 46-year-old businessman.
On how it could influence the public auction, given little official information, it can only be speculated.
Agricultural Fund spokeswoman Lubomíra Černá responded to Právo’s questions by saying that the police actually carried out the arrest at Pilsen’s headquarters and it was during a public auction.
"It's about a customer, not one of our workers," he said.
According to Černá, the public auction is called "Call for the submission of land transfer proposals in accordance with the land law" and is carried out when several interested parties appear in relation to the land offered.
"These people, during the call, offer a value higher than that of their restitution, which must be transferred before the value of the land is announced."
"Depending on the offers, the order of the buyers is determined and, then, the Agricultural Fund transfers the land to the person who offers the greatest offer," the Fund's spokeswoman explained.
Luboš G. had information on who was going to be submitted to the call and, since at the same time he is an interested person because he acquired various rights of restitution, he proposed to those interested in the field that he would not participate in the auction.
Please charge for a commission.
This would explain that, at the time of his arrest, he had so much money on him.
Luboš G. had special contacts in the Pilsen Bottom for some time now.
It was included in the case of the former head of PF Václav Sankot, who was accused of dispatching the replacement of land for which a construction decision had already been issued.
For example, Luboš G. has just obtained land planned for the construction of public infrastructure from the Fund, in particular in the place where a large road should be built to the Borská pole industrial area.
Afterwards, he changed them very beneficially with the magistrate for a house in the historical center of the city with a much higher value.
In this case, the city council had to intervene because, otherwise, the blocked land would avoid construction.
Families will be required to pay additional expenses for several hundred kroners per month
Households will have to pay from January not only for all types of energy, but also for water and electricity, heating supplements and, in some cases, higher rentals.
Consequently, families will have to remove several hundred kroners a month more than now.
The price of electricity increases by 4.6%, the gas around 2%, in some cities they will have to pay up to 10% more for the water supply and many also increase the price of central heating.
Rising electricity for families living in apartments should rise to about 100 more kroners a year than now.
However, the main electricity suppliers reduced prices, as it has increased support for the production of energy from renewable sources, particularly photovoltaics.
However, gas is moderately expensive, especially at the time when heating is most used and, therefore, greater consumption.
In this case, they must also have a couple of hundreds more kroners per year.
While electricity and gas are rising throughout the country, the price change from central heating varies from place to place.
The rise in prices is mainly planned by the largest municipality.
For example, Pardubice or Hradec Králové households will pay since January for central heating about 50 kroners per month, while Liberec families will pay more than 1,000 additional kroners per year.
Residents in Prague will also have to support a 9% increase in the price of water and heating, especially in the event that the city increases the rent of infrastructure.
If the rent does not vary, the Praguenses will pay an additional 6.8%.
Prices in Prague, however, will remain among the lowest in the entire country.
In northern Bohemia, where current prices are among the highest, both rates will become 7.4% expensive.
They can considerably increase the rental prices of 450,000 apartments rented in Prague, where the regulation will end in January.
For example, the company CPI Byty, which owns in Ústí nad Labem about 2,500 apartments, will increase the rent from January by up to 100%.
The company will take to court those who do not agree with the new rental.
Regulated rentals, which benefit residents around 300,000, could increase next year to 10%.
Young Czeches get drunk with Fernet and rum. Beer is no longer in a way, because it works slowly
Fernet, rum, vodka and even good whiskeys stolen from their parents.
Those are the drinks that cause them to end up in medical care young drinkers with an age less than fifteen years.
Children who end up lying in the hospital continuously to overcome the hangover are no longer an exception.
This stems from the experience of physicians who encounter such cases in hospital pediatric admissions.
"Drinking beer or wine isn't fashionable anymore, because it takes a long time to get them drunk."
"Now fashion is strong alcoholic beverages," Hořovice Hospital chief of pediatry in central Bohemia Luděk Pelikán told Právo.
"When they manage to catch a bottle from their cupboard father, it may be whiskey, but it's usually about Fernet and vodka."
"Vodka has the advantage that it lightens with juice and doesn't smell so much," he explains.
This is also confirmed by Motol University Hospital spokeswoman Eva Jurinová.
"Normally, it's usually Fernet or rum."
"Those are modern alcoholic beverages, particularly among schoolchildren."
"In some cases, they use brandy they took at home," Jurinová adds.
According to Chief Pelikán, they are drinkers of thirteen and fourteen years who usually appear in hospitals.
Before Hořovice, where he works since this year, he worked for a decade in pediatrics in Motol, where ambulances took young people from nearby areas.
"A girl had to enter maybe four times."
"It began at the age of twelve, when she was brought in with 3.5 blood alcohol, in addition to a mixture with ecstasis," the department's doctor said.
“He was at Holešovice fair at a dance party.”
"What's interesting is that his mother wasn't even surprised that his daughter disappeared 48 hours."
It is precisely the lack of interest of the parents or even their clear tolerance for drinking on the part of their children that is considered the main cause of the increase in alcohol consumption among young people.
Experts point out that, in the case of children, you cannot talk about dependency.
"Alcoholic is understood as a person who has a chronic dependence on alcohol for several years, with all the phenomena associated with it, which does not occur in children."
"In those who drink alcohol repeatedly, it is rather due to a systematically failed social or family environment," explains Chief Pelikán.
According to spokeswoman Jurinová, this year in the pediatric clinic of Motol were hospitalized until mid-November for forty drunk children.
However, the statistics only show those who come with severe intoxication and should remain in the hospital.
Young drinkers who, after the walking medical examination, go home with the parents or the police, do not count.
In addition to the cases in which children simply gather after school and drink brandy, for Pelikan there are three seasonal ups and downs that can be mentioned: the day of witches, New Year's Eve and the end of the school year.
"In such a circumstance, they drink for bad grades or, on the contrary, celebrate the beginning of the holidays."
"On witches day I've come to see in admissions up to seven drunk children at a time," Pelikán adds.
Drinkers who spend the night in detox under the supervision of doctors, paradoxically, have the advantage that the next day they do not suffer a terrible hangover at home.
The morning headache, caused by dehydration and lack of glucose, is avoided with the injection of a serum with glucose and with the nutrients that are missing from the body.
"These take him much better, because after the hectic night, they get up well and don't hurt anything."
"They're only confused about where they're," Pelikán says, adding that patients often lose their good mood at least when they see the expenses they've generated.
Ambulance transfer and hospitalization in a room for serious patients costs a dozen thousand kroners.
"I've often used the cost as a means of educating them, when in the vision I tell them directly to the children how much their drunkenness has cost and that they would have to spend the whole summer in a job for young people so that you can earn enough to pay for that night," Pelikán adds.
Health insurance, in his opinion perhaps as a regressive measure, which usually returns to people the costs of treatment, does not do so in the case of children who drink.
Two L-159 aircraft meet for the first time in foreign hands in Spain
Two L-159 aircraft out of a total of five, for which the Czech Ministry of Defense replaced the Casa C-295M aircraft, departed on Tuesday for Seville, where the manufacturer of the Casa-EADS aircraft is located.
It’s the first time such a device has been in the hands of someone other than the Czech army.
At the moment, they were only abroad for internship or demo flights.
It is the two-seater version L-159T1, which until now used the Czech army and was still in a state of flying.
The remaining three aircraft are L-159 cars.
These are still preserved in Aer Vodochody and the intermediary of the sale, the Omnipol company, already took care of them last week.
Jan Peješk, of the defense press department, says it is not yet known when Vodochody’s planes will retire physically.
"L-159 single-seater aircraft, on the other hand, were separated from long-repaired aircraft, without investments or additional contributions from the Ministry."
"The L-159T1 two-seaters were exchanged in their current state," Pejšek told Právo.
He added that the army does not run out of two-seater aircraft, since in May 2009 the army signed with Aer Vodochody a contract for an amount of 200 million kroner for the construction of unnecessary L-159 devices for two-seaters.
One of the new planes already received by the army in July, the other will be delivered at the end of the year.
According to Pejšek, EADS pledged that the aircraft will be added to the equipment of some allied countries.
"The contract clearly states that EADS is the one withdrawing L-159 aircraft and that they will serve the practices of pilots from NATO countries."
"Therefore, it's up to the buyer to decide how he's going to use them in this framework," Pejšek says.
Hospodářské noviny newspaper published ahead of time that, for example, the Spanish army is not interested in the L-159.
The exchange of five L-159s for a Casa aircraft and the purchase of three other aircraft for 3.5 billion kroner were approved last year by the Mirek Topolanek government.
The Ministry of Defense did not convene a tender for the purchase and negotiated directly with EADS.
The current defense management states that the exchange promotes the future export of L-159 overseas.
The Czech army acquired Aer 72 aircraft and only uses 24, so it wants to sell the rest.
Purchasing Casa aircraft will possibly have judicial consequences.
The European Commission already denounced the Czech Ministry of Defence before the European Court of Justice because it should choose the planes by placing a European tender and not contacting a single manufacturer.
The army has already received three Casa planes and the last one should arrive at Kbely’s Prague airport in fourteen days.
A longer index finger than the override indicates a lower risk of cancer
Men who have the longest rate than overring are subject to a lower risk of prostate cancer.
This is the startling conclusion of a study published Wednesday in a British journal on cancer.
It could depend on the level of testosterone, the male hormone.
"Our results indicate that the relative length of the finger may constitute a simple test of the risk of prostate cancer, in particular for men aged up to 60," said study co-author Ros Eeles, a professor at the Institute for Cancer Research at the University of Warwick.
Therefore, the finger could help determine which men should have a prostate cancer test, particularly if this characteristic is associated with a genetic risk or other harmful factors, such as familial health problems, according to Eeles.
During the period 1994-2009, he followed up with a British counterpart of more than 1,500 patients with prostate cancer and, simultaneously, 3,000 healthy men.
More than half of the participants in the study had a shorter-than-till-right-handed toe.
They had the same chances of suffering from prostate cancer as 19% of those whose fingers were of the same length.
In the case of men with a longer rate than an annular finger, this risk was reduced by 33%; for men younger than 60% the reduction reached 87%.
The relationship between the length of the two fingers in question, determined in the prenatal period, reflects different levels of hormones to which the fetus in the matrix is exposed.
A lower testosterone level corresponds to a longer index.
Previous studies indicated that testosterone influences the development of prostate cancer.
The great Czech geneticist and former president of the Academy of Sciences, Professor Václav Pačes, told ČTK that he knows the study, but that he does not think that the relationship between finger length and cancer can be "completely demonstrated".
The Czech Republic under snow: D1 and D5 are closed
Czech, like the majority in Europe, dawned on Thursday covered in snow.
Roads and sidewalks are mainly covered by compact snow and snowplowing machines circulated overnight in numerous areas.
In the morning the D5 road was closed between kilometers 89 and 93 towards Rozvadov due to several accidents of commercial vehicles and passenger cars, as well as the D1 in Brno in the direction of Prague.
The news was conveyed by the operator of Ústředního automotoklubu (ÚAMK).
At kilometer 91, a truck collapsed, in the 93, two industrial vehicles collided that caused injuries.
Also several passenger cars ended up in the gutter.
There are also problems on the D1 motorway at kilometer 185 towards Prague.
Two trucks crashed there.
Drivers must travel slowly after Brno at kilometer 190 or traverse Brno and enter kilometer 182 in Kývalce.
Traffic jams of several kilometers have been formed there.
There has also been an accident on Highway D11, right next to Prague.
Prague is covered by a layer of snow of several centimeters and thermometers indicate -7 °C.
They are forming jams for example in Prosek, although Štěrboholská is also congested.
The safest means of transportation is the metro.
The trams circulate late, for example on the line between Karlovo náměstí and Náměstí Míru.
At eight in the morning, there will be another state of crisis in Prague.
Problems also affect railways and the airport.
Because of the snow, the Czech railway company had to cancel seven connections for three fast.
The problems affect, for example, the line between Prague and Kralupy or Prague and Beroun.
Travelers should see what the current situation is.
Ruzyně airport was closed from eleven o’clock on Wednesday night until five in the morning.
Currently, you can already circulate on most roads in southern Moravia, but with extreme caution.
