American soldiers have a new "smart weapon"
The U.S. military is employing in Afghanistan a new "smart" weapon, known for the moment only by the coded name XM-25.
The generals hope that the new rifle will influence the local conflict with the Taliban and to "change the progress of the game" towards its victory.
The new weapons are like a small pistol with an extremely precise aim.
His bullets are fired with a laser with an accuracy of several meters.
The special 25 mm caliber ammunition created for this rifle and the high burden of explosives it contains cause it to actually function as a small grenade.
Projectiles can be programmed to explode not only when they hit the target, but at any point in their ballistic trajectory.
They can be used against enemies hiding behind an obstacle, a wall or a trench.
Undoubtedly, normal protection cannot be used and, when it throws its killer shower of metal splinters, it can be essential in battle.
According to Richard Audette, who spoke about the development of the XM-25, the new rifle is the first infantry weapon to use “smart” technology.
"The soldiers manipulate it in such a way that they find the target, point to its laser beam, which determines the distance, then obtains a precise point of sight and, depending on it, corrects the projection line and squeezes the trigger."
"Let's say 543 meters have been measured."
"When the trigger is pulsed, the weapon is loaded and the projectile launched at a distance of 543 meters, plus minus one, two or three meters," Audette said.
400 jobs are moved
The Fujitsu consortium moves its branch in Bad Homburg to Frankfurt.
