Omba Stops New Oil Drilling
From the coast of the U.S. state of Florida to the Gulf of Mexico, no new oil drilling has appeared in these years.
The government of Washington issued a strong ban and thereby overturned its previous plans unappealably.
Also facing the Atlantic coast, new perforations remain closed for the moment.
Interior Minister Ken Salazar announced on Wednesday.
What provoked this strict ban, which will at least be in place for the next five years, is the explosion of the "Deepwater Horizon" oil rig in April.
Prior to the misfortune that killed eleven workers and sparked America’s biggest environmental catastrophe, President Barack Obama had admitted new drilling in the western part of the Gulf of Mexico under pressure from the opposition and the industry.
Supporters thus wanted to reduce U.S. reliance on new oil supplies from abroad.
Until today it hasn’t quite been clear whose responsibility is for the irreparable black tide in the gulf.
At the centre of criticism is the British energy consortium BP, which faces millions of lawsuits.
A commission of inquiry commissioned by Obama will present its results in December.
In October, Ken Salazar, the interior minister, lifted the moratorium on drilling after the explosion.
Later, different petero consortiums submitted applications for new drilling attempts.
Obaba and Salazar want to rule out a new catastrophe using tougher controls and stricter security regulations.
Currently the responsible supervisory authority, which after having been accused of corruption charges has fallen into discredituty, is reversed at the root.
Obama announces in parallel that lucrative fiscal gaps for oil and gas consortiums will be closed.
The industry publicly announces that total extraction in the gulf will be lower thanks to stricter regulation, to date.
“Government’s plans are the biggest brake on the growth of the oil and gas industries,” reads the result of the current survey conducted by the advisory company BDO – ahead of Wednesday’s extraction ban.
American Energey Alliance complains about insecurity in the sector: "No one knows what exactly changes with regulation."
Investments in new extraction projects are therefore prolonged to the detriment of growth.
Ogonyland is ruined
River water is marrón oil, it smells like a gas station: Millions of liters of crude have shattered nature in Nigeria’s Ogoniland.
People live by theft, corruption and the illegal oil trade.
Celestine Akpobari thinks it's okay.
“Don’t get tired,” the environmental activist reassures reporters: “Today enough oil will pass before your eyes.”
The 37-year-old Nigerian has managed to photograph boys wading in the water to the chest with the brightness of lights reflected in turbulences on the surface: With the colorful brightness of the water, the scene seems almost beautiful.
You can't see brown oil stains floating on the surface.
Previously here I caught shrimp to handfuls, explains Esther Badom on the bank of the Bodo-Jetty river.
Meanwhile, the flowing waters are totally dead.
Five years ago an impressive amount of oil was dumped in the formidable wetland through a broken pipeline.
The fish around the small town of Bodo had to swim six hours to get to waters full of fish: Which, in the heart of the Niger delta and located in Ogoniland, is considered to be one of the most stinky regions in the world.
On the path of the river that passes through mangroves, extensive desolation is obvious.
Instead of clear water, the ship’s engine removes a brown broth, which immerses his hand in the water, will take some full of oil.
The roots of mangrove trees rise from the ground like dark licorice sticks, sadly isolating the palm trees that collide and hang their palms.
In vain flocks of birds are searched, as if they were in the wetland as always: As soon as the barrier of the outside is silenced, a kind of ghostly calm of an earlier paradise with the smell of a gas station expands.
"What happens here is an ecological struggle," he says, hurraño, Celestine Akpobari. "We live in constant danger to health."
Warm comparison proceeds.
The area of the Ogoni settlement has been a quarrelsome battlefield for years: Here the first European explorers stormed more than 60 years ago, here the first resistance was organized in the 1980s with the writer Ken Saro-Wiwa, later executed, here had to place his first factory the Shell consortium in 1993, because it would be too dangerous for oil employees.
Even today the return of companies to Ogonilandia is prohibited: While they pumped more than two million barrels of black gold daily from the entire delta region, three times larger than Switzerland, with more than 5,000 extraction sources and 7,000 kilometers of pipelines, the oil fields in Ogoniland were calm, the location is too unstable for the consortium.
Every two-hundred meters stood out steel shelves as quirky water sculptures: "Christmas trees" affectionately called the heads of the perforators.
They are up to 50 years old, most have total corrosion.
At every moment a head fails, Akpobari says, then flows a constant jet of oil into the water.
According to experts, more than two billion liters have been dumped in 50 years in the delta ecosystem: Year after year, the amount of oil penetrates, as in the "Exxon Valdez" ship breakdown 21 years ago in the Alaskan Sea, one of the greatest ecological catastrophes in the history of humanity.
Whoever is responsible for the lasting environmental region will deal with it.
While Shell’s consortium and organized oil thefts are responsible for more than 90 percent of pollution, environmentalists see heads about to break down and leaky pipelines as the main causes.
In Nigeria, Shell mainly applies low safety standards normally in the conventional world, scientist Richard Steiner reproached the consortium in 2008: Pipeline defect rates are “over one multiple greater than elsewhere.”
In a place, which from afar seems forgotten, appears a workshop full of oil under the free sky, figures emerge from among the mangroves: They are naked and covered in oil.
Among one of the many barrels, which are sometimes attached to perforations, sometimes arbitrarily scattered on the ground, a fire flies: a small illegal refinery.
In the barrel, oil heats up into the fire more hours, explains Michael, the group’s head.
First of all, the gasoline penetrates through the pipes in the vessel, later the oil pipe drips and finally comes out diesel.
In this way, Michael and four employees produce up to four barrels of 300-liter diesel daily: "It gives us 34,000 naira", about 22 dollars.
Delta people take advantage of fuel shortages in one of black Africa’s largest oil states.
Nigeria has only a single refinery.
With the absurd and cheap diesel price of just 40 euros-cent per liter, illegal heaters can offer even less: Thus they reduce the value of their raw material.
It comes from "those of bunkers", which tricked the network of pipelines that Ogoniland crosses: According to experts, at this rate at least 16 million liters of oil are lost a day in the entire Niger delta.
Only an insignificant part of the stored provisional raw material is clearly refined illegally: Organized criminal networks, to which politicians and senior military officials also belong, keep the best part in cisterns and sell it cheaply on the world market.
Activist Akpobari considers purely academic the debate about whether the environmental catastrophe is due rather to the management of the population or the loose way of production.
"If the oil companies didn't behave like invaders and hadn't completely destroyed the fish's natural habitat, there would be no sabotage, no theft, no illegal refineries."
While the conditions of the Ogoni are destroyed mainly by Shell to the point that only one deduction from the consortium appears: "Perhaps it is better to present another nationality, such as China."
Michael's getting nervous.
"It's about time you disappeared," says the muscular Ölmann.
"The soldiers can come any minute."
If the special army unit picks up illegal refiners, it doesn't threaten to arrest them, but with a painful bribery.
Soldiers from the north also want, like all Nigerians, to take advantage of the raw material.
"This doomed invention has completely shattered our country," Akpobari said.
Investigators detect dangerous factory defects in A380 engines
Apparently the A380 has a huge security issue.
In an investigation of a machine that made a forced landing, investigators found a possible factory defect, which can produce "a catastrophic engine failure."
Qantas wants to sue the engine manufacturer.
Passengers at Qantas’ Airbus who made an emergency landing in early November were much luckier than you can imagine: Australian aviation safety authorities perceived thanks to their investigations a big safety issue in the engines of some A380s, and warn of a possible factory defect.
The potential defect has complicated repercussions: In a cuff connection, fissures can be caused by fatigue or cracks, ATSB authorities announced in a report released Thursday.
This could have caused "a catastrophic engine failure."
This affected the engines manufactured by Rolls-Royce belonging to 20 A380 series aircraft.
Among the Qantas-Airbus who had to make a hard landing on November 4 in Singapore.
The Australian airline starts legal averages against the British turbine manufacturer.
He filed a lawsuit in Sidney’s federal court enabling further legal action against the company.
Alan Joyce, director of Qantas, stated that his airline will claim compensation for the incident in early November.
In the engine mark they have lost parts and their wing is deteriorated.
Rolls Royce distinguishes the type of gasoline as the cause of damage.
After the incident, up to 40 engines from around the world have to be replaced.
Australian investigators have announced that they will carry out more safety tests on superjumbos equipped with affected engines.
The security recommendation is the result of a collaboration with Rolls Royce, said Martin Dolan, an ATSB spokesperson.
The security issue has become important enough to inform all those affected.
After emergency landing of the Qantas apparatus, the airline kept its A380 fleet on the ground for many weeks.
Singapore Airlines kept approximately three of its eleven A380s temporarily on the ground.
Lufthansa replenished the engine of an A380 as a precaution.
This double-deck aircraft is the world’s largestest passenger aircraft.
Nevada across the country causes traffic chaos
Totally white Germany: From one day to the next it has snowed almost the whole country.
In many streets chaos prevails, trains and airplanes fail.
At least three people have lost their lives in traffic accidents.
Winter ice Germany: In almost all of the country, freezing temperatures were reached during the night of Thursday, which has unleashed chaos in traffic at many points.
In some parts of the north and east, there is a layer of smoth 15 centimeters thick.
Traffic is very limited on most highways and on many streets.
On the A7 at the height of Flensburg, the snowplows are in continuous operation, removing the new snow again and again, but it is not prepared.
In addition, strong gusts of wind sweep the Baltic Sea coast.
The Federal Maritime and Hydrographic Agency of Germany (BSH) expects live tides with water level 1.2 meters above its usual level on the Baltic Sea coast Schleswig-Holsteins.
In Saxony the accumulation of snow caused considerable problems on highways and national roads.
According to the meteorological service Meteomedia, the red alert in Sächsischen Schweiz is called, in fact the highest alert.
There are partially 30 centimeters of newly fallen snow and cold and strong winds are responsible for swirling it.
A lot of trucks and cars were trapped overnight there.
Also in Sajonia Anhalt law enforcement authorities notified major riots in the streets.
On the A2, snow and wind paralyzed traffic and created traffic jams.
In Thuringia the snowfall affected the A4 and the A9.
According to Deutscher Bahn, major upheavals are expected in Saxony, Saxony Anhalt, Thuringia and northern Bavaria.
Snow on the road, fallen trees on lanes or shattered catenarie and holes are the main problems.
In Saxony the intercity service from Leipzig to Nuremberg is suspended.
In Saafeld, Thuringia an ICE train had to stop their trip to Munich.
Firefighters and the rescue service treated the 165 passengers on the train with blankets of wool, food and drink.
Winds with snow sweep through the streets.
At Germany’s largest airport, snowy winds caused numerous flight suspensions.
According to a spokeswoman of the flight information station, some cancellations have already been announced in Munich.
In Frankfurt am Main, according to a Fraport spokeswoman there are already 40 flights canceled.
Snoothers are in continuous operation.
However, there are few stranded passengers at the airport, despite that there are 400 campaign beds prepared for them.
In many streets, on Wednesday afternoon there have already been many accidents.
In the afternoon, a truck driver died in a collision with a train at a track crossing in Ostholstein.
The driver suffered minor injuries.
In the vicinity of Nuremberg there was a similar accident due to the snowy streets: Also here a truck was hit at a crossroads because the vehicle was docked.
The driver lost his life.
On the train four passengers were injured, Bayreuth’s route to Nuremberg was blocked for this reason in the morning.
A 30-year-old woman also died after flipping with her car near Aschaffennburg on a slippery road.
Meteorologists have good news: The snow will be increasingly softer and dust-like and therefore swirling on gentle streaks.
But the wind will decrease.
Only the Baltic sea will be affected by strong and fast winds.
It will certainly continue to be very cold, in some places up to 15 degrees negative.
