Thousands of people in mourning for deceased miners
In New Zealand, more than 10,000 people thought of buried miners, who died after many explosions in a mine.
The bodies have not yet been found, because the security measures remain precarious in the place of misfortune.
Almost two weeks ago there was an intense explosion at the Pike River mine near the city of Greymouth.
29 miners aged between 17 and 62 were buried in the mine.
A probe was introduced into the site of misfortune, but did not uncover any signs of survival.
Due to the high concentration of toxic gases and successive explosions, no salvage equipment was able to come down for safety reasons.
After the second intense detonation, authorities and experts deduced that there was no chance that the miners would have survived.
Bodies have not been retrieved to date.
Currently family and friends remember the dead with a commemorative act.
Ten thousand people have changed with the glow of the sun in Greymouth on the New Zealand South Island.
They are silent for two minutes.
In homage of the buried miners, 29 tables were placed with helmets, lamps and names of the miners.
There were also photographs and personal objects, under clothing and surfboards.
The flags waved at half-mast in front of the country’s government buildings.
New Zealand expected miners to return to the surface "from the depths of the earth," John Key, the president of the New Zealand government, told participants of the commemorative event.
“But they never came home.”
He can't assume the death of his son until he's seen his body, said Lawrie Drew, whose Zen son was buried in the mine.
Together with the president of the government and the relatives of the victims, many foreign diplomats participated in the memorial event.
In the first explosion on November 19, miners of British and South African origin were also buried.
Salvage workers began extinguishing a fire inside the coal mine on the southern island of New Zealand.
As soon as the explosive gases have been extracted, scorching temperatures inside the mine will begin to descend, Gary Knowles told reporters.
To hell, warrior!
Does gore fit into artistic cinema?
Director Nicolas Winding Refn re-explores the borders of the genre with his "Walhalla Rising" battle epic.
In which audience will Danish director Nicolas Winding Refn have thought when shooting "Walhalla Rising"?
A single-eyed Viking warrior (Mads Mikkelsen) frees himself from his torturers and murders his enemies with almost superhuman struggles, it sounds ideal for young viewers looking for bloody action, precisely because our hero likes to employ brutal methods to kill and rip his enemies the guts from their bodies alive when he is in a bad mood.
On the other hand, Unojo, as it’s actually called, doesn’t say almost any words throughout the movie and the other characters get carried away at most one or two phrases or a couple of painful deadly screams.
Otherwise they shut up and enjoy the beautiful and terrifying montuous landscapes of Scotland, which the backstages offer the Viking world of about a thousand years ago.
Quitely ideal for filmmakers grown and interested in existentialism and very calm chamber movements.
When a movie has multiple groups of recipients it’s a shame they can’t dismiss each other.
Director Winding Refn considers his savages and trilogy about the "Pusher" drug-trafficking hairstyle as a kind of Danish version of Quentin Tarantino, but stoically refuses to make his works for all audiences with a little more humor in the mainstream or somewhat less brutality of the artistic house.
A brain mass and sprouting guts can’t deter most fans of artistic cinema.
Most fans of the action die of boredom, if Unojo takes a ship to Jerusalem with a troop of violent missionaries for interpretable motives and becomes stuck an eternity in calm and fog before his team arrives in America and shuts up there again.
It's a pity that "Walhalla Rising" hasn't found a film distributor in Germany and it's only released on DVD.
It is a calamity against the magnificent images, but also because, behind the presumptuous and bloody façade, it hides a sometimes exciting piece of cinema, which deserves some attention.
This film is not about dialogue or a logical action, everything is atmosphere, a febrile dream transformed into a film.
And that's why there's no escape for spectators.
Refn hides just a moment of relaxation and its glacial images and the gloomy trance soundtrack make a knot in his throat, above all, the incredible hatred, which from the hearts of the characters goes towards him.
Only a little blonde youth, who faithfully supports Unojo, retains something like innocence, everyone else is possessed by anger.
Above all, the one-eyed hero, whose motives are never explained, becomes at any moment a warrior out of hell for enemies and provisional allies, possibly in the ambassador of the devil himself.
He represents perdition, but perhaps only for those who are lost anyway.
"Walhalla Rising" is a mysterious film you can hate and admire, to get angry, altered and surprised.
An experience.
