No Trace of Affliction
In contrast, the home team dominates over its customers from Sweden, Finland and the U.S.
Fans scream with joy as loud music rumbles, they hit the ground with their feet.
The Bailey club vibrates in the South Cork, because the players throw themselves, and find, pingpong balls to the beer glasses from a distance of three meters
The strange spectacle is called "Beerpong", and the prize for the winner is 500 euros plus plane tickets to Las Vegas for the beerpong world championship in January.
Cian Tanner is a quiet guy, this quirky drinking game and the discreet computer scientist don’t actually make good crumbs.
And the 26-year-old boy tries to have fun with it, to be distracted from the fulminant question, what the future will bring.
The fun in Las Vegas is not in his plans, in January he will conduct a personal interview in New Zealand.
"I have a bad feeling," he says.
“It’s a long way from home and I still don’t have a job there.”
At least: His girlfriend awaits him in Auckland, "from one shot he got the job of his dreams as an event manager, maybe it's a good sign," says the young man and smiles.
Because of poor home prospects, two of Tanners' three brothers have already searched for luck abroad.
His sister Yvonne, a social worker, is in Sidney, her brother Colin currently works as an IT advisor in London.
Young people leave: On the island there are many families such as the Tanners.
100,000 Irish will leave the country in the near future, forecast economics experts and Dublin’s ESRI social research institute.
Until 2015 there are 200,000 emigrants.
A great loss for this small country with only 4.5 million inhabitants.
The forecasts are confirmed, there is a migratory wave comparable to that suffered by the island in the 1980s.
After a decade of progression that brought great well-being to the Irish, melancholy is currently spreading.
The economic crisis confuses above all well-trained young, Irish people, for whom until now, everything is uphill.
Job loss and social decline seemed absurd in the boom years.
Everyone needs money in abundance.
But three out of ten men and women under the age of thirty are unemployed.
In the Irish Times there are letters to the mass director of young emigrants, who complain that: "Everything sinks in Ireland."
"There should be an uprising against politicians and bankers, but who can, goes and loses our country and with it an entire generation."
"The last to close the door."
Leanne Caulfiend, a law student and president of the union of Irish students sees a bleak future appear because the government is only dedicated to montages and no longer offers future prospects to young people.
“They reduce minimum wages, but they don’t have strategies to achieve new jobs,” the 20-year-old says.
He observes how his companions range from unreminded practices to the following.
His biggest concern, he explains, is having to emigrate in four years.
"This idea is sad and depressing."
People like Stephen McLarnon are trying to look at this topic positively.
McLarnon organizes the "WorkingAbroadExpo", a fair that offers a service around the topic of working abroad.
“For those starting to work, doing it abroad is an excellent opportunity, the first steps to running your career,” he said.
On his website there are photos of one boy surfing in Australia and another practicing snowboarding in Canada.
The message is clear: Working abroad is not like doing it in the galleys, but it entails freedom and fun.
For half a year, Seán O'Donovan knows what it's like to build something elsewhere.
Since there was no longer anything to do for the 29-year-old engineer, he currently works with 15 Irish comrades in construction in Germany.
Now they're running a fashion store in Gelsenkirchen.
In January he went to Hannover or to Berlin, to the next project.
O'Donovan sees his homeland only rarely because he works overtime to be able to pay credit for his home in Ireland.
"The situation is a little crazy," he says, since a long time ago from the first time he stepped on the threshold of his house.
"I make money in Germany for a house in Ireland, where I'm never so good."
He laughs somewhat embarrassed and goes for icy rooms.
The small house is in an elegant residential area with well-kept gardens in front of the houses, at the entrance are parked large and expensive cars.
O'Donovan bought the house together with his sister four years ago for 380,000 euros, 100 percent financed by a loan.
"It was crazy times, there was easy money without a lot of questions," he says.
Are you afraid to fail with credit?
O'Donovan thinks for a while, serves more hot tea and says, "I don't have children I have to feed and I'm young; if everything goes wrong, I can start from scratch."
The civil engineer doesn’t like the complaints he often listens to today.
"No one should go hungry, our situation is not, by far, as dramatic as in times spent here on the island," he says.
"Working a season abroad isn't the worst thing that can happen, maybe with it we feel for the first time as Irish."
He laughs and tells about his father, that in the last great economic crisis he went to England.
"After seven years he returned and life continued in Ireland, very simple."
Cian Tanner likes these ideas.
Leaving the future pending and simply seeing what life is like elsewhere.
He already worked for a couple of years in London and why wouldn't he and his girlfriend in New Zealand do?
But these days there’s one thing he’s terrified about: celebrating his 27th birthday on Friday in Dublin.
He's afraid he can degenerate into a farewell party.
“Only, not too much emotions,” he says and gives a great drink to his beer.
"Farewell, it sounds very distressing."
"We'd better call it a marching party, very relaxed, with a couple of friends, a couple of drinks, a couple of games."
The Democratic Routine
Support for democracy in Latin America, as well as support for private companies, continues to increase.
Crime raises greater concerns than unemployment.
In addition, in much of the region, Brazil is seen as a country more influential than the United States.
These are some of the conclusions of the latest Latinobarometer survey conducted in 18 countries and published exclusively by The Economist.
Given that the survey has been held regularly since 1995, it can monitor attitude changes in the region.
In the results of this year’s survey, conducted in September and the beginning of October, two related issues stand out.
The first is the relatively lively humor reigning in Latin America.
The second is the increasing stability of attitudes towards democracy and its main institutions.
Support for democracy has increased considerably in several countries on the Pacific coast of South America (see Table 1).
For example, in Peru, where economic growth has reached an average of 6% annually since 2002, support for democracy has risened from 40% in 2005 to 61% this year.
It also grew in Mexico, where the economy has rebounded after suffering a large decline in production last year.
However, only half of the respondents from Mexico and Brazil, the two most populous countries in the area, are convinced Democrats, which lowers the regional average (see figure 2).
About 44% of respondents in the region were satisfied with how democracy works in practice in their country, the same figure as last year, but considerably higher than the 25% recorded in 2001 (Figure 3).
About 34% said that they trusted their country’s Congress and 45% in their government, the same amount as last year in both cases, but considerably higher compared to 2003 (17% and 24%, respectively).
A decade ago, economic turbulence in Latin America severely affected support for democracy.
The fact that it remained during the global financial crisis is likely due to the fact that many Latin American countries were only affected by a brief recession and enjoyed a strong recovery.
In addition, the existence of a stronger social security network has helped protect the poorest.
(The United Nations Economic Commission for Latin America reported this week that the proportion of Latin Americans living in conditions of poverty only marginally increased in 2009 and has declined again this year to 32%.)
However, mood varies considerably from one country to another (see figure 4).
Brazil’s good economic returns and the popularity of its outgoing president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva, is reflected in an overall sense of progress.
This sentiment is less common in Mexico and Central America, where the recession was more severe and violent crimes have increased.
For the second time since the polls began, more respondents in Latin America mention crime, rather than unemployment, as the most serious problem facing their country (see figure 5).
About 31% say that, whether they or a close relative, they have been the victim of crime in the last year, a figure that has fallen since 38% last year and that is the lowest percentage registered since 1995.
The most positive economic returns are reflected in greater support for private companies and the market economy: about 71% of respondents in the region say that private companies are indispensable for development, while in 2007 it was only 56% (see figure 6).
About 58% agree that the market economy is the only system that will allow their country to develop, up from 47% last year.
Interestingly, 62% of those surveyed in Venezuela say the same (and 80% support private companies), although its leftist president, Hugo Chavez, is expropriating numerous private companies.
Brazil is the country that exerts the greatest leadership in the region, as indicated by 19% of respondents (compared to 18% last year), followed by the United States (9%, same figure as last year) and Venezuela (9% compared to 11% a year ago).
However, the U.S. is the country considered most influential by respondents in Mexico and much of Central America, while Venezuela holds such a title in Ecuador, the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.
Elections to Trash
No one expected the November 28 general election in Haiti to be perfect.
However, they passed in a much worse way than foreigners who tried to help the country come back after their problems.
Votes were introduced at the polls, some people voted several times, ballots thrown in the sewers, destroyed electoral colleges and acts of intimidation.
The polling stations opened with hours of delay.
Numerous Haitians, including groups displaced by the January earthquake, roamed the area trying to figure out where they had to vote.
Some of them, in the end, gave up.
A dozen presidential candidates, including several of the most popular, denounced fraud and called for the annulment of elections.
The protests, some of them violent, spread across the country.
However, Edmond Mulet, Haiti’s highest-ranking United Nations official, stated that they were “a relatively good election in many ways.”
Observers of the Organization of American States and the Caribbean Community stated, with caution, that "we do not believe that these irregularities, however serious, necessarily invalidate the process."
In other words, the show continues.
The electoral authority, which according to its opponents is committed to René Préval, the unpopular outgoing president, and his candidate, Jude Célestin, should announce the preliminary results on December 7.
The tiebreaker is expected to take place in January.
All hopes regarding elections have not yet been lost.
The two main opposition candidates, Michel Martelly, a rapper, and Mirlande Manigat, a former first lady, have withdrawn their initial petition in which they requested the invalidation of the vote.
But problems can still arise if the electoral authority declares that Mr. Célestin, who has little support, if he has any, has moved on to the second round.
Considering that the cholera epidemic ravages the country and more than one million earthquake survivors continue to live in tents, great abstention is expected.
When the time came, many Haitians wanted to vote, but failed to do so due to a lack of organization.
About 250,000 identity documents, needed to vote, had not yet been circulated shortly before the elections.
There's a lot at stake.
Aid donors consider the choice of a popular and legitimate government to be an essential step in the reconstruction of Haiti.
And as it has become clear, the Haitians themselves also think so.
Advancing tiptoes to the center
In weeks since she lost her husband, Cristina Fernandez, the president of Argentina, has enjoyed great sympathy among the public that has increased her popularity rate by 20 points, to more than 50%, the highest level she has registered since her election in 2007.
In spite of everything, that it can turn it into a successful campaign for a second term in next year’s elections will depend on whether the clearly affected Mrs. Fernandez ignores the intransigence of her late husband, her predecessor and political partner, Néstor Kirchner.
And, at this point, the indications are inconclusive.
Mr Kirchner, who continued to take charge of economic policy even after his wife succeeded him as president, had a habit of drawing up budgets that underestimated tax revenues, economic growth and inflation.
This allowed the presidential couple to spend additional income at their discretion.
In theory, Mrs. Fernandez’s government lost its majority in congress last year.
However, the opposition is divided, a situation that has worsened after Kirchner’s death from a heart attack on Oct. 27.
Some dissenting members of the Peronist movement in possession have confronted Mrs. Fernandez.
However, after some chaotic sessions in which one deputy punched another in the face and other deputies accused the government of bribery, Congress decided to reject the 2011 budget.
"I'm not going to argue," she said.
The 2010 budget, therefore, will be applied next year, which will offer the president an even greater margin of action when spending it.
The economy is improving considerably: it’s likely to grow to 9% this year.
The official inflation rate is currently at 11.1%, but numerous private calculations, in addition to the wage requirements of the unions, put it about 30%.
Mr Kirchner, who took power in 2003 when Argentina was on the verge of economic collapse, made growth and poverty reduction his priorities, rather than inflation control.
He refused to negotiate with the IMF, pressured the private sector and made INDEC, the statistical institute, adapt official inflation statistics.
Will President Fernandez now moderate these policies?
It has convened meetings between the private sector and trade union leaders.
In addition, its officials say they want to limit wage increases next year to 18%.
The economy has been boosted by the high prices of Argentine agricultural exports in the world.
However, inflation has limited the momentum to growth achieved with a major devaluation in 2002.
Perhaps that’s right, Mrs. Fernandez is signaling that she wants to restore Argentina’s financial ties to the rest of the world.
At the beginning of the year, the government reached an agreement with bondholders who had refused a pre-debt forced restructuring that Argentina had not paid in 2001.
Currently, it seems that Mrs. Fernandez is about to enter into an agreement with the creditor governments grouped in the Paris Club, to which she owes about 6,700 million dollars.
The Kirchners always insisted that this would not imply the IMF’s oversight, as usual.
However, last month Amado Boudou, the Minister of Economy, announced that the Fund will offer "technical assistance" to develop a new national consumer price index, which seems the prelude to the cleaning of INDEC.
It is not clear if President Fernandez wants to remain in politics when her mandate ends, taking into account that she no longer has her husband by her side.
If you do, you will need to gain the support of the middle class, much of which has grown tired of Kirchnerism.
When the Argentines return from their summer vacation in March, the wave of sympathy for it will have declined.
If you want to win other elections, you’ll have to decide whether to repudiate or not, calmly, many of your husband’s policies.
China's half-hurrah
Voters in Taiwan are slowly stepping closer to a closer union with the continent.
What does Taiwan think of its great brother on the other side of the strait?
More than 7.5 million voters, 70% of those eligible, went on Nov. 27 to elect mayors and councillors from five urban districts in what appears to be an extensive assessment by President Ma Ying-jeou.
Mr. Ma himself has become an advocate of sentiments for the continent, forging innovative business ties, including a partial free trade pact, the biggest changes in relations with China since 1949.
Mr. Ma passed the test, but narrowly.
Although his Kuomintang (KMT) party ended up winning three of the five mayoral elections, he failed to win a majority of the vote.
Instead, the Progressive Democratic Party (DPP), which has leaned over Taiwan’s total independence, garnered virtually 50% of all votes against 45% of the KMT.
If it had been the presidential elections, Mr. Ma would be in trouble.
Since his election two years ago, his party’s share of votes has declined by about 8%.
For the first time, PPD tied with the KMT in municipalities.
In the elections you could see the typical elements of the Taiwanese elections, including noisy campaign trucks and large agglomerations with flags and light girls of dancing clothes.
Nor was the mysterious attempt at rigorous murder missing.
Sean Lien, Lien Chan’s son, honorary president of the KMT, was campaigning for one of the KMT candidates the night before the election.
A man from the crowd shot the young man directly in the face, wounding him and killing a passerby.
The curious thing is that it has not been possible to determine if the man intended to harm Lien or take revenge on the candidate.
In any case, says Tsai Ing-wen, the leader of the PPD, the shooting affected the elections, sowing suspicions about the PPD and causing voters to lean for the KMT.
Perhaps the only big winner of the campaign was China.
It was surprising that it wasn’t about the continent itself.
Instead, candidates spoke of public accommodation and job creation plans.
Taiwan normally enjoys its de facto independence and detests China’s military threat.
Consequently, the KMT often avoids publicizing its links with the government in Beijing.
But this time also the PPD avoided encouraging sentiment against China.
It responds to the new awareness that Chinese trade is essential to Taiwan’s economic survival.
For his part, Mr. Ma stated that it had been confirmed that his policy with respect to China was acceptable to most Taiwanese.
The Beijing Government is expected to continue to bribe them with trade, in accordance with its strategy to bring Taiwan back into the fold.
China wasted no time sending a negotiator to Taipei to prepare a summit on investment protection and medical cooperation.
Undoubtedly, he would also like to achieve political integration, but the former comes first.
It will be best to wait for the 2012 presidential election through for it.
In any case, the PPD has been reinvented.
Two years ago he was in a bad situation: his former leader, Chen Shui-bian, was jailed accused of corruption.
President between 2000 and 2008, openly instigated explicit independence.
However, under Mrs. Tsai’s leadership, a new generation of moderates has emerged and the PPD is striving to attract professionals and youth.
Mrs. Tsai has been pleasantly received as PPD’s candidate for the presidency, as has Su Tseng-chang, who served under Chen.
Now his party is doing everything it can to show that it can also have relations with Beijing.
Ms Tsai announced plans to establish a group of experts to act as a point of contact between the PPD and China, provided that negotiations can take place without preconditions.
Ms. Tsai tells the Taiwanese public that her party represents a "reliable alternative" to Mr. Ma's hasty approach to China.
Mr. Chen seems furious about change.
In a statement issued from his cell, he stated that PPD candidates would have come out better if they had insisted that Taiwan is an independent country.
He even ordered Mr. Su, a pragmatist, not to run for president.
Even without Mr. Chen interceding, the PPD will find it difficult to choose among its potential candidates.
The KMT must also conduct an examination of conscience.
Mr. Ma’s assessments have been poor, despite impressive economic growth; KMT candidates did not want to campaign with him.
Its lack of popularity seems to be mainly due to unemployment and growing differences between rich and poor.
A KMT spokesman says it will restructure its cabinet as well as local teams.
Large samples of the PPD suggest that Taiwan’s support for an approach is limited.
At the same time, most Taiwanese believe that economic integration with China is inevitable.
According to Antonio Chiang, a well-known columnist, "the point of non-return has already been crossed."
Grammy Nominations: Jazz singer Esperanza Spalding is the surprise choice in the Revelation Artist category.
Act at the request of a president and possibly the people will look at you.
Esperanza Spalding, the 26-year-old bassist and jazz singer personally selected by President Obama to perform at her 2009 Nobel Peace Prize ceremony, was one of the surprises of the Grammys this year with a high-level nomination in the category of Best Revelation Artist.
Considering that Spalding has been an acclaimed jazz figure since his 2008 album "Esperanza", the nomination gives what to think, but he respects the rules of the Grammys according to which he must be an artist who has not released more than three albums before recording what "determines the public identity of said artist".
His ambitious 2010 album "Chamber Music Society", certainly made a breakthrough and, taking into account his light and pleasant vocal style, as well as a great skill with influences of pop, funk and Latin rhythms, you can at least establish a distinction between Spalding and the path to Norah Jones' success in 2003, the closest equivalent for Grammy voters, who have recently focused on the realm of jazz for this category.
In the remaining categories, Grammy voters rightlyly honored "Historicity" by Vijay Iyer Trio, an exciting and magnificent album that was a favorite last year.
Iyer's competitors include veteran saxophonist James Moody with "Moody 4B" and keyboardist John Beasley for "Positootly."
Trombone Shorty, a raucous favorite in New Orleans and a regular guest on HBO's "Treme," was honored in the category of contemporary jazz, which for the rest leans considerably towards fusion as in the albums of Stanley Clarke, John McLaughlin and Jeff Lorber.
Darcy's savage punk James Argue's Secret Society garnered well-deserved recognition in the category of big groups, competing against the Billy Childs Ensemble's great chamber jazz and ever-risky Dave Holland, whose live album with his octet, "Pathways", was a great example of the bassist's democratic leadership in his band.
Grammy Award nominations Michael Daugherty and Steve Mackey for Best Classical Album Direction
Nominations in the field of classical music for the 53rd Grammy Awards include a mix of musicians and songwriters from around the world.
Major nominations went to Michael Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina", by piano and orchestra, of five movements, and Steve Mackey with "Dreamhouse", a work for eclectic three-movement orchestra.
Among the nominees with ties to Southern California are Estonian composer Arvo Pärt for his #4 symphony, "Los Angeles," a piece commissioned by the Los Angeles Philharmonic and premiered at Walt Disney Concert Hall in 2009.
Los Angeles-based composer Avner Dorman is also nominated for his Mandolin Concerto.
Classical nominations are distributed in thirteen categories spanning orchestra, opera, choral and solo albums.
The classical producer of the year includes five nominees: Blanton Alspaugh, David Frost, Tim Handley, Marina and Victor Ledin, and James Mallinson.
Nominees for the classical music album are Bruckner by Symphonies No. 3 and No. 4 of the Royal Concertgebouw Orchestra; Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina" and "Metropolis Symphony" of the Nashville Symphony Orchestra; Steven Mackey for "Dreamhouse" from the Boston Modern Orchestra Project and Synergy Vocals; "Sacrificium - the Art of the Castra
In the opera category, the nominees are Berg for "Lulu" of the orchestra of the Royal Opera House; Adolf Hasse for "Marc Antonio e Cleopatra" by Ars Lyrica Houston; Kaija Saariaho for "L'Amour de Loin" of the Deutsches Symphonie-Orchester Berlin; Rodion Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer" of the orchestra and choir of the Mariinsky Theatre
Grammy-nominated composers for contemporary compositions include Pärt for Symphony No. 4, Daugherty for "Deus Ex Machina", Hans Werner Henze for "Appassionately Plus", Magnus Lindberg for "Graffiti" and Shchedrin for "The Enchanted Wanderer".
Pärt’s Symphony No. 4 premiered at Disney Hall in January 2009 in a session recorded by the Los Angeles Philharmonic, directed by Esa-Pekka Salonen.
The piece, approximately 30 minutes, is nicknamed "Los Angeles" and is dedicated to former oil tycoon Mikhail Khodorkovsky, imprisoned in Russia.
Dorman's "Mandolin Concerto" is nominated for instrumental solo performance with orchestra.
The Los Angeles composer wrote the 15-minute piece specifically for him to be played in mandolin Avi Avital, who performed the New York-based Metropolis Ensemble.
"He composed it with sounds from the Middle East and numerous North African scales and rhythms," Dorman said in a telephone interview.
Conductor Pierre Boulez, 85, was nominated in the orchestra rating category for conducting the Chicago Symphony Orchestra during a recording of Stravinsky's works.
Nominated instrumental soloists include pianists Nelson Freire and Marc-André Hamelin; organist Paul Jacobs; violinist Julia Fischer; and harpist Sarah Schuster Ericsson.
Nominees as vocal soloists are all women this year: Anne Sofie Von Otter, Cecilia Bartoli, Lucia Duchonová, Vivica Genaux and Measha Brueggergosman.
In the classic mixing category, soprano Jessye Norman was nominated for her recording of "Roots - My Life, My Song."
The proposed mosque in Temecula obtains the approval of the commission of order
Temecula’s mosque project has garnered the unanimous support of the local planning commission after more than five hours of heated public testimonies.
Critics attacked the project saying it fosters terrorism and traffic jams.
However, the members of the commission decided on Wednesday night that the new religious center respected local legislation and would be aesthetically impressive.
"Many good people came to show their support, both Christians and Jews or Bahais, of all religions," Hadi Nael, president of the Temecula Valley Islamic centre, said Thursday morning.
"The truth is, as an American citizen, I'm proud."
“It demonstrates that justice prevails.”
"Opposition is really minimal," he adds.
"They just talk very loud."
“In this valley there are wonderful people.”
The meeting began at six in the afternoon and lasted practically until midnight; most of the time was devoted to people who interceded in favor or against the center, practically the same amount on each side, according to Press-Enterprise.
Debate continued on the newspaper's website.
"The United States no longer exists," one of the comments reads.
“It’s about another planet of the apes with Mrs. Liberty dead in the sand.”
"America has fallen."
Plans to build Islamic centers have also sparked protests in other parts of the country, including near ground zero, the New York area where the September 11, 2011 attacks took place.
In Temecula Valley, Imam Mahmoud Harmoush has denounced acts of violence.
His congregation of about 135 families have been living smoothly in Riverside County for years, but he wanted a space greater and better than the warehouse he rented on a shared property with a manufacturer of water filtering systems.
The new 24,943-square-foot mosque, with a Mediterranean architectural style, would include two minarets and rise to 43 feet.
It would occupy an area of 4.32 acres on Nicolas Road, about two miles east of the Chaparral Institute.
Opponents can still appeal the project to the City Council.
An arsenic-resistant microbe raises the possibility of new forms of life on Earth... or elsewhere
The discovery of a strange bacteria that can use arsenic as one of its nutrients expands the possibility of finding new forms of life on Earth and possibly on other planets.
Although the researchers discovered the curious bacteria here on Earth, they say it proves that it is possible that there are forms of life without the main elements they considered essential.
"This organism has a dual capacity."
“It can grow either with phosphorus or with arsenic.”
“That makes it very peculiar, albeit it’s hard enough to be a type of really ‘alien’ way of life,” said Paul C. W. Davies of Arizona State University, a co-author of the report featured in Thursday’s online issue of the Science publication.
It has long been considered that there are six great essential elements for life: carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus and sulphur.
However, researchers have found that the bacterium, found in Mono Lake, California, may continue to grow after replacing phosphorus with arsenic.
"It makes you wonder what else is possible," said Ariel D. Anbar of Arizona State University, co-author of the report.
According to Anbar, the finding is important for the search for life on planets other than Earth, since researchers need to be able to recognize life, to know what it looks like.
The study focuses on a microbe discovered on Earth.
However, the convening of a press conference to discuss it, in which no details of the discovery were disclosed, generated great speculation on the Internet about the possibility that the report indicated the discovery of extraterrestrial life.
He didn't.
What the discovery "yes shows is that in other planetary environments, there could be organisms that use other elements to boost their biochemistry and that the set of "standard" elements that we consider indispensable for life may not be so fixed," said Charles Cockell, professor at the Planetary and Space Sciences Research Institute, Open University, in Milton Keynes, United Kingdom.
Cockell wasn’t part of the research team.
"This work is novel because it demonstrates the substitution of one element for another in biochemistry and basic biochemical structure," Cockell added.
The discovery did not happen by chance.
Felisa Wolfe-Simon of NASA’s astrobiology institute, who led the study, focused on Monkey Lake because it has elevated levels of arsenic.
Arsenic and phosphorus are similar from the chemical point of view, so it speculated that a microbe exposed to both could replace each other.
"Arsenic is mostly toxic because its chemical behavior is similar to that of phosphorus."
"Accordingly, it can be difficult for organisms to differentiate them."
"However, arsenic is different enougher that it doesn't work as well as phosphorus, so it comes in and, to put it in some way, slains the biochemical machinery," Anbar explains.
The researchers harvested bacteria known as GFAJ-1 and exposed them to increasing concentrations of arsenic, to which they managed to adapt and used to grow.
Microbe grows best with phosphorus, but proving that it can live with arsenic in its place raises the possibility that there may naturally be a form of life that uses arsenic, either in other areas of the Earth or on another planet or moon where arsenic is more common.
Jamie S. Foster, assistant professor of microbiology at the University of Florida, said the idea that arsenic can be replaced by phosphorus is not new, but an example had never been found to prove it works.
Arsenic was more common in the early ages of the Earth, he noted, so the researchers speculated that the first forms of life may have used it.
"It does suggest that there could be other means of generating life, not just the way life was formed on Earth in the past," said Foster, who was not part of Wolfe-Simon's research team.
The research was supported by NASA, the Department of Energy and the National Institutes of Health.
Stolen dogs missing from Compton have been found
The two dogs missing from the stolen four have been founded in front of the Bellflower Motel, Los Angeles County sheriff's deallers said Thursday.
The four racing dogs, two Akitas and two Welsh Corgi Pembroke, went missing on Tuesday when someone stole the 2006 Chevrolet Express pickup in which they were spending the night in front of the Bellflower Motel 6, authorities said.
Two of the canines recovered Wednesday afternoon and the remaining two were found Wednesday, late at night, according to Steve Whitmore, a spokesman for the Los Angeles County Sheriff's Department.
"The good news is that dogs are okay," Whitmore said.
"They're in good shape."
"They're back with their rightful owners."
Whitmore did not want to indicate exactly where the dogs were, in which neighborhood, or the circumstances of the discovery.
"An investigation is underway."
"We don't want to say anything," Whitmore said.
"We're following some clues."
Don’t find the dog owners, Kristina Rickard and David Peek, to comment Thursday.
They met with their two Akitas at a county animal shelter in Downey on Wednesday afternoon.
Sheriff's deputies found the van and the two dogs separately in Compton.
According to authorities, the Akitas (Trace, a 3-year-old dog, and Didi, a 10-month-old bitch) had not suffered damage.
But Bunny and Peter, the Corgis that Rickard and Peek intended to exhibit in local competitions this week on behalf of the Japanese owners of the dogs, were still missing until Wednesday night.
Peek and Rickard, who lived in Redding, were in town for the AKC/Eukanuba national championship in Long Beach this weekend and were intended to showcase Trace.
The other dogs were going to exhibit them in other competitions scheduled for the days leading up to the AKC/Eukanuba championship, Peek told The Times on Wednesday.
Southern California is one of the stops on the couple’s two-month tour participating in canine competitions.
On Monday night, Peek and Rickard checked-in at the motel.
They usually stay in motels and park the van right in front of their bedroom window.
They carried three other dogs (a boxer brood, a Parson Russell terrier and a Brittany, who slept with them in the motel room.
But they left the Akitas and Corgis in the van because they have a very thick fur and would be cooler.
They left the windows slightly open so that the dogs could breathe fresh air.
Peek and Rickard denounced the robbery of the van Tuesday at six in the morning.
Peek had said that they would offer a reward to anyone who could drive them to the Corgis, equipped with microchips to identify them.
On Thursday it was not clear whether it would be given a reward or not.
The lion is ready to roar again, as the restructuring of MGM gets final court approval
After 18 months of confusion and uncertainty, MGM has finally made the green light to start again.
On Thursday, a New York federal bankruptcy judge officially approved the “pre-armed” plan by creditors to erase approximately US$5 billion in debt, paving the way for the 86-year-old entertainment company to focus its efforts on producing and premiering movies and TV shows.
Thursday’s ruling confirms that the plan meets U.S. Bankruptcy Code requirements, so there are no other legal hurdles for MGM to relaunce under Spyglass Entertainment founder Gary Barber and Roger Birnbaum, who will become top executives of what will be a small studio.
Personnel will be reduced from 400 to approximately 320 members.
In reality, MGM won’t leave Chapter 11 until JPMorgan Chase shuts down a planned 500-million-dollar credit instrument to finance the operations and productions of the new MGM.
The investment bank is currently syndicating credit, which on its own is contributing US$75 million and is expected to be financed in mid-December.
The study’s restructuring process began in May 2009, when investment bank Moelis and Co. was hired to give advice on the management needed to order its financies, while struggling to make interest payments for US$300 million a year.
In August, Director Harry Sloan was replaced by Stephen Cooper, a former Enron president and bankrupt business recovery expert, known for his work at Krispy Kreme Doughnuts.
Moelis and Cooper’s attempts to resolve MGM’s financial tribulations and satisfy the study’s more than 140 creditors required much longer than expected.
After several months of discussions, at the end of 2009 the parties agreed to put the company up for sale.
However, the auction attracted few bidders and none willing to pay the more than 2 billion dollars that creditors were looking for.
Prior to the spring of this year, he returned to the design table as the MGM team began working on a restructuring plan that, after months of debate and internal disagreements, was finally backed by creditors in late October.
When MGM officially goes out of bankruptcy in the coming weeks, Moelis and Cooper companies will receive multi-billion dollar bonuses for their work.
Moelis and Co., which has already received 1.2 million dollars, will get a payment of 9.5 million dollars.
Cooper’s companies, which have been raising US$375,000 per month for their company management services, will receive “success fees” for US$4 million.
Barber and Birnbaum plan to resume production on MGM, which premiered just one film this year, and assembled an annual slate of seven or eight films coming out in 2012.
They also plan to co-finance two films based on "The Hobbit", along with Warner Bros.
The studio needs to arrange a separate loan of US$265 to US$275 million to finand its share of those two films, which will be directed by Peter Jackson.
Investor Carl Icahn, who owns approximately 18% of MGM’s debt, had been trying to fix a studio merger with Lions Gate Entertainment, in which he is the largest shareholder.
However, recent talks between the two companies did not result in an agreement.
Tech Toys wants to keep the balance of their gift budget
While the iPad is the main candidate to be this year’s most wonderful toy, followed by, perhaps, the Microsoft Kinect video game system, both can mean a significant dent in their gift budget.
Consider that for the price of an iPad you can buy five BMX bikes of 99 dollars each, and a Kinect game costs the same as 10 classic jump strings adorned with beads, or about 48 play-Doh cans.
But there are ways to keep your budget for gifts in balance.
I recently visited three national toy vendors, Wal-Mart, Toys "R" Us and BJ's Wholesale Club, to see which electronic toys I could buy for less than 80 dollars.
It was immediately clear that Moore’s Law has been working; this year’s tech toy will likely have more power, costing the same and even a little less compared to last year.
In addition, while there are generally fewer newer toys with associated websites, two of the most well-known, Webkinz and Club Penguin, have more offers.
And toys powered by button batteries are increasing, as well as toys with rechargeable batteries.
Here are some recommendations.
(All prices are from Toys "R" Us, unless indicated otherwise.)
Sing-a-ma-jigs (Mattel, US$13, for 3 years and over) are guiney-sized stuffed toys that sing a note when their bellies are squeezed.
A squeeze equates to a note, and a second squeeze begins to put together a song.
If you buy more than one, they will sing in harmony.
Caution: Once it starts squeezing, it can't stop.
For singing combined with dance, Dance Star Mickey (Fisher-Price, 60 dollars, for 2 years and more) is the best option.
Borrowing from last year’s Elmo Live technology, the 14-inch animatronic doll plays turn-shifting games like Simóm Dice, and can do the lunar passage, thanks to its innovative articulated feet with wheels with locking device.
Children who tried the toy used Mickey as a dance instructor, though their parents complained about the lack of volume control.
If you’re an 80’s kid, you can remember Alphie, one of the first computerized educational toys.
He has returned (Hasbro/Playskool, 40 dollars, for 3 years and more), with a backpack full of question cards designed to turn a car ride into a session with an autocorrection exercise notebook occurring.
If you are wary to give your child your smartphone, here you’ll find three independent alternatives.
MobiGo from VTech (60 dollars, from 3 to 8 years old) is the cheapest and the easiest to use, presenting a qwerty keyboard; although the games do not teach as effectively as the Leapster Explorer (70 dollars, from 3 to 12 years old), it is the first important set-up of the Leapster line, with a newer and brighter screen.
None version is compatible with old cartridges.
Fisher-Price’s iXL (U.S.$90, 3 to 7 years old) uses an iTunes-type system to introduce games and e-books, and has an SD card slot that lets you view family photos.
Finally, Scrabble Flash (30 dollars, Hasbro, for 8 years and more) is a set of five blocks barely larger than a box of matches, each with its own LED screen.
The goal is to put together words by placing the blocks in a row before time is over; it’s formidable for improvised word-arming competition.
There are more and more toys that have some kind of online connection.
Based only on their presence in toy stores, the two largest are Webkinz and Club Penguin.
To buy your child a pet online at Club Penguin, for example, you can get a Disney Club Penguin 2" Mix 'N Match Figure Pack, (5 dollars, from 6 to 12 years old) that includes a coin with a hidden code.
When you redeem code online, your child gets enough credits at the Penguin Club store for two items.
These could include roller skates or a Puffle pet toy.
Slightly larger than the bristle of your toothbrush, Hexbug Nano (10 dollars, for 5 years and more) is the latest in a line of button-powered microrobots.
The false insect moves quickly on 12 vibrating silicone legs, in random directions.
It's fun to make a labyrinth of blocks to see if you can find your way out.
It's durable, too.
Ours was able to leave the kitchen table and even spend some time inside our dog’s mouth.
A more moderate side of order maintenance is promoted in China
As an urban sergeant, Tang Shenbin marked the passage in a city square, severely inspecting the nervous people in his charge, issuing low-loud orders with military authority.
He wanted the female members of Chengguan, the burly police officers of China’s urban order, feared and despised for their whimsical crackdowns and their inclination to violence, to communicate a certain impression to a handful of spectators.
"Firm!"
"Attention!"
Show them, she whispered, "How are the pretty girls!"
Four girls who barely spent adolescence with white gloves and identical olive green jackets and tuned pants to attract attention.
Four pairs of perfectly aligned black shoes.
Four elegant hats placed perfectly on top of hair tied with blue and white striped buns.
“Personally, I think they have a normal appearance,” said Mr. Tang disdainfully.
"Models are cute."
More than one government has tried to clear the image of China’s urban inspectors.
One city ordered that all new recruits have a degree.
Guangdong Province changed the grayish-green color of uniforms to a supposedly more attractive blue.
Wuhan, in central China, replaced the use of force with intimidating glances: in 2009, according to a report, 50 officers surrounded a discolous snack cart, and watched it with a frown constantly for half an hour, until the street vendor picked up his things and left.
Xindu, an urban district of 680,000 in Chengdu, has chosen a radical makeover.
Since 2003, the district has added to its urban police 13 women, expressly chosen for their appearance, proportion and youth.
The idea is to give the coarse police a softer, more feminine side.
Sadly, even Scarlett Johansson would have to fight to increase China’s tiny respect for these city squadrons.
And for a good reason, critics would argue.
Unlike the police, these officers are authorized only to enforce city ordinances, imposing fines and other administrative penalties.
But the Chinese news outlets routinely portray a different reality.
In January 2008, Hubei Province inspectors beated a passer-by after using their cell phone to film them while dissolving a protest against a waste landfill.
Last year, a training manual for Beijing’s inspectors was stolen and posted on the internet, describing how to effectively whip criminals without bleeding them.
This year, a watermelon street vendor from Shanghai suffered brain damage after a fight with five officers.
A video game full of violence, available to download online, features Chinese-trained inspectors attacking street vendors.
“Chengguan has left a scar in the government,” said China Daily, a national publication last year after another controversy about tactics.
The newspaper demanded "really meticulous cleaning."
Skeptics say that the current approach is far from this.
After the district requested through a notice eight new female recruits in October, an editorial in The Beijing Evening News wondered if the women had real duties or were simply a scenic distraction.
The answer seems to be a bit of both.
The district's notice called for women's candidates aged 18 to 22, with a good figure "and the five harmonious facial features."
They had to have an above-average height, greater than 1.6 meters or 5 feet, 2 1⁄2 inches.
Retirement at the age of 26 is mandatory.
Officials said the work was physically too arduous for women over the age of 25.
“His image is the important thing,” an anonymous district official told Rednet.com, a quasi-governmental website.
"First, the external qualities of the candidates will determine whether they meet the requirements, such as height, weight, facial features, etc."
Then comes temperament "and inner qualities."
The female chenggans are like vases, she said, adding, "in addition to being vases, they will have other responsibilities."
Zheng Lihua, deputy director of the district city’s administration office, is unwilling to support that description.
But he noted that height demands were standard in many Chinese job advertisements for both sexes.
Then comes the demand for harmonious facial features.
If this means to be handsome, it’s a matter of debate among the Chinese.
Surely the handicapped or disfigured should not occur.
“We can’t allow a lame person or a hunchback to come to work here,” Mr. Zheng said.
"His image wouldn't be okay."
Liu Yi, who patrols Baoguang Square near a 22-year-old monastery, with its red cheeks and a subtly curved mouth.
She doesn't think the accent on her look is sexist, she said.
"Do you think I look sexy in this uniform?" he asked with a sardonic look.
His dimpled co-worker Xu Yang, 21, said "Our work must present the picture of the city."
They also don’t object to their determined-time work, they said, because they harbor major career ambitions than simply scaring away vendors in the alleys where they are supposed to circumscribe their business.
Every morning, the squad faces roughly a dozen street vendors throwing themselves on foot or by bike, trying to sell as many muffins or tofu bowls as possible before being thrown out.
"Mr. Wang, you've got to leave."
“We’ve told you many times!” Ms. Xu said as a vendor escaped on foot, temporarily abandoning his bike-drawn cart with noodle linings.
Officers describe their duties as more monotonous than exhausting.
"This is pretty much the same every day," said Huang Jing, 20, who studies marketing in his free hours.
"Very Routine."
One reason is that female officers lack the power of their male counterparts to confiscate the merchadery of vendors.
They can only threaten to denounce offenders to their male supervisors.
This tends to protect them from sudden public demonstrations of animosity against bureaucracy, which are common throughout China.
This year, hundreds of citizens in Kunming, Yunnan’s provincial capital, rioted after the false rule that chengguan officers had killed a salesman.
More than a dozen chengguan polices or officers were injured in the night episode; 14 government vehicles were overturned or set on fire.
Xindu has prevented such violence.
Calm is barely guaranteed.
Just two blocks from quiet Baoguang Square, where female officers patrolled that morning, more than 50 people gathered in a corner.
Officers had confiscated a motorcycle that was being repaired on the sidewalk rather than in a workshop, as required by the regulations.
The owner of the bike was screaming rudely.
The situation stagnated for 15 minutes before the officers, with a bad face, made their way to their elbows to their vehicles and left quickly with the motorcycle and its owner.
Li Xuedong, 40, a coordinator assigned to the men’s squadron, remained behind, with his white badge turned around to hide his name.
Like female officers, coordinators, men 40 and older, play a purely supportive role.
Unlike them, they aren’t taught to maintain an elegant image.
“Sometimes we fight verbally.”
"Sometimes we fight physically," Mr. Li said naturally.
"Most of the time it's the audience starting the fight."
Prosecutors won't bring charges against Senator Ensign
The senator’s attorneys announced Wednesday that Justice Department prosecutors have exculpated Senator John Ensign from the criminal indictment that arised from his affair with a former campaign attendant and his efforts to secure work through lobby for the woman’s husband.
The decision ends with the most serious legal threat Facing Ensign, the Nevada Republican who was seen as a potential Republican presidential candidate for 2012, until the scandal over his affair made national headlines last year and damaged his career.
Ensign’s office said he was “surely pleased” by the decision, which came after a full-year investigation.
The senator could still face disciplinary action from the Senate ethics committee.
Ensign’s attorneys said the Justice Department had “informed us that Senator Ensign is no longer an objective of his investigation and that he has no plan to bring charges against him on this issue.”
The Justice Department declined to comment on the case.
The announcement came as a surprise to many ethics attorneys outside the Justice Department, who said there appeared to be significant evidence to indicate that Ensign may have colluded to violating a federal criminal ban on lobby.
Interviews, e-mail, and other documents that were first released in October 2009 in the New York Times indicated that Ensign had helped Douglas Hampton, who was one of his aides, to obtain lucrative work through lobby, to achieve the silence of Hampton and his wife, Cynthia, about the senator’s affair with her.
Mrs. Hampton once worked as the treasurer of an Ensign campaign.
Ensign contacted several political sponsors to find Mr. Hampton work and advocated on their behalf after they had hired Mr. Hampton to lobby them, according to documents and interviews.
As Hampton had recently been an aide to Ensign, he was excluded under the law of lobbying for his former employer or the Senate for a year.
Ethical experts said the unusual arrangement between Ensign and Hampton, who were close friends before the affair, came into disagreement with the so-called “revolving door lobbying ban,” which seeks to restrict former state employees from using their connections to do personal business.
“It appeared to be a prima facie case of a conspiracy to substantially violate the revolving door statute,” said Stanley Brand, a Washington attorney who specializes in defending clients investigated by the government.
While Justice Department officials refused to explain the reasoning behind the decision in the Ensign case, Mr. Brand and other ethics attorneys said the department appeared wary of accepting political prosecutions after several major setbacks for its public integrity section, including last year’s decision to drop prosecution of former Senator Ted Stevens, amid procedural misconduct charges.
Federal prosecutors “are clearly cowards,” said Melanie Sloan, executive director of Citizens for Accountability and Ethics in Washington, an advocacy group that brought several complaints against Ensign in the case.
“This basically means that high-ranking government officials can now assume the laws don’t apply to them.”
Paul Coggins, Ensign’s top lawyer, said in an interview that he received a phone call from a Justice Department prosecutor on Monday and another call the next day, telling him that the department didn’t want to raise charges against his client.
Ensign's defense team had been cooperating with prosecutors in an attempt to persuade them that the senator, as his office reiterated in a statement Wednesday, "acted in accordance with the law."
Coggins declined to explain in detail the legal arguments he gave to prosecutors.
"I don't feel comfortable going into details about my discussions with the Department of Justice, I can only say that we think they made the right decision," he said.
Last month, the Federal Elections Commission decided to change an investigation into a separate complaint by the ethics group, which alleges that a US$96,000 payment from Ensign’s parents to the Hamptons violated federal election law.
The electoral commission said it didn’t have enough evidence to prove that the money was nothing but a gift.
NV Energy is a major Nevada company that had long supported the senator, who got a job through lobby thanks to Ensign’s help to Hampton.
An email sent in December 2008 showed that after the company accepted Hampton as a lobbyist, Ensign’s chief of staff, John Lopez, communicated with Hampton and told him he was “crushing” the Interior Ministry on a disputed issue.
Ensign also attended a lunch with Hampton and another Nevada client, Allegiant Air, and called the transportation secretary to set up a meeting on behalf of the airline after he had hired Hampton to lobby.
When affair news first appeared last year, Ensign apologized to his constituents and renounced his position as fourth Republican leader in the Senate.
After maintaining a low profile for many months, two weeks ago Ensign told reporters he planned to run for a third term in the Senate.
When Ensign’s spokesman, Jennifer Cooper, announced that Ensign was no longer the subject of a criminal investigation, he said the senator hoped the ethics committee would quickly follow the Justice Department’s example and conclude its investigation.
"Senator Ensign has a desire to continue his difficult work on behalf of the people of Nevada," he said.
Cheshire's appeal will target Twitter
There were half a dozen of them "chatting" in the ranks of the courtroom.
Minute by minute, they sent reports of the gruesome testimony via Twitter.
In the end, the defense says, there were more than 140,000 Twitter messages about the process that addressed the rape and the children tied to their beds in a house that was set on fire.
On Thursday, the widely followed trial of Steven J. Hayes, who was convicted of the murder of three members of a Cheshire, Connecticut family, is ready to reach an official end, with the imposition of the death penalty judge voted by the jury.
But Mr. Hayes’ attorneys have filed briefs in court that outline the arguments of the appeals they are likely to occupy the courts for years.
One of the most provocative states that intense reports of the process, including the widespread use of Twitter by mass media journalists, created “a circus atmosphere,” and such widespread and immediate saturation with exalting details caused public passions to exert inappropriate influence over the jury.
The claim could force appellate judges to deal with the question of whether the new technology requires new rules in the courtroom.
“According to what I experienced during this process, I think there is a real danger that the inclusion of the new technology in the courtroom is affecting the individual’s rights to having a fair trial,” said Thomas J. Ullmann, Mr. Hayes’s defense chief counsel.
State Superior Court trial judge Jon C. Blue laconically rejected the lawsuits in a ruling last week, saying the news outlets were "carefully controlled" and that there was no evidence that the jury had been led by passion.
But it probably won't be the last word on the subject.
For decades, appellate courts have recognized that coverage of celebrate cases can raise questions about the impartiality of a process.
In a landmark 1966 ruling, the U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. U.S. Supreme Court overturned the conviction of an osteopath from Cleveland, Dr. Sam Sheppard, for the murder of his wife, saying in his decision, in the Sheppard vs. Maxwell case, that there was "an atmosphere of carnival in the process" because of the media.
It is believed that the crime had been inspired by the TV show and the movie "The Fugitive".
Some lawyers said the appeals courts that examined Mr. Hayes’ conviction half a century later could be intrigued by the argument that new rules might be needed to control the form of communication from courtrooms, something unimaginable in Dr. Sheppard’s case.
"There's a general cause for concern about the impact of the new media" on high-profile processes, said John J. Donohue III, a Stanford School of Law professor, who studied Connecticut's death penalty and wrote a report saying it was arbitrarily imposed.
Professor Donohue said some appellate judges might be intrigued by the idea that limiting immediate communication from courtrooms could make trials fairer.
The issue of Twitter is just one of many issues outlined by Hayes’ attorneys, which will likely be discussed for years, delaying an execution.
Defense attorneys also claim, for example, that Hayes could have been harmed when Judge Blue refused to allow them to test the reactions of potential jurors, showing them creepy photographs of the crime scene during jury selection.
Some jurors have said they were horrified by photographs of the victims, Jennifer Hawke-Petit, 48, and their daughters, Hayley, 17, and Michaela, 11.
Defense lawyers say they will also question the constitutionality of the death penalty and argue that the judge incorrectly removed a jury that had criticized prosecutors, and that he had incorrectly pushed for a verdict for his bizarre decision to order the jury to deliberate for a weekend.
Jurors were not abducted.
But their arguments about the effect of intense coverage of the process may attract most interest.
They observed in a recusal of the verdict filed last month that the case dominated the news in Connecticut and was discussed during the election campaign by candidates for governor and the U.S. Senate.
They argued that the media were continually fueling the fire of emotion about the case.
“Daily agglomeration of media tweets, chambers, and reporters out-of-court,” the attorneys wrote, “was something never seen here in New Haven and maybe across the state.”
Your attention on Tweeter coverage is controversial.
In some interviews, some journalists said the complaint about the fact that immediate news was more important than the reports delivered more slowly, were a ludity view that would make the court’s policy on coverage control weak in future processes.
But some attorneys said appellate courts were often drawn to arguments about adaptation that was required in the courts due to social and technological changes.
Hayes’ attorneys are pushing for that claim.
In their brief, they referred to the 1966 Supreme Court decision, which overturns Dr. Sheppard’s conviction due to coverage of the process, and made it clear that they would demand that the new media would require new rules.
"This case," they wrote, "represents the Sheppard vs. Maxwell case of the 21st century."
Can Civismo build a bridge to unite divisions?
After a remarkable campaign for invectiveness and negativity, this week has triggered a bit of civism in Washington.
Is it a harbinger of genuine future bipartisan cooperation or an illusory time before a quick return to partisan combat in the capital?
Tuesday’s meeting between President Obama and congressional leaders made no progress on the big issues that should be addressed during the outgoing session.
But from what everyone says, it was at least a polite discussion about the differences of the two sides, as well as a recognition of their shared responsibilities to govern, rather than being another empty episode to earn partisan points.
Wednesday’s public meeting of the president’s deficit commission was equally civilized, despite political differences remaining between members.
It seems clear that the close interaction over a period of months generated a mutual respect that transcends the ideological gap that still separates the members.
That's barely enough to declare that a new era of cooperation has arrived.
Now both sides are in a probationary period, probing each other to identify signs of cooperativism and engagement, as they try to assess the limits of public opinion and voter patience for a renewed partisan war.
Reason why Tuesday’s meeting and subsequent assessments of it by the participants were made with a minimum of demands or accusations.
The philosophical differences between Obama and the Republicans are too large and too deeply entrenched to suggest any rapid climate changes that existed over the past two years.
Whether it’s taxes, expenses, health care or social assistance, the two parties are at opposite poles.
However, November’s election has softened the mood in Washington, at least in the short term.
“There’s no doubt that things have changed with regard to how they say things,” said Pete Wehner, a Bush administration official now at the Center for Ethics and Public Policy.
"They're unavoidable after the election.
Elections arouse party passions, which are then extinguished.
That's good."
On some issues, in particular those on the agenda of the outgoing session, Republicans and Democrats have no choice but to find a solution.
Bush’s tax reductions expire at the end of the year, and no one is in favor of this.
Both parties want to find a way to preserve some or all of the reductions, permanently or temporarily.
Similarly, there is likely to be a commitment to expanding unemployment benefits, probably because Republicans can’t believe they’re in favor of tax reductions for the wealthy, denying unemployment subsidy for unemployed workers.
After Tuesday’s meeting at the White House, there’s greater optimism for a possible dealing on these issues during the outgoing session, along with the ratification of the new START (Strategic Arms Reduction Treaty) nuclear disarmament pact.
But William Galston, of the Brookings Institution, warns that successful cooperation on the pressing issues of the outgoing session will not necessarily lead to continued bipartisanship when the new 112th Congress meets in January.
On the contrary, he stated that it is very likely and even unavoidable that a period of disagreement will begin.
"After the things that need to be done are carried out, in the short term psychology will revert the situation to one of confrontation," he said.
Wehner also expressed skepticism (which is widely shared) about the fact that civics can lead to true cooperation.
"I see most of what will happen between now and 2012 as an essay for the main event of 2012," he said.
The reasons have much to do with the constitution of the new Republican majority in the House and the voters of the Tea Party, who contributed decisively to its creation.
No matter how conscientious Republican leaders have about the dangers of exaggerating their position, they know that they should try to fulfill their promises to cut state spending and override the new health care law.
Those party lines are already clearly seen in the initial reactions to the recommendations of the deficit commission, which include the reform of the tax code and the increase of some taxes and the retirement age, and the spending cut.
Co-Chairs Alan Simpson and Erskine Bowles have taken care of one of the most difficult jobs in Washington and, according to their own assessment, have forcibly introduced the issue of the deficiency on public agenda in a way that cannot be ignored.
But they need 14 votes on the 18-member commission to publish their plan as an official recommendation, and they have been struggling to get close to that figure.
The greatest resistance is among the officials elected to the panel, suggesting that in fiscal matters there are, at least currently, little basis or desire for commitment.
Galston predicts two phases in battles between White House Republicans and those of Congress.
The first will happen next year in the form of supporting clashes, when Republicans challenge Obama about the budget, taxes and medical care, followed by a period of increased bipartisan cooperation.
What would cause change?
public opinion.
There is considerable evidence that, despite the partisanship of the recent election, a majority of Americans still want to see the two parties working together.
If the confrontation leads to a stalemate, as happened in 1995, then both parties will look closely at the reaction of the people.
Whoever they think you’re losing will likely change course and seek commitment.
However, this can be an optimistic view.
Partisanship and polarization are currently greater than they were in the mid-1990’s, and there appears to be a larger ideological gap between Obama and congressional Republicans than there were between President Bill Clinton and Republicans at the time.
At the same time, activists from both parties see 2010 as the mere prelude to a more momentous election in 2012.
Given this reality, the civilism shown this week may be welcomed by many Americans.
But this will require much more effort from both sides to find commonalities on the issues that still divide them.
Leaked U.S. document portrays Moscow as paradise of corruption
The control of the city is in the hands of a "kleptocracy", leaving a part of the bribes and protection money that it collects directly from the Kremlin, the U.S. Embassy in Russia reported in February.
It was reported that both the police and the Federal Security Service handle huge blackmail systems, helping to explain the high cost of living in Moscow.
They raise money not only from legitimate businesses, but also from organized criminal groups.
Every stratum of bureaucracy, what Prime Minister Vladimir Putin has dubbed the “vertical authority,” takes its part as money rises on the scale.
The note, which was sent to Washington under the name of Ambassador John Beyrle, was posted by WikiLeaks on its website Wednesday.
The note is based on sources whose names have been censored.
It was written while Yuri Luzhkov was still mayor of Moscow, and he blames it for much of the corruption.
President Dmitry Medvedev fired him in October, but there has been little evidence of a clean-up ever since.
Luzhkov and his wife, Yelena Baturina, who owns a major construction company, have systematically denied allegations of corruption over the years, and he has won several lawsuits for libelo about such accusations.
In September, when on a Russian TV channel he was asked about a documentary that suggested he was corrupt, he said: "It's insane, it's a hoax, a nuisance."
According to polls, the Russians overwhelmingly believe that their society is corrupt.
But it is unusual to find the details explained in detail, since they were in the report of the embassy, and it is unhearded that it is the diplomats of a foreign country to do that.
“The direct links of the government of the city of Moscow with criminality have led some to call it ‘dysfunctional,’ and to claim that the government functions more like a kleptocracy than as a government.”
“Criminal elements enjoy a ‘krysha’ (a terminus of the criminal world and the mafia that literally means ‘roof’ or protection) that crosses the police, the Federal Security Service (SFS), the Ministry of Internal Affairs (MVD), and the prosecutor’s office, as well as all sectors of the bureaucracy of the Moscow city government.
"Analysts identify a three-level structure in Moscow's criminal world."
"Luzhkov is at the top."
"The SFS, MVD and militia are on the second level."
"Finally, ordinary criminals and corrupt inspectors are at the lowest level."
"This is an ineffective system in which criminal groups fill a void in some areas, because the city doesn't provide some services."
The report states that the SFS raises the money of the largest companies, and that the police are aimed at small businesses.
One source, the report stated, “explained that Moscow’s business owners understand that it’s best to get protection from the MVD and SFS (rather than organized crime groups) since they not only have more weapons, resources and power than criminal groups, but are also protected by law.”
"For this reason, protection against criminal gangs is no longer a priority."
The note notes that, while raising money is complete, protection can be irregular; even those who pay are subject to arrest.
Those who don’t pay quickly find their businesses closed for one pretext or another.
Medvedev has spoken several times about the fight against corruption, but has confessed that it has made little progress.
The embassy’s note reports on men taking suitcases, probably full of cash, inside Kremlin itself.
"In its fight against corruption, Medvedev has to rely on bureaucrats," Georgy Satarov, director of Indem's Moscow expert group, said in a recent interview.
“But he’s a part of this bureaucracy.”
"He doesn't belong to a political class, because there's no longer a political class in Russia."
Without politics, without an opposition, without a separation of powers, he claimed, corruption is inevitable.
Nationwide, Indem estimates that corruption costs Russia more than 300 billion dollars per year.
In a recent survey on global perceptions of corruption conducted by the non-governmental organization Transparency International, the country was ranked 154 (countries are classified from least to most corrupt).
The revelation of the U.S. Embassy’s note, which didn’t happen until late at night Moscow time, will surely cause disgust within the Russian government, and even though Luzhkov may be blamed for Moscow’s weaknesses, it could be an opportunity for the Kremlin to argue that it’s fixing the problem.
Interviewed for CNN by Larry King, Putin suggested the WikiLeaks documents could be lies and reacted furiously by a revelation claiming U.S. diplomats called him Batman and Medvedev Robin.
"The truth of the matter is that this relates to our interaction, which is an important factor of domestic policies in this country," he said.
"But to be honest with you, we don't suspect that this would have been done with such arrogance, with such pressure and, you know, it was done in such an immoral way."
Khmer Rouge veterans forced to face the past
In a weakly enlightened concrete classroom, with stained and husky walls, the director of Anlong Veng High School remembered the man who had built it, the last leader of the Khmer Rouge Ta Mok.
“Everyone here loves Ta Mok.”
"He was a good leader and cared about his people," Sreng Kor, 42, said.
Known as "the Butcher" for its brutality during the Khmer Rouge government, the commander remains popular in this remote former Khmer Rouge stronghold in northwestern Cambodia, where he built hospitals, bridges and other infrastructure, and where thousands of former organization soldiers still live.
But this year, 12 years after the Khmer Rouge surrendered to the government, long-held loyalty is finally being challenged in Anlong Veng.
In April, a local forum for truth and reconciliation allowed victims to publicly confront people who had participated in the regime.
In June, the government circulated here a high school textbook that for the first time teaches the history of the Khmer Rouges to the sons of their former soldiers.
And in July, a United Nations-Cambodia mixed court issued the first conviction of a former Khmer Rouge member, sentencing the former head of the well-known Tuol Sleng torture center, Kaing Khek Iev, better known as Duch, to 35 years in prison.
With each of those events, anxiety has grown up among Anlong Veng’s Khmer Rouge veterans, complicating efforts for reconciliation and their attempts to reintegrate into Cambodian society.
“There’s resentment and fear among former Khmer Rouges, but they can’t do anything,” said Chhang Youk, director of the Cambodian Documentation Center, an independent organization investigating the regime.
"For them, life under the Khmer Rouges was glorious, but the regime has become a symbol of evil."
"This is generating divisions within families."
During the Khmer Rouge government of Cambodia, between 1975 and 1979, approximately 1.7 million people were executed or died of starvation, disease or overwork.
When in 1979 the Vietnamese invaded and overthrew the government led by Pol Pot, the remnants of the regime and its military fled to Cambodia’s border with Thailand.
There they launched an insurrection that lasted until the last movement that surrendered in December 1998.
As Pol Pot, Nuon Chea and Ieng Sary directed guerrilla warfare from their bases in the mountains and jungles of western Cambodia, Ta Mok cultivated followers at Anlong Veng.
But in the mid-1990s, after a U.N.-sponsored peace deal led to the country’s first democratic elections in 1993, large groups of Khmer Rouge fighters began deserting into government, culminating in Ieng Sary’s crackdowns in 1996, and Nuon Chea and Khieu Samphan in 1998.
Pol Pot died of natural causes in 1998, and Ta Mok, who had resisted, was captured by government forces in 1999 in the nearby Dangrek Mountains.
He died in Phnom Penh prison in 2006 while awaiting trial.
Without the support of their benefactors, the older Khmer Rouge fighters fell into the absolute misery that is common in rural Cambodia, and many feel nostalgic for the movement.
Although a few elite Khmer Rouge officials retained their local administrative posts in exchange for laying down their weapons, sad soldiers remain poor and unskilled farmers.
"These people have made little profit after surrender," said Sok Leang of the Center for Justice and Reconciliation, which offers public forums throughout Cambodia, even in Anlong Veng.
“They have incorporated the utopian agrarian ideology of the regime.”
“They were bred without any concept of doing business.”
Sor Lim, 55, who joined the Khmer Rouge when he was a teenager in 1974, struggled in the jungle near Anlong Veng before devoting himself to life as a poor rice farmer in 1998.
"Life with the Khmer Rouge was fine," he said.
"Ta Mok fed everyone."
"But now life is difficult because we have to self-supply."
The current Khmer Rouge court has also caused concerns here.
Earlier next year, the court is expected to begin prosecuting Nuon Chea, Khieu Samphan, Ieng Sary and his wife, Ieng Thirith, another former Khmer Rouge minister.
The court’s mandate is to prosecute top leaders and those of greater responsibility for the crimes, but it hasn’t said whether it will pursue cases in addition to those four.
This doesn’t help much to soothe fears in Anlong Veng.
Recent media speculation has centered on 64-year-old Im Chaem who was a district chief in Banteay Meanchey province during the Khmer Rouge government in the late 1970s.
In 2007, she told investigators at the Cambodian Documentation Center that she had overseen the construction of the Trapeang Thma Dam, a project in which thousands of forced workers are thought to have died.
During a recent muggy afternoon, Im Chaem returned from working in the fields home on wooden piles on a dirt road on the outskirts of Anlong Veng.
When the sun cast long shadows over the dry grass, Im Chaem refused to talk about his past in the Khmer Rouge.
She claimed that if the court summoned her she wouldn't show up.
"Cambodia is at peace and stable," he said.
"If there's more prosecution, there'll be war."
Prime Minister Hun Sen, who defected from the Khmer Rouge in 1978, has also repeatedly warned that there will be instability if the court persecutes more suspects, though experts and historians question him.
"Cambodia must dig a hole and bury the past," he said.
But facing the past is precisely what Cambodia must do to move forward, said Chhang Youk of the documentation center.
"Reconciliation, according to Khmer, is to rejoin the broken pieces," he said.
"It's our obligation to gather these broken pieces so that we can understand."
The center produced the first government-approved textbook on the Khmer Rouge, called "A History of Democratic Kampuchea," 75-page.
It was distributed in Anlong Veng in June as a supplement to the Ministry of Education’s high school history textbook, which contains less than four pages about the Khmer Rouge.
As in most of Cambodia, the young people of Anlong Veng know few details about the Khmer Rouge, despite the city’s link to the regime.
Toque Valeak, 19, a student at Anlong Veng High School, said the new textbook helped students understand an important part of their history.
But his parents reacted with skepticism when he took the book home to study.
"My family rejects many parts of the textbook and the court," he said.
“They have suspicions because they’re not sure about the number of people the court will prosecute.”
Such resistance has made the reconciliation process complex and difficult, Sok Leang said.
But public forums, the textbook and the court are beginning to make an impact, he said.
Anyway, here the Khmer Rouges retain a mighty charm.
In the Dangrek Mountains, a path with too many weeds and covered in discarded plastic bags leads to a black soot rectangle covered by a rusty tin roof.
Pol Pot's body was burned here on a pile of tires after his death in 1998.
Nuom Sothea, 31, a cellphone salesman on the edge, said he didn’t know much about the man who was cremated there.
"But he has a strong spirit and many locals go there to pray to him," he said.
It was Nuom Sothea’s birthday, and later that day he decided to walk to the place of Pol Pot’s last break, where he would leave a bunch of ripe bananas hoping to have good luck.
Belarus agrees to give up its uranium reserve qualified for armaments
In a surprise victory for President Obama’s campaign to protect nuclear material around the world, the Belarusian government announced on Wednesday that it will give up its highly enriched uranium reserve, a critical component of nuclear weapons.
The deal was revealed in a joint statement released after Foreign Minister Hillary Rodham Clinton met Belarusian Foreign Minister Sergei Martynov on the sidelines of a security summit.
For years, the U.S. government had been trying to persuade Belarus to give up its highly enriched uranium and it looked lift that they had reached an unreturned point last spring.
But U.S. officials told the Washington Post that a pair of operations classified with Belarus were carried out over the past two months to remove 84.82 kg qualified uranium for weapons from a Belarusian research facility.
This paved the way for Wednesday's agreement to remove the rest of the material, approximately 227 kg, according to an official, who spoke from anonymity due to the confidentiality of the issue.
The deal appeared to be one of the most important results of the 47-nation Nuclear Security Summit chaired by Obama in April, which excluded Belarus.
And apparently it was a good achievement to secure the invitation of the former Soviet republic to the next summit in 2012.
"I was really fascinated by the fact that they wanted a place at the 2012 Nuclear Security Summit."
“This demonstrates that this process is working,” said Ken Luongo, president of the Society for Global Security, a group that promotes nuclear safety.
Clinton said the deal is "a very significant and important step" taken by Belarus.
In April 2009, Obama promised in Prague to protect all vulnerable nuclear material around the world in the next four years.
Since then, the U.S. government has helped six countries get rid of all their highly enriched uranium.
Belarus would be the seventh.
A dozen countries did so under the George W. Bush administration.
At the time of Obama’s Nuclear Security Summit, Belarusian President Alexander Lukashenko had declared that the nation would never give up its uranium.
“We’ve conserved highly enriched uranium (hundreds of kilograms of what is basically uranium qualified for armament) and low-enriched uranium,” Lukashenko said at the time.
"This is our raw material ... we're not going to make dirty bombs, and we're not going to sell them to anyone."
"We use it for research purposes, that's all."
However, Belarus approached the United States a few months ago, indicating interest in reaching an agreement.
Officials stated that the first task of removing uranium from Belarus, in October, was particularly complicated, as the material had been slightly radiated.
Officials said it was loaded on a special train that traveled more than 2,000 km to a Russian facility for storage and disposal.
According to officials, the second operation ended on Monday and was made by plane.
The U.S. government spent US$13 million for the tasks, and most of the money was used to supply Belarus with the installation of low-enrichment uranius investigations, which is unsuitable for nuclear weapons.
Andrew Bieniawski, an Energy Department official working on Global Threat Reduction, said "this was carefully coordinated and orchestrated to strengthen trust" that led to Clinton's signed agreement.
The James Martin Center for Nonproliferation Studies estimated this year that Belarus possessed at least 40 kilograms of uranium qualified for armaments, as well as hundreds of kilograms of highly enriched uranium.
Belarus will try to remove its reserve by 2012, according to the joint statement.
Relations between Belarus and the United States have been tense for years, mainly due to Lukashenko’s harsh repression of human rights.
But Wednesday’s statement suggested a potential strain.
“While welcoming progress on these global security issues, the United States and Belarus recognized that greater respect for democracy and human rights in Belarus remains a central issue for improving bilateral relations, and is essential to the progress of the country and its citizens.”
The Spanish government made no qualms about CIA flights
The Spanish government made no qualms about CIA flights with Taliban and al Qaeda prisoners heading to Guantanamo for Spanish territory, the U.S. ambassador to Madrid said in a telegram.
This is reflected in the documents leaked (cables) by Wikileaks to the newspaper El País, where it is added that the then first vice president of the Government, María Teresa Fernández de la Vega, asked that the Spanish Executive be informed to "be able to demonstrate that they exercised an appropriate surveillance on the foreign planes that cross Spain."
In June 2006, following a report by the Council of Europe accusing a dozen countries of complicity in anti-human rights practices, including Spain, there was a meeting between former U.S. Ambassador Eduardo Aguirre and Fernández de la Vega.
The vice president acknowledged that the report of the Council "had caught Zapatero's government offside" and insisted that Spain had nothing to hide in this matter.
"De la Vega stressed that Spain has no qualms about intelligence flights through Spanish territory."
"They just want to be informed" to avoid being "experenced catches," Aguirre recounts in a cable.
Also the then head of Foreign Affairs, Miguel Ángel Moratinos, pointed out to the ambassador that the Spanish Executive "wanted to give this matter the lowest possible profile, although, being in the hands of the judges, the Government had a limited capacity for influence."
For his part, in January 2007, prosecutor Vicente Gonzalez transmitted to the Embassy that he would not try to block the judge’s initiative to request the declassification of documents from the National Intelligence Center (CNI) and the Ministry of Defense over CIA flights.
"These reports do not contain any incriminating elements or any sensitive information," the embassy explained after speaking with the prosecutor.
However, the U.S
"This coordination between independent investigators will complicate our efforts to handle the case discreetly between governments," Aguirre said.
In March 2007 at a lunch between the U.S. ambassador and U.S. Interior Minister Alfredo Pérez Rubalcaba, he told him that "although the Spanish judges are rabidly zealous about their independence and will do what they deem appropriate", he had instructed his collaborators "to avoid comments of inflamed rhetoric, in an attempt to lower the temperature."
The ambassador also expresses his concern about the action of the PP in this matter.
“Despite its overall pro-American orientation, our experience suggests that the PP won’t hesitate to capitalize on any indication that Zapatero’s government tolerated or deliberately ignored U.S. actions that could be interpreted as possible human rights violations, even if these disclosures have negative consequences for the U.S. government.”
