The populars would win 4 seats in Congress and the PSC would lose 9
With the results of 28-N, the PP could be decisive in the municipalities of Lleida, Tarragona and Barcelona
After the significant increase in votes and deputies experienced in the recent regional elections, the Catalan PP already has an eye on the general ones because, there is an advance or not, the prospects are more than positive, since the number of votes that this formation obtains in those elections tends to double the one it receives in the Catalans.
Thus, the popular Catalans obtained 610,473 votes in the 2008 general elections, while in the region of 2006, the figure amounted to 316,222.
Last Sunday, the PPC won 384,091 votes.
If this upward trend continues, which has been nurtured by a transfer of votes of the PSC and the loyalty of the electorate of the PP, analysts of this formation have calculated that, if general elections were held, the PPC could go from 8 to 12 deputies in the Congress, while CiU would also win four (now it has 10) and the PSC would lose no less than 9 deputies (has 25).
These calculations would verify that the Catalan square is fundamental for PP and PSOE.
According to the analysis of the people, ERC would lose a deputy - he now has three - while ICV would stay with a deputy or win one.
But, if the electoral calendar remains, the most immediate appointment will take place in May 2011 with the municipal elections, where the people can also be decisive in the end of the socialist hegemony in the four provincial capitals and in their relay at the head of the Provincial Council of Barcelona, one of the administrations that manages the most budget - 661 million euros for 2011 - .
Both nationalists and people agree in the possibility that the PSC loses the municipalities of Barcelona, Tarragona, Lleida and Girona, where the socialists would not get the majority or possibility to resort to their traditional partners, ERC and ICV.
Given the Republican debacle, it would not be far-fetched for this formation to lose its representation in the Consistory of Barcelona - it is necessary to obtain 5% of the votes - .
The PP has increased its number of votes in the four capitals and could be a decisive force in Barcelona, Lleida and Tarragona.
The nationalists are convinced of being able to lead the overturning in the Tarraconese capital, where they ruled with the PP before the current socialist mayor, Josep Félix Ballesteros.
In Lerida, the PP has placed only 500 votes from the PSC, whose mayor, Àngel Ros, maintains very good relations with Mariano Rajoy - during this last campaign, they met on several occasions.
The popular have also grown in other important municipalities such as Badalona, Castelldefels or Reus.
Currently, the PP is present in 34 municipal governments in Catalonia and aspires to form lists in the maximum number of populations.
The Catalan cell provided thousands of passports to Al Qaida
In addition to the seven detainees in Barcelona, there were three in Thailand
The seven Pakistanis and the Nigerian arrested by agents of the General Police Information Commissariat in Barcelona formed a jihadist cell that, directed from Thailand, provided passports to different groups of Al Qaeda, including "The Tigers for the Liberation of the Tamil Land" (LTTE) and "Laskar e Taiba" (Let), the latter author of the Mumbai attacks that caused the death of 106 people in 2008.
The Ministry of the Interior has attached great importance to these investigations, to the point of ensuring that "Operation Kampai" has been "weakened" the falsification apparatus of Al Qaeda internationally and, "therefore its operational capacity".
Investigators calculate that the cell, in the two years it has been active in Catalonia, has been able to send thousands of passports to Thailand, where they were falsified and then distributed to the groups that make up the Al Qaida criminal galaxy so that their "combatants" can cross European borders and other Western countries with total impunity.
The released cell is not the only one that the jihadist network had operation in Europe, hence the investigations have participated, in addition to Thai services, other European police units, so it is not ruled out that in EU territory will be carried out in the coming days blows similar to the one developed last Tuesday night in Barcelona.
In coordination with this operation (Pakists Junaid Humayun, Atiqur Rehman, Jabran Asghar, Malik Iftikhar Ahmad, Mohammad Saddique Khan Begum, Tanveer Arshad and Nigerian Babatunde Agunbiade were arrested), in Thailand another was developed with the capture of the Pakistanis Muhammad Athar Butt and Zeeshan Ehsan Butt
These were those who ran from Bangkok the cell settled in Spain and in other European countries.
The Catalan network - part of it was hit in 2009 - mostly robbed tourists passports that met the requirements set by the so-called "World Islamic Front".
That is, the documents had to be in the name of men between 25 and 45 years of age, with a validity of several years and without printed visas.
In the media of the investigation it is stressed that, at the moment, no data has been found linking those arrested in Catalonia with the Mumbai attack, although the perpetrator of this attack, "Laskar e Taiba", has been one of the receiving groups of passports stolen in Spain and then falsified in Thailand.
However, it should be remembered that days before this attack was committed, from Catalonia were sent through a transfer agency about a thousand dollars that were "invested" in the purchase of mobile cards that were later used by the members of the "command" that killed 106 people.
The police have seized abundant documentation.
The PP requires Mas to park the fiscal concert
The leader of the PPC, Alicia Sánchez-Camacho, had the opportunity yesterday to personally explain to Artur Mas his conditions to support his inauguration as president of the Generalitat, identical to those already proposed during the campaign and that are summarized in giving priority to the recovery of the economy and dispensing with philo-sovereignist adventures, as is the economic concert that the nationalist leader wants to pose at the equator of his mandate.
The president of the popular Catalans went to the meeting of Artur Mas with the security of having obtained 18 deputies in the elections last Sunday, a figure more than enough to make himself loved, since the candidate of CiU needs that at least one of the formations that will exercise the opposition abstains in the investiture vote.
From a possible understanding with the PP depends the future alliances that the nationalists want to maintain in the Congress when general elections are held, but, for the moment, the conversations held these days by Mas with the rest of political leaders are made in Catalan key.
And with regard to the president of the PP, to support an investiture "we will have to park all the sovereignist and independence discourses", as well as the economic concert - similar to that of the Basque Country - that the convergent leader even intends to submit to referendum.
Both leaders do have in common their commitment to thinning the political apparatus of a very inflated regional administration and the elimination of the inheritance and donation tax.
From the details of the negotiation on the inauguration of Mas will be occupied by the PPC, Jordi Cornet and Dolors Montserrat.
Mas has already met with Joaquim Nadal (PSC) - who promised a tough opposition - Joan Herrera (ICV) and today will do so with Joan Puigcercós (ERC), Albert Rivera (Ciutadans) and Joan Laporta (Solidaritat Catalana).
Geriatric claims deaths were not due to "lack of measures"
Olot center owners don’t rule out their guard killing more than 11 elders
Those responsible for the geriatric La Caritat de Olot (Gerona) offered yesterday a press conference to highlight the damage caused to the institution by the criminal conduct of Joan Vila Dilmé, the nurse who while there was employed killed at least eleven elders in his care.
The director of the residence, Joan Sala, appeared accompanied by the lawyer of the center, the lawyer Joan Cañada, and announced that "the wish of La Caritat would be able to exercise the accusation against the perpetrator" of the deaths but "the legality and responsibility force us to assume the procedural situation of civil officials."
And it is that in addition to the social smear that the entity may suffer for not having detected in time the criminal activities of the guard, the great threat to La Caritat now takes the form of substantial compensation that the legal representatives of the relatives of the victims are already calculating.
Joan Vila Dilmé has confessed that the first crime dates back to August 2009 and has not stopped killing since.
The judge already pointed out in the car that ordered the exhumation of eight bodies - former geriatric patients - who suspected that in many cases no doctor personally explored the deceased but, according to the age and pathologies of the patients, the procedure was dispatched by telephone.
La Caritat, through its director, strongly denied this extreme yesterday and pointed out that "the lack of measures may not have been at any time the cause of what has happened."
La Caritat’s lawyer added that “in the case there is not a single indication that there has been reckless action on the part of anyone other than the perpetrator himself.”
The already baptized "Angel of Death" confessed yesterday 11 murders and detailed that the first crime was committed on August 29, 2009 for, a month and a half later, to kill a second grandmother.
In this case it caused the death by puncturing an overdose of insulin, unlike the first crime that committed it by helping a mixture of sedative pills.
The director of La Caritat points out that it is difficult to control that the staff of the center has access to medicines.
Insulin punctures and sedative cocktails would have been the mechanism used in six of the nine crimes that the nurse, in his eagerness to alleviate the suffering of the inmates, used to kill them.
But it will be the analyses that are being done in the lab that must clarify if Vila does not lie and sought for her first victims a less cruel death or if, on the contrary, she burned them inside giving them bleach as she has already confessed that she did with the three women she killed between October 12 and 17.
In La Caritat they do not give credibility to the manifestations of the guard, not even to the number of confessed murders, which do not rule out that he is superior, and justify his attitude in the different versions that Vila has given in his visits to the court and in the little sense that he has to change his way of proceeding after having successfully committed eight murders.
That is why they say that they make themselves available to justice to clarify the scope of this event.
The ECB threatens
Investors reacted sharply yesterday to Trichet’s threat yesterday warning “markets should not underestimate our ability to react.”
Peripheral spreads have closed sharply and shares of European banks have risen more than 5%.
However, we must also not underestimate the ECB’s ability to make mistakes and the euro has barely rebounded which is a worrying sign.
What has occurred yesterday is typical of an iliquid market that as it collapses recovers but motivated by the closing of short and very speculative positions.
The institutional investor will be slow to lose fear.
A month ago the ECB summoned us today to withdraw liquidity measures and it will have to be retracted tomorrow for the fourth time in two years and those that remain.
The Bundesbank demanded the completion of the ineffective public debt purchase plan and eliminating all the extraordinary measures of term liquidity and leaving only unlimited access per week.
Following the Irish crisis, we expect them to extend current liquidity measures "at least until March" and maintain the quarterly auction with unlimited access at the end of the quarter to ensure liquidity at least until June.
We also hope that they will continue with their bond-buying program and even signal to intensify it.
If they do not meet the expectations generated they will reverse the excellent reaction of the markets yesterday and if they remain obliged to withdraw liquidity measures, may God assist us!
As in their meeting in May in which they affirmed that they had not even contemplated the purchase of bonds and the markets collapsed, history could be repeated tomorrow.
The ECB must be credible and persistent.
In May he announced the bazooka on Monday and on Wednesday he said that he had already bought a lot, causing a new market crash.
Yesterday the auction of letters to a year in Portugal has been very poor.
The Treasury only issued 500 million to 5.28%, versus 750 million to 4.81% of the previous auction.
Even so rates in Portugal fell 30 basis points throughout the curve, clearly reflecting a change in market sentiment following Trichet’s words.
In Ireland, figures from Allied and Bank of Ireland’s hole have been made public.
Estimated figures are about 10,000 million short-term and another 15,000 potential and have until February to raise private capital or they will be nationalized.
Although Ireland does not have any credibility after not detecting the problem in July with the stress tests, the figures are acceptable within the Aid Plan and help reduce uncertainty.
However, next Tuesday, the government will have to obtain parliamentary approval of its new budget and does not yet have sufficient support, so Ireland remains a focus of uncertainty.
Angela Merkel has moderated the tone of her speech to relax the tension for the next Summit of Heads of State and Government on 14 December in which a political commitment is necessary to demonstrate that we are a union and that we all touch the same score.
The problem is that Germany wants to touch Wagner and the rest Beethoven’s hymn of joy.
In Spain, the president had the gift of the opportunity by presenting yesterday the plan of measures that the Council of Ministers will approve on Friday.
Pension reform, transparency in autonomous communities and financial institutions, deficit reduction, liberalization, privatization, measures to reactivate employment sound in the ears of investors such as the four resorts of Vivaldi.
The double effect Trichet and ZP explains that the Spanish Stock Exchange has risen 4.5%, twice as much as the rest of the European Stock Exchanges.
Unlike February, the reaction of the Government and the Bank of Spain has been swift and forceful and we are benefiting from the surprise effect.
Today there is a key auction of a three-year bond that will benefit from the Trichet effect so we hope that it will be resolved with strong demand and help dispel doubts about our refinancing ability.
For Spain asking for an Aid Plan would be self-immolation.
They would give us a line of liquidity that we don’t need, the Treasury have more than 40,000 million in available liquidity as high as in March, at expensive rates and it closes access to the markets without day.
However, the strategy must remain that of Caesar's wife, "in addition to being, it seems so."
Cheer up, we can.
Eulen’s president will contest Vega Sicilia’s board requested by her children
The storm does not amaina in the open conflict in the Álvarez family, owners of Eulen and El Enebro.
David Álvarez, founder of the group, will contest, considering it "null and void", the judicial board of El Enebro of next day 16, requested by five of his seven children and in which they will try to assert their majority and take over the management bodies of the titular firm of Bodegas Vega Sicilia.
David Álvarez and his children Jesús David and María José are tuning the legal battery for the new judicial chapter in their litigation over the control of part of the group.
The founder of the largest corporate services holding company seems unwilling to give in to his litigation to be present in the governing bodies of El Enebro.
From its board of directors it was downloaded by five of its seven sons who were appointed administrators at the shareholders’ meeting of January 18 of this year and whose challenge is annotated in the Commercial Register.
The judicial board of El Enebro for the next day 16, convened by the magistrate of Mercantile No. 2 of Madrid, has again opened the thunderbox within the Álvarez family.
The appeal to the judge to fix the assembly is, until the conflict is resolved, the only legal mechanism that guarantees the convening of the board.
Juan Carlos, Pablo, Emilio, Marta and Elvira Álvarez Mezquiriz’s latest movement is described by David Álvarez as a “desperate recourse of the applicants his five children to, through procedural argument, in clear law fraud and abuse of law, try to validate and heal the radically null meeting of January 18,” according to his official spokesmen.
This argument will be the basis that, once the board has been held, to proceed to its challenge on the grounds that it is "null and void".
The same protagonists, who maintain a discreet silence, already tried unsuccessfully for the magistrate to convene a meeting in June.
In El Enebro, a heritage company and head of Bodegas Vega Sicilia, among other assets, the seven sons of David Álvarez control 100% of the capital.
The founder of the service group claims, however, usufruct over 51% of capital, according to family protocols.
This matter is the substantive issue that the judge must decide once the hearing is held on the impeachment application, for which there is still no stated date.
Red Bull triumphs in F1 with a secret weapon from Siemens
Software designs real-time car upgrades.
Since Red Bull broke into the world of Formula 1 in 2004, its history in this select universe of speed has been marked by surprise and success.
At first no one took into consideration the options of a newly arrived team and, despite the upward trajectory of the last seasons, last November, in the final stretch of the championship everyone bet on the triumph of the Fernando Alonso Ferrari.
Without detracting from the skill as driver of the finally champion, the German Sebastian Vettel, nor of the capacity of the Renault engine that propells his car, in the world of Formula 1 has become a legend the "secret weapon" that the multinational Siemens has put in the hands of Red Bull to turn it into a winning team.
It is in fact a powerful process simulation software that the German put on the market in 2007 for purposes far removed from the high automotive competition.
Called PLM, this powerful computing instrument has been used to design assembly lines from manufacturing factories around the world, power plants, high-precision instruments.
The objective is to reproduce, faithfully simulate the product or the process before building it in a real way, thus avoiding failures and avoidable expenses.
PLM has facilitated the development of Canon’s cameras, Sukhoi aircraft or Nissan’s most famous utility cars.
Siegfried Russwurm, head of the industrial division of Siemens, explains that the use of this computer instrument in its Formula 1 factory has allowed Red Bull to design and carry out "in a few hours, almost in real time", the modifications, changes and improvements that are detected during races and tests.
Russwurm explains that teams in 1992 had an average of 10 technicians in their team who were able to introduce a maximum of three or four vehicle upgrades during each season.
In the Red Bull team, 180 high-skilled young professionals work today who, equipped with powerful computer equipment, analyze and project all sorts of modifications in the bolides.
The Siemens executive explained that the PLM software is able to analyze and improve all the elements of Red Bull bolides with the exception of the engine, which is Renault’s full responsibility.
Materials, fuel, aerodynamics and safety elements are modified.
Changes can be taken from virtual simulator reality to reality in less than 24 hours, thanks to advanced laser production systems and high-tech autoclaves.
As the only limitation of this madness of technological improvement is the figure of 40 million dollars that each Formula 1 team can spend maximum in each season to facilitate the competition.
Red Bull has assembled the high-tech factory to design and produce its cars in a town about 50 kilometres from London.
These are two buildings in which visits are restricted, especially for journalists.
Everything that happens there is considered "high secret".
The squad for this task amounts to 580 people, including its two pilots, champion Vettel and Mark Webber.
Red Bull’s Formula 1 hero, without a doubt, is recent champion Sebastian Vetell.
Among the immense technological resources that the brand of energy drinks has put at the service of its participation in the circuits, hide other protagonists whose competition is priceless and its null notoriety.
The factory’s head of technology, Steve Nevey, claims that five years ago it could be said that R&D advances in wagon cars contributed 50% of the victories and drivers were responsible for the rest.
Today these percentages have varied and the technology adds 85% of the success, while the pilots contribute 15% of the added value.
Nevey acknowledges that the Vettel or Webber will never be replaced by computing since "they are unpredictable gladiators."
HP will install 20 own stores with local distributors in two years
Hewlett-Packard (HP) inaugurated yesterday in Barcelona its first exclusive store in collaboration are its partner Pont Reyes.
This will be the first of the network of 20 shops of between 80 and 100 square meters that the multinational plans to install in Spain during the next two years.
The company will reconcile this initiative with the 16 areas already present in multi-brand centers such as PC City, Carrefour, Saturn and El Corte Inglés.
The vice president of HP Spain, Helena Herrero, gave as an example the remodeled store located in the Gran Vía of Barcelona, owned by Pont Reyes, with whom she has collaborated for 30 years.
The North American group has prepared a plan to locate 200 brand outlets in Europe in 2013, a strategy that includes the 20 Spanish establishments.
The first to open were Bucharest (Romania) and Belgrade (Serbia) last week, with Barcelona being the third.
Herrero did not want to unveil the following possible openings, although he acknowledged that Madrid could be the next destination.
The also director of HP personal systems assured that the philosophy to install the stores will always be the same, relying on a local distributor with experience and knowledge of the sector.
The American group advises and is in charge of the exclusive furniture design of the stores (with products that can be played interactively), which in Europe will have an identical image.
In addition, HP covers the training of establishment staff.
Helena Herrero denied that it was a franchise system and stressed that the exclusive network of shops aims to "improve customer service and SMEs in the purchase, attention, after-sales and even packaging."
The Barcelona store includes a space with a pioneering pilot program worldwide that is responsible for solving the possible problems of HP computer equipment under warranty.
An online service connects with a technical center located in Maastricht (Netherlands).
"Thus, in 10 minutes we can solve problems that can sometimes take up to two days."
"This system is specially designed for SMEs and users, since large companies already have contracted technical assistance services," Helena Herrero said yesterday.
Sabadell bets on 'stealing' a million customers from rivals in three years
More optimistic than other executives, Banco Sabadell’s CEO, Jaime Guardiola, believes that 2011 will be a tough year for banking, but initially dismisses a new liquidity crisis and predicts the end of the liability war.
In spite of this, it foresees a collapse of interest margins, which will try to compensate in part with increases in commissions and the price of credit.
Sabadell expects to capture in three years a million private clients, their pending subject.
Sabadell has found a way to exploit the next three years and thus survive in a very complicated economic environment.
The bank will launch in 2011 a new master plan that will be valid for three years.
The objective, in addition to surviving without resorting to FROB’s public aid, is to gain market share by attracting one million new private customers -700,000 if those who can leave the bank are discounted-, their great pending subject, since until now it had pampered SMEs and companies more than the retail customer.
This year it has attracted 250,000 private customers and 50,000 companies.
Its market share in individuals is 3.5%, while in companies it exceeds 10%.
Another of the bases of the 2011-2013 plan is the commitment to productivity, or what is the same, of austerity, with reduction of costs as an objective, and whose challenges will be exposed by the bank when it presents the results of the year.
Few banks dare in these times to set some objectives and make them public since the financial crisis has disrupted all the plans of world banking.
Sabadell, in fact, will close this year its master plan Optima, set four years ago, althus initially it was for three, but the situation of the markets made it expand one more year.
Customers who let the savings banks escape will be their main goal.
In a breakfast organized by the APIE, Guardiola insisted that 2011 is going to be a "difficult and momentous" year.
The executive, in fact, believes that the exit from the crisis in Spain "will be slow".
In addition, in two or three years it will be "very difficult to create jobs", something fundamental for the survival of the banking business, he insisted.
And, although he believes that the war of the liability – which penalizes margins – is shrinking, the price of risk premiums that banks and banks are paying for their issuances to obtain liquidity are already shifting to the price of credit to compensate for falling margins.
“The issue of liquidity and financing is structural, and it’s going to be a factor of pressure on credit.”
"We haven't closed the faucet, but the differentials are higher," Guardiola said.
Banesto on Thursday gave orders to his network to limit long-term long-term credit and raise the price of spreads - see CincoDías on November 22 and 27.
He also acknowledged that the banking trend now "is to raise commissions", but "there is a competitive framework".
Sabadell’s management plan is based on organic growth, although, as always, the group does not rule out corporate operations.
Not in vain, he assures that in Spain "larger sizes will be required."
"Next year it's going to move everything," he said.
He claims that capital needs are "highly raised" and does not rule out that any Spanish financial entity enters in red numbers.
And he points out that the FROB can supply the market in raising funds against new recapitalizations.
After assuring that Sabadell can survive the temporary closure of the markets, he downplayed what some bankers already predict as a new liquidity crisis.
He points out that it cannot be issued, but contrary to what happened in June, short-term financing can be obtained through repos, which cushions the crisis.
The ECSC rules out a second wave of mergers in the boxes
The director general of the ECSC, José Antonio Olavarrieta, ruled out yesterday that there will be a wave of mergers of savings banks and again insisted on the speed with which the restructuring of the sector is being executed.
When the process of concentrating the Spanish savings banks began, there were several entities that ensured that they would not participate in this first phase and that they would be reserved for the second round.
However, the director general of the Spanish Confederation of Savings Banks (CECA), José Antonio Olavarrieta, ruled out yesterday that this new wave of operations will occur.
"I think that the largest part of the mergers has been done, the incorporation of Cajasol into the Civic Banking group cannot be said to be a second round, what there are are adjustments."
"Most of it is completely done; it may that instead of 17 we go to 13 or 14 but all the great processes have already been done and supervised by the Bank of Spain, which in that has collaborated a lot as well," said the director.
In statements during a breakfast of the Association of Managers and Executives of Aragón (ADEA), Olavarrieta has stated that the process of concentration is being undertaken "at the highest possible speed, and we have to be very satisfied of how it is occurring in the deadlines that in its day, not now, were marked in the business plans with the Bank of Spain," he stressed.
"Going from 45 boxes with an average of 28,500 million asset volumes to 17 with 75.500 million is an unparalleled process in history, reducing the number of entities by 62% to be much larger to comply with Basel," he said.
Olavarrieta acknowledged that "there are a series of rather unlucky opinions about the slowness of the process" that demonstrate "a lack of seriousness and sense", because "what the Bank of Spain does is remember that we have to be within the deadline before the end of the year".
The director general of the ECSC, José Antonio Olavarrieta, said yesterday that Spain has the most rigorous financial system in all of Europe and "probably" in the world, and attributed a "lack of knowledge" to those who call it into question a "lack of knowledge".
The director pointed out that, within the current economic situation, "we can be very calm" with the situation of financial institutions and savings banks, a system that has embarked on a process of concentration "very important" and that "goes at the fastest possible".
He also recalled that the Spanish bank came out very well from the stress tests carried out by the ECB in July.
Shoemaker and the German Red Line
Berlin has opted to stay in the euro, but has set conditions for the entire Union.
Tensions in the markets have provoked a dual government response.
On the one hand, José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero has advanced new adjustment measures, from tax reductions to SMEs, regulation of private agencies of placement or suppression of the 426 euros of aid to unemployed without provision, to the privatization of 49% of AENA, the management of the airports of Barajas and El Prat and the 30% of Lotteries of the State.
On the other, Vice-President Elena Salgado, with the support of former president Felipe González, has joined the criticism of Chancellor Angela Merkel for her responsibility in unleashing the current crisis.
How do we interpret those movements?
Go ahead that Merkel’s position is not a mistake in her communicative policy.
On the contrary, it is another turn of the screw in the difficult redefinition process of the euro.
And there Germany has a radically new vision of what its future should be, which clashes with that of maintaining the ancient traditions of much of its partners.
Faced with the European resistances, in Germany it begins to speculate about the three possible outlets to the crisis.
The first, a strong euro, composed of itself, Austria and the Netherlands, among other countries, while the south would leave the monetary zone.
The second, the recovery of the framework, although it now seems science fiction, with a very favorable current of opinion, close to 50% of the population.
The third, a redesign of the euro, in line with the German proposals of greater macroeconomic control, budgetary rigour, fines to the transgressive countries, possible suspensions of payments of some of them and extension to the private sector of the costs of potential adjustments.
The German position is clear and has been defined for a long time, so now there are no excuses.
First, in 2009, when both the IMF and the European Commission stimulated the deficits, the German government took away a constitutional reform to prevent them from 2016.
This was the first sign of divergence between what Germany believed should be done (plans of fiscal rigour and stability in public finances), and what their European partners, including France, were practicing.
The second on the forehead was given by the Central European country when Merkel painfully lengthened Greece’s rescue decision.
It was already the object of fierce criticism, but the truth is that the political consensus in Germany and public opinion and the published one were very much against the rescue of Greece.
Finally, in the last days of October, again Merkel emerges with a clear position, demanding the reform of the Union treaties, to make possible the rescue of a country and, at the same time, forcing private lenders to bear part of the cost.
This position has been interpreted as anti-European, as it has precipitated the Irish crisis and everything else.
But his proposal, albeit somewhat sweetened, has been approved and, in the coming months, we should attend the process of reform of the Union to enable those measures from 2013.
Germany has drawn a red line that it does not plan to cross.
And he is telling us all that, although now we have to take measures with which he perhaps does not quite agree, from that date the old operation of the euro, it will be over.
2013 will mean the birth of a new monetary area, in which offending countries will be severely sanctioned, they may be forced to testify in default and, in that case, the private sector will also bear part of the cost.
Germany is pointing out that we must follow a severe path of growth with high rates of savings and investment, moderation in consumption, improvements in productivity and competitiveness and positive external balances.
Another alternative based on debt and construction, with huge holes in the balance of payments, such as the one that has presided over Spanish growth in the boom years, simply will not tolerate it.
Envite is clear.
And we'd do it wrong not to understand it.
It’s not just about redirecting the public deficit to 3% in 2013.
What we're required to do is much tougher.
And the worst thing in these circumstances we could do is deceive ourselves.
I hope that the current turbulence will give way to greater tranquility.
And that we take advantage of the window of opportunity they have given us, until the spring of 2013.
It seems that President Zapatero has also understood this and his double manifesto (the reforms promised after last Saturday’s meeting with large companies and yesterday’s announcement of new measures) suggests that, despite the criticism of Merkel, the thesis has been paved that order must be put at home.
But the red line that Germany has drawn there is, and it doesn’t look like they’re going to move
And, among the possibilities that are contemplated, that of leaving a monetary zone too crowded with unserious countries, it cannot be excluded.
Then, Germany can always tell us that it had already warned us.
Sport and law fraud
Tretas like those of Alonso and Ramos against Ajax demonstrate ingenuity but not willingness to escape a sanction.
The expulsion allegedly devised by two Real Madrid players (Xabi Alonso and Sergio Ramos) in the match played on November 23 against Ajax in the Champions League has caused rivers of ink to be written about whether or not such behavior is unsporting and if, eventually, both players should be sanctioned by UEFA.
As is well known, with a few minutes to finish the match (which his team had already clearly won), the two players deliberately lost time when it comes to taking two fouls to cause the referee to show them the second yellow card.
In that way, and since Real Madrid will end up as leader of their group, both players will comply with the regulatory sanction the next match of the league, against Auxerre, and will be able to pass card clean to the next phase.
Finally, UEFA’s disciplinary committee has sanctioned the team’s coach, José Mourinho, with two matches, and with different economic amounts to the players involved.
The provocation of a yellow card in circumstances like this or similar is not a new practice, but rather frequent in the world of football.
It is so widespread that it is part of the ethos, of the unwritten rules, but followed, of this sport.
Part of the debate emerged in the reported case is due to the fact that it has been starred by a team of the Real Madrid entity (which in its anthem presumes of nobility and honor, and that it has sometimes fined its players for similar acts) and orchestrated dramatization, apparently, by Mourinho, who adds one more episode to his long history of histrionic and provocative technician.
In any case, the debate revolves around whether this type of behavior not expressly prohibited by the regulation contradicts the principles of sport, i.e. if these actions commit law fraud; because, in effect, they are actions respectful of sports regulations, but they seem to circumvent their meaning.
The question is to examine the type of damage they cause to the sporting spirit.
One of the goods understood by the sporting spirit is the physical integrity of the opposing players, an aspect that with these actions was not jeopardized, i.e. there was no violent game.
Another of these assets that are alluded to with fair play is to prevent the team of the offending players from being unfairly taken advantage of by the opposing team, which did not happen either.
However, the paradoxiquity of the action of the Real Madrid players is that he did not accommodate what we normally characterize as a trap or fraud of law, since his violation of the rules was not done in secret to try to avoid the sanction, but that precisely his action tried to be the most obvious to receive the punishment of the referee.
They waited and gladly accepted the sanction.
Another question is what kind of advantage the offenders gain in the intentional offenses examined.
Here it seems necessary to verify that the opponent is not disadvantaged in the same situation, because obviously the result of the action means leaving the team itself with fewer players.
And it is also not a useful strategy for the next game, because the club will not be able to count on those players.
The advantage, obviously, is that the players will start the next stage of the Champions League without cards that could threaten their participation in a future match.
But it is an advantage in the long term, with a certain degree of indeterminacy, because the team can be eliminated to the first changes, and in addition with this action the players fulfill a penalty game and go to the second cycle of cards, in which the suspension for accumulation of cards occurs with one less than in the first cycle.
So, from passing rounds, it’s not so clear that it’s an advantage.
In short, these actions do not go against the sporting spirit, of which it is part to make strategic decisions that favor making the most of what the rules allow.
In the supposement, the players did not act to evade the application of the sanction (this if it would be a clear case of law fraud), but rather chose when to submit to it.
They made a strategic decision without circumventing the punishment or running away from it.
Although the border between ingenuity and law fraud is fuzzy, on this occasion I think it was a clear case of ingenuity.
Controversy, it seems to me, arises by two factors: because it was two self-expulsions and because of the way in which the transmission of the guideline was carried out to provoke them.
The damage caused to the principles of sport by these actions is not so much ethical and aesthetic.
Offensive of offers to capture Christmas group dinners in BCN
The dictatorship of the calendar concentrates this year an avalanche of reserves for days 16 and 17.
Self-suffraged celebrations by employees proliferate, who want to spend between 20 and 30 euros.
As the nougat and the lottery, with the arrival of December also return the group dinners prior to Christmas, which continue to serve as an oxygen balloon for the restoration and leisure of Barcelona, but which because of the crisis have led to an offensive of adjusted prices to be able to attract the majority customer: almost all want to spend between 20 and 30 euros in dinners or meals, the entrepreneurs and associations of the sector interviewed coincide.
To finish complicating the accounting, this year’s calendar (when New Year’s Eve falls on Friday) has concentrated the petitions in the week of December 13, with the result to date of an avalanche of dinner reservations for peak days (16 and 17) and barely movement for the previous week.
As has already happened since 2008, when the stage of thin cows began, there are many companies that have eliminated collective dinners by budgetary issue.
So as not to dispense with the tradition, many workers keep it paying out of pocket.
Whatever the conjuncture, both demand battle prices, confirms the president of the Gremi de Restauració de Barcelona, Gaietà Farràs.
The representative of the employers, today, does not dare to predict what the celebrations of this month will look like.
“People think about it long before they book.”
"He wants to get out but he has the alerts on the issue of prices," he says.
The business will go well "to those who give quality at a good price and with good service", he adds, and remembers that customers tend to lower a step with the crisis, so that the strip around 25 euros has become a majority in demand.
In a survey in various central establishments the collapse of petitions for the night of 17 is observed.
In the Soteras group they indicate that there is a real boom of applications for that Friday, with the intention of spending less than 30 euros, to which they have responded by adapting their menus to the conjuncture, with complete formulas of 25 to 35 euros.
In another of the most powerful groups, Andilana, with 16 establishments in the city (Hostal de la Rita, Market, La Fonda and others), the peculiarity of the calendar has led to more early booking, even since the beginning of November.
"We've done the impossible to keep the same prices last year, with menus for everyone from 19.95 to 30 euros," they detail.
In AN Grup, which champions El Mussol, Attic, QuQu, and up to a total of 23 venues, they remain optimistic after detecting a billing boom from the second semester and a good, albeit concentrated, business prospects for Christmas.
Sources of the group highlight that 18 will also be a hyperactive day at the level of groups of friends that precelebrate the holidays.
To its six group menus they have added a Christmas one, with 30 euros or more as a star budget and the perception that there is a predisposition to spend 10% more than last year in exchange for demanding more details, such as cava.
They also see green shoots in some companies that re-invite their employees and a feeling that the volume of groups is somewhat larger.
On the part of Fecasarm (which includes entities such as the Association of Bars, Restaurants and Leisure of Barcelona), its vice president, Enric Gomà, hopes to start seeing the end of the tunnel and maintains that together the reservations are "animated", although customers "negotiate to the maximum".
Of course, the illusion for the celebrations has not descended, whoever pays, he says.
The Pakistani nuclear program generates great concern in the United States
Islamabad produces atomic weapons at "a faster rate than any other country."
Wikileaks leaks confirm ties between the Taliban and Pakistan.
The contents of the correspondence between the U.S. embassy in Pakistan and the U.S. Department of State leaked by the Wikileaks portal confirm the enormous concerns caused in Washington by the Pakistani nuclear program.
Apart from the enlightening and juicy details they offer about the content of the conversations and the thoughts of American diplomatic representatives accredited in Islamabad, the documents reveal that cooperation between both countries on atomic security, rather than increasing, "declines", and that the unstable Asian country produces nuclear weapons "at a faster rate than any other country in the world".
The particularity of the Pakistani nuclear program - which the weapons are not assembled but remain stored by components to hinder their destruction in the event of an attack - constitutes one of Washington's main revealing reasons.
Anne W. Patterson said: “The proximity of some nuclear facilities to territory under Taliban attack, the rumored dispersal of nuclear materials, and the vulnerability of the transport of atomic materials.
The high number of people involved in nuclear arsenals and missile programs is contemplated, not only by the United States, but also by Russia, as a proliferator and reason to maintain the guard.
“Pakistan has had to hire personnel with strict religious beliefs to protect nuclear installations; given the drop in educational and cultural levels, extremist organizations have more opportunities to recruit people working on nuclear or missile programs,” expert Yuri Korolev of the Russian Foreign Ministry said in a meeting with their U.S. counterparts.
The latest of the documentation leaked by Julian Assange’s website is the diminishing U.S.-Pakistan cooperation on nuclear proliferation issues and Pakistan’s high pace of nuclear weapons production.
An embassy office in May, which calls communication with Pakistan "frustrating", argues: "Transparency is often non-existing; offers of help are left unanswered or discarded."
In another message, Peter Lavoy, National Intelligence official for South Asia, notes that "despite the economic catastrophe, Pakistan produces nuclear weapons at a faster rate than any other country in the world."
Ambassador Patterson also asks Washington, during a visit by the head of the army, General Ashfaq Kayani, to make it "very clear" to the perpetrator that "the Pakistan army must end tacit support for terrorist groups."
On the hunt for the head of Wikileaks
Obama appoints senior counter-terrorism official to prevent further leaks.
Interpol issues a red alert in order to follow the movements and locate Assange wherever he is.
Everything related to Wikileaks and its founder, Julian Assange, forms a complicated labyrinth where it is difficult to have certainty about the facts.
Yesterday Interpol issued a "red alert" to "locate and follow Assange's moves."
However, this is not a traditional arrest warrant.
In addition, it is not new, but dates from November 20.
And it has nothing to do with the latest leak of more than a quarter of a million U.S. diplomatic documents but with the accusations of sexual abuse in Sweden.
In addition, Wikileaks creator Mark Stephens' lawyer said: "The police know where and how to get in touch with Mr. Assange."
It was day 20 when the international policing force based in Lyón (France) issued the order at the request of Sweden, but did not reveal it until yesterday, after receiving authorization from the authorities of that country.
Interpol’s 188 countries have indications of identifying or locating Assange “in view of his provisional arrest and extradition.”
Although Interpol cannot order the arrest of an individual affected by a red alert, many of the member countries, according to the organization, do consider the so-called free-way notice network to proceed with an arrest, which must be undertaken by the national policing force, especially if they have a bilateral extradition agreement, as is the case of Sweden and England, where Assange is believed to be.
The movement has outraged Assange’s lawyers, who yesterday published a letter denouncing that the founder of Wikileaks, 39, has been willing to respond by videoconference to questions from the Swedish authorities.
They also remember that Assange requested and obtained permission to leave Sweden from the prosecutor of his case, which they compare with the mythical Lavrentiy Pavlovich Beria, Stalin’s head of security.
"This seems like a hunt, not legal persecution," the letter said.
As the world searches for him - and from Australia his mother defends the innocence of his son -- Assange is still unknown, but not silent.
On Monday he gave an indeterminated interview with the director of Time magazine through Skype, a conversation in which he called for the resignation of U.S. Secretary of State Hillary Clinton.
"If it can be shown that he was responsible for ordering U.S. diplomats to spy at the U.N
His opinion was denounced yesterday by White House press secretary Robert Gibbs, who called the request for resignation "ridiculous and absurd".
"I'm not entirely sure why we should care about a guy's opinion with a website," he told CNN.
Obama himself has commissioned a senior anti-terrorism official, Rusell Travers, to prevent new leaks of documents.
President Barack Obama’s spokesman’s attempt to downplay Assange contrasts with views proclaimed by conservative politicians and commentators these days.
Peter King, the congressman who from January will chair the Homeland Security Committee in the Lower House, has asked the State Department to consider including Wikileaks on the list of terrorist organizations “to intervene and their funds and pursue anyone who gives them any contribution or help.”
Tom Flanagan, an adviser to Canadian President Stephen Harper, has had to rechance after saying Assange should “be killed.”
"Obama should turn it into a target and use a drone or something."
Mike Huckabee, Baptist minister, former governor of Arkansas and a possible presidential candidate.
"Which in the government who leaked the information is guilty of treason and anything other than execution would be too kind a punishment," he said.
Puigcercós seeks peace in ERC with nods to those related to Carod
The Republican leader will allow the party a secret vote on its continuity.
Huguet proposes a more plural executive and the end of the assembly system.
Joan Puigcercós seems to have found the recipe to circumvent the umpteenth internal revolt in the ranks of ERC, without north for the loss of 11 deputies and half of the votes.
The president of the party is aware that his wizard of resignation is insufficient for those related to former leader Josep Lluís Carod-Rovira, but he also knows that the influence of that sector is today as diluted as his own mentor and that at all it can cause the headaches he had with other extinct currents.
Therefore, the leader of Esquerra will try to win over new critics with winks that allow him to shield himself until the municipal elections.
To begin with, Puigcercós announced yesterday that the more than 200 ERC national advisers will be able to pronounce on their continuity in secret.
Through ballots, and not by freehand.
If the carodists want to vehiculate a certain vote of punishment to the president, they can do so without fear of reprisals.
In fact, the head of ranks wants to avoid the image of division as much as that of subjugation to his person.
In several interviews, he affirmed that it would not be considered ratified with half plus one of the votes, but added, without specifying percentages, that neither would be good a result "to the Bulgarian".
Those close to Carod demand the president something more than putting his position at the disposal of the militancy, a gesture that does not apply when Puigcercós controls the highest body between congresses.
But not having a sufficient majority to force resignations, they are going to try to agree with the executive a roadmap from here to May.
To this end, they hope to participate in the preparation of the strategic reorientation document that the management will put to the vote of the national council on 18.
His intention is to endorse Puigcercós in exchange for this promise to advance the congress to the autumn of 2011, something that the leadership already takes for granted.
The ERC leader acknowledged that "polyphony" harms stability and that his goal is to make, once and for all, the organization able to wash the dirty rags from doors in.
In this line, it is symptomatic the article that the acting councilor Josep Huguet signed on his blog.
Considered the ideologue of Puigcercós, the head of Innovació assumed that the leader will be endorsed "in exchange for various objectives".
And he listed five, of which two stand out.
The first, "recompose" the executive to represent at least 90% of the militancy, that is, include new faces that come from Carod.
The second can be the great novelty.
Huguet proposes that ERC once liquidate assembly as an operating mechanism.
Thus, it proposes a reform of the statutes that allows to hold the congresses through delegates elected by suffrage.
Ending the assembly model, an exception in the Spanish party system, has been an obsession of the Puigcercós team, which already struggled in this regard in the last two congresses.
In fact, the convulsive conclave of 2008 left the commission to the management to review assembly in a specific charter reform congress.
This appointment is still pending, because the internal tide of recent years discouraged facing the issue.
Now, with no sectors critical of lurking, it can be a good time to pick it up and try to shut it down forever.
23 executed in November; 290 during 2010
According to MILENIO’s count State of Mexico there was a decrease of four cases compared to 2010.
During November, 23 people were executed in the State of Mexico, four fewer than the previous month according to the MILLENNIUM State of Mexico content.
Among the murders are that of three women and the discovery of a pair of severed bodies in the Valley of Mexico.
In the penultimate month of the year there are 390 deaths allegedly related to organized crime.
Among the cases is that of a body found in San Diego de los Padres Cuexcontitlán, municipality of Toluca.
The subject had at least three gunshots and the shot of grace in the head in addition to the feet and hands tied wired.
The next day, the body of a subject was found in a suitcase inside the trunk of an abandoned vehicle on Mixtecas Street, in the Colonia Santa Cruz Acatlán, in the municipality of Naucalpan.
In the early hours of Thursday, ejidatarios found a body with signs of torture and at least three lethal shots in the thorax, on the Tenango-Ixtapan de la Sal quota highway, in the section that crosses the municipality of Villa Guerrero.
Next to the body was found a message allegedly signed by the criminal organization La Familia Michoacana.
A day later in Nezahualcóyotl, a 50-year-old gentleman was killed with eight bullets after leaving his home to begin garbage collection.
On November seven, two 30-year-old subjects were killed of a bullet impact on the head each in the San Pedro Xaloxtoc colony, municipality of Ecatepec.
In Chalco, with the shot of grace on the forehead, the corpse of a man was found on a lone road in Colonia El Naranjo and under the body they found an impercuted 25-caliber firearm casing.
In Ecatepec, parts of a human body were found in the sewage channel of the Golondrina Beach colony.
Urgency bodies conducted an exhaustive search of the other members of the body, however they were not located.
Neighbors of Tepotzotlán warned about the discovery of a corpse that was in the Peña Colorada place, which was tied from the hands backwards and with its head blindfolded.
The man had a gunshot wound on his neck.
In the middle of the month the execution of two men was reported, who were engulfed in blankets and abandoned in the trunk of a vehicle in streets of the Villas de la Hacienda fractionation in the municipality of Atizapán de Zaragoza, along with the victims two messages of death warnings were also discovered.
A day later, a cardboard box containing a human head was found in the Valle de los Reyes colony, in the municipality of Los Reyes La Paz.
In a nearby colony he was found wrapped in a black plastic bag the rest of the body with a message signed by El Incorrigible.
On 17, wrapped in blankets, two women were found dead in the village of Zoquiapan in the municipality of Ixtapaluca and the next morning the body of a man in Huixquilucan who was engulfed in a yellow blanket with blue, allegedly identified with the name of Mario Ríos according to a message that his executors left next to him.
In Ixtapaluca, with gunshots to the neck, the lifeless body of a woman was found in the town of San Francisco Acuautla.
On the woman’s skin, tattoos of Santa Muerte were detected, as well as necklaces with her image.
In the last part of the month, the body of a man in the municipality of Zinacantepec with at least three bullet impacts and a message signed by a criminal group was found aboard a car.
Two days later, the corpses of a pair of men with their headed heads and a message were found in the community of Puente de Piedra in Santiago Tepatlaxco.
Faced with the bodies there was a message allegedly signed by a criminal organization that said: "This happens to those who do not line up."
In Ecatepec, with signs of torture and tied hands, two bodies were found inside an abandoned taxi behind the Justice Center of the San Agustin colony.
On the last day of the month the bodies of two men were found in the municipalities of Los Reyes La Paz, and Nezahualcóyotl.
The first of them was beheaded in the Cuchilla Ancón colony and was wrapped in black plastic bags, tied with cinnamon tape and next to a message; while the second was tortured and found in a plastic bag in streets of Colonia Juárez.
Next to him the agents found a cardboard with a message.
China maintains targets to reduce greenhouse gases
The representative of that country at COP16 assured that his country has made enormous efforts to reduce the carbon footprint and detailed that it maintains its supply to reduce by 2020 between 40 and 45 percent its greenhouse gas emissions.
China maintains its supply to reduce its greenhouse gas emissions by 40 to 45 percent by 2020, said Su Wei, the country’s representative at COP16.
In his first public appearance at the 16 Conference of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (COP16), Su Wei assured that his country has made enormous efforts to reduce the carbon footprint and reconvert its industry.
In fact, he said, in the last four years his country has invested 245 million dollars in efforts to reduce the emission of greenhouse gases.
However, he made it clear that China would not change its objectives, but it would continue to work together with the international community in that area.
He said that the Chinese strategy on climate change is divided into four items: dialogue; capacity-building; institution-building and support for pilot programmes.
In all those areas, progress has been made, including the approval, last year, of a new Energy Law, which includes the objectives raised in terms of reducing gas emissions, he explained.
On the other hand, he stressed that in China, federal authorities have been working in coordination with state and local authorities, to achieve the objectives of energy savings, efficient energy use and emission reduction.
In addition, it abounded, they are underway or have already concluded various multi-annual plans, both financed by the Chinese government and by governments of other countries.
For example, he said, the Efficient Energy Use Program, which was implemented over the past five years and which received investments from the United Nations (UN), government and private initiative.
A provincial climate change project is also underway, another for changing incandescent pockets for savers and one more for mitigation and adaptation, he said.
At the same table, Martin Krol, scientific adviser to that country’s government on climate change, assured that China’s efforts have been greater than what is generally thought.
For example, he explained, the per capita emission of greenhouse gases in China is six tons per person, while in the United States it is 100 tons; China is responsible for 9 percent of the historical contribution in pollution.
In addition, it has made unprecedented efforts in the use of energy, such as increasing energy efficiency by 20 percent in the last five years and expects to make another one by 2020, it abounded.
Attached to legality expulsion of Espino: César Nava
The national leader of the PAN detailed that the former panista leader is entitled to go to the National Order Commission to claim whatever suits him.
The national leader of the PAN, César Nava Vázquez, assured that the expulsion of Manuel Espino Barrientos was attached to legality and that the former panistic leader is in his right to go to the National Order Commission to claim whatever his right agrees.
In an interview prior to Gabino Cué Monteagudo’s takeover as governor of the state, he said that in any case Espino’s expulsion is a decision taken locally.
"This is an autonomous decision of the Sonora Order Commission."
"Mr. Espino has his right to go, where appropriate, by complaint to the Commission of Order of the National Council, in such a way that it is a decision taken within the scope of a competent body," he noted.
On the other hand, he clarified that it is not time to think in 2012 and that an alliance for the presidential candidacy was never at the negotiating table.
Nava Vázquez was convinced that National Action will be strengthened from the internal election on December 4 and 5, in which his successor must be elected.
"That (an alliance in 2012) is an issue that was never at the negotiating table, at least not for now, it was an approach to the local election that was successful and met the objective," he said.
The panista leader specified that the PAN is backed by 71 years of dialogue, so "we are not afraid of internal competition; the contario "we enjoy it and it strengthens us," he said.
He said that undoubtedly the council will know how to elect the best or the best leader who requires National Action for the next three years, hence he will deliver the scam "to whom the council determines."
"I have that absolute peace of mind and openness, to any of the five I would be proud to hand him the office of the president of the party."
He predicted that the rebound that the party registered in the last elections on July 4 will be reflected in the next contests, although "it is not yet time to think in 2012."
"For now it's about accompanying Governor Gabino Cué on this task."
In this context, he stressed that the three governments emanating from alliances in Oaxaca, Puebla and Sinaloa will be the flag and will be an observance throughout the country, hence "many hopes, yearnings are on them, and I am sure Gabino will live up to expectations."
When questioned about the PRI’s criticism of President Felipe Calderón for his speech within the framework of the four years of his government, César Nava called on the PRI to reflect on the six-year terms of former presidents Ernesto Zedillo and Carlos Salinas de Gortari.
"If the PRI thinks that the governments of Zedillo and Salinas were better than those of Fox and Calderón; that they begin to defend them; that they leave the closet," he said.
He said that the head of the Federal Executive can make expressions such as those he made in his speech in the National Auditorium, because in the country there is no gag or much less censorship.
Promotes Obama on Twitter law in favor of undocumented students
"If you agree that passing the Dream Act law is appropriate, call your senators and install them to pass this legislation," the U.S. president tweeted.
President Barack Obama today joined the national mobilization in support of the Dream Act to legalize undocumented students, with a message on his personal Twitter account.
"If you agree that passing the Dream Act law is appropriate, call your senators and install them to pass this legislation," Obama tweeted.
His brief message maintains a cyber link with a broader document that asks the public to immediately communicate with the senators, given that the vote of the legislation is imminent.
"With a vote expected to happen soon, now it's time to make your voice heard," he says.
The message was released after the 42 Republican senators threatened to block the vote of any legislation, unless the extension of Bush-era tax cuts and government funding passed.
Such a blockade, announced in a letter signed by the 42 Republican senators, could affect the vote on the Dream Act and other bills such as the ratification of the new START treaty with Russia against the proliferation of nuclear weapons.
Questioned by the Republican blockade of the Dream Act law and other bills, presidential spokesman Robert Gibbs hoped that once the fiscal issue was resolved, a timetable for the rest of the bills can be set.
“I think there’s plenty of time...”
"We all agree that we have to settle the tax matter before the end of the year..."
"I think we can reach an agreement at some point in that and then we can plan the sequence of the rest," he said.
Senate Majority Leader Harry Reid announced on Tuesday that he would call for a “closing-of-debate vote” for the Dream Act, so the initiative to legalize undocumented students could be voted starting next Thursday in the Senate, though it’s more likely next week.
The senatorial version of the American Dream Act would legalize some 800,000 young people who entered the U.S. before the age of 16, have remained in the country for the past five years and entered an institution of higher education or the military.
But supporters require 60 votes to approve the initiative, so they require the contest of a handful of Republicans, at a time that party organizations of the project star in intense lobbying to guarantee the passage of the law.
The Democrats, most of whom support the Dream Act, have 58 seats.
However, at least one of them, Senator Ben Nelson of Nebraska, has said he will vote against, and only one Republican has voiced his support for the law, Richard Place of Indiana.
Therefore, supporters of the Dream Act need to join several Republicans in case other Democrats decide to turn their backs on the initiative.
Among the most polite Republicans are the senators of Maine, Olympia Snowe and Susan Collins, as well as outgoing Senator George Lemieux of Florida, who completed Senator Mel Martinez.
Other Republicans who supported the Dream Act in the past are Orrin Hatch of Utah and Kay Bailey Hutchinson of Texas but have stated that they will not endorse it another time.
Another Republican who supported, John McCain, hasn’t defined his stance at this time.
Although the House of Representatives has a different version, analysts agree that the Dream Act faces the biggest hurdles in the Senate.
With literature they put their finger on the sore
The second round of The Pleasures of the Language was a walk where the participants displayed the narration
"That about Sex in the language I understand but . ... the finger in the sore? what is expected of me?", asked the writer Luisa Valenzuela invited to open the second round of The pleasures of the language, a cycle that celebrates literature in Spanish and that this occasion used the subtitle of his question (at another fair the round table was dedicated to Sex in the language, he explained).
The finger in the wound ... "puts us in trouble", the Argentine writer sweetly told the public, mostly of young people, who gathered at the Guadalajara International Book Fair (FIL) to listen to this round table moderated by Marisol Shultz and where Valenzuela, Sergio Ramírez, Mayra Montero and Luis García Montero participated.
Luisa Valenzuela read a text that she dedicated to Gonzalo Celorio and that Shultz would later define as a great story, with Luisa in the first person as the protagonist who in search of knowing what is the "sore", goes from doctor to doctor, of which she does not get answers but clues in the same narrative thread.
From the infectologist it is convinced: "patience is not my fort, it may be my sore."
In the end, after a frustrated approach with the traumatologist who has stolen his breath in the middle of the aisle, but who clarifies that it is not a scientific term; it is the psychoanalyst who reveals the mystery: "the sore is the open real, the symbolic is the scar."
The protagonist sighed relieved and concluded "from the sore I only managed to know that it is the real open and that it does not exist", but convinced that the pleasures and traps of the tongue must be very encountered.
Valenzuela, the writer, played with both.
Sergio Ramírez also exposed a delightful narration titled Elogio of the invention, where the finger in the sore is not named but at one point it is discovered.
With strokes of magical realism, it tells of a famous sorceress, who the same touched clouds, brought back men who had gone away or repaired the virtues that had lost a maiden, narration that turned to speak America, the great novel that did not miss from the beginning his own chroniclers who told it and quoted Bernal Díaz del Castillo who criticizes the excesses of what was told by the captains
"Cervantes who invented us all (the writers) made fun of the pretend witnesses who appeared in the stories presented as real, but otherwise it would never have come to tell the story of America with imagination that lacked even modesty."
However, he stressed "it's the crazy and the liars who best imagine" and that's where reality and lies converge, where it is no longer known that the narrated belongs to one side or another, where fictional literature reaches its peak.
It is in fact called "cervantine tradition'.
"By his inheritance we are what is counted."
In her turn, the writer Mayra Montero narrowed down and applauded the variety of interpretations that gave rise to the invitation of the FIL with her round table The finger in the sore.
The Cuban-Puerto Rican aimed hers towards the great wound of social violence that crosses her country - and shares Latin America - as a result of the disputes in the trafficking and sale of drugs.
"I believe that good literature, good poetry, good songs contain a tacit commitment that of course is not premeditated," he said, citing that the reality of literature is the communication and the dimension of the commitment that is established between author and readers, and that in a "chemical reaction" of the latter.
The night was closed by the Spanish poet Luis García Montero whose intervention also emphasized "that finger in the wound of the committed writer of the writer".
He considered that in a world that lives in a hurry, also in the intellectual customs and control of the consciences that results in dogmatisms, forgetting "that behind every affirmation there is a little no and behind every denial there is a small yes."
"To own our thinking and own reality we have to nuance: and nuancing means many times putting your finger on the sore and annoying those people who are much more comfortable thinking of caricature, in black and white, in established ideas and without nuance and also annoying the powers that prefer a world in black and white," he said, inviting to recover the sense of writing.
Romano goes for his first title
Rubén Omar Romano has three defeats in finals, he has had ups and downs in his career, but he has already managed to reach the decisive series on three occasions.
With the duel of Santos Laguna and Monterrey in the final, they also face two different styles marked by their technicians, who find with each other with a contrast marked by the results.
Rubén Omar Romano has three defeats in finals, he has had ups and downs in his career, but he has already managed to reach the decisive series on three occasions, although until now he has gone blank and that is why this occasion is very special for him.
The Argentine has ended up comforting himself with the runner-up medal, he has stayed one step behind with the winners and curiously with the same team has played consecutive finals.
The strategist Santista reached his first final as coach in the Apertura 2002 Tournament, when he had the reins of Morelia with which he fell by global of 4-2 against Toluca; subsequently, in the Clausura 2003 he also reached the decisive duel and also lost it with an overall of 3-1 and was precisely against Rayados.
Already with Santos Laguna, in the Bicentennial Tournament 2010, again against the Diablos, Rubén again suffered a setback, since his team finished tied 2-2 against the choriceros after 210 minutes of play, but in the penaltis he took advantage by 3-1 with two chances to seal the title for finally three players of his missed from the 11 steps and suffered the flip 4-3.
Rubén already lost two finals with the Red Devils and one with Monterrey, his rival in turn, therefore he does not want to run the same luck, seeks to get rid of the curse and be able to celebrate for the first time a title, although he affirms that for him the most important thing is the institution.
"I want to be champion, I don't want to break any statistics, this is because of a club, because of a hobby to which I owe myself and consequently the title will come for me, but the above is the most important thing," said the helmsman when questioned about the numbers he has against playing finals.
It has the previous final, of which, he says, has learned a lot, "the only teaching we can have is that until the referee whistles the end we can not celebrate, we are prepared in the line, the players too, because the previous time, with two penalties for pulling we were already prepared to run and I think that is a teaching, until the end of the game we can celebrate."
To know that this series could be defined in penalty kicks, something that does not disturb him, because he trusts his players: "One never seeks to reach the penalty shootout, one seeks to finish in regular time, but sometimes there is no other and you have to be prepared, in the previous tournament we were prepared, unfortunately we made a bad play the destination, sometimes because of the nerves or the anxiety of having the title in our hands, but we left
Roman has in his hands to stop being a runner-up coach and try for the first time the glory given by the titles.
U.S. Changes Rules to Obtain Visa
Individuals must go to Visa Applicant Service centers prior to their Consular Section interview; the cost will be lower and as well as waiting time.
From January 10, 2011, the procedure of processing a visa at the U.S. Embassy and consulates will change.
With the new procedure, most applicants will need to go to Visa Applicant Service (ASC) centers prior to their Consular Section interview.
ASC personnel will take the applicant’s biometric information, which will be reviewed by the Consular Section prior to the interview.
The new procedure will have certain advantages for applicants, including:
The total cost of processing the visa will be lower, it will no longer be necessary to make a payment to arrange the appointment, another payment to process the visa application and another for the courier service to deliver the document.
The applicant will only make a single payment that will cover the three services.
The current fee will remain unchanged: the equivalent of US$140 for a tourist visa, US$150 for petitioned visas (including temporary worker visas) and US$390 for merchant and investor visas.
People who wish to renew their visa will not have to attend an interview with the Consular Officer, but will only go to the nearest ASC where they will present their documents and take their fingerprints.
Because biometric information will be taken at the ASC, applicants who require a second appointment to be interviewed in the Consular Section will remain in this less time than in the past.
Likewise, those who apply for a visa at the Consulates in Ciudad Juárez, Monterrey and Nuevo Laredo will no longer pay the extra service fee of 26 dollars.
For additional information about the new visa application procedure, please visit the following page http://mexico.usembassy.gov/bulletines/sp101201_Visas-FAQs.html
Nasa will announce discovery of extraterrestrial life
The U.S. space agency quoted the press to present tomorrow a finding that will have an impact on the search for living beings in space.
The U.S. National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA) will unveil an astrobiological discovery tomorrow Thursday that it could prove the theory that extraterrestrial life does exist.
The finding will be presented at a press conference Thursday at 13:00 a.m. at NASA’s headquarters in Washington by experts in astrobiology, a science described as studying the origin, evolution, distribution and future of life in the Universe.
Citing the press, NASA said it will present "a finding that will have an impact on the search for extraterrestrial life," prompting speculation from experts from other countries.
The discovery could demonstrate the theory that there are creatures on par with us and in hostile environments that were thought uninhabitable, and they would put on the table the possibility that they will become intelligent beings like humans if they improve the conditions in which they live.
Apparently it is a microbe that was found at the bottom of Monkey Lake in Yosemite National Park, California, where it lives in an environment that was thought to be too poisonous for any form of life, since the lake is rich in arsenic.
Somehow, the creature uses arsenic as a way to survive and from this ability increases the possibility that similar life could exist on other planets, which do not have our benevolent atmosphere.
According to astrobiologists, if these organisms use arsenic in their metabolism, it will be shown that "there is another way of life, a second way of life, different from what we know."
The space agency will unveil the true extent of the findings at tomorrow’s press conference Thursday.
Geobiologist Pamela Conrad, who studies the possibility of life on Mars; and biologist Steven Benner, one of the scientists studying Titan (Saturn’s greatest moon) will participate in the conference, from the prospect that its chemistry is similar to that of Earth.
Gesta PRD, PT, Convergence and Opponent 'Block' Pattern
With reforms to the Organic Law of Congress, it gives rise to the arrival of 'bipartisanship', they denounce.
The 60 Legislasture did not be installed when an opposition bloc emerged in Congress, headed by the deputies of the PRD, PT, Convergence and Jesús Manuel Patrón Montalvo, who declared himself independent, and the four questioned one thing: the arrival of 'bipartisanship' in the Chamber.
Before starting the installation session of the 60 Legislature, Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez, of Convergencia; Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza, of the PT; Javier Corvera Quevedo, of the PRD; and Patrón Montalvo, 'throtoned' against the reform to the Organic Law, since it excluded them.
With this reform, they reiterated, it begins 'bipartisanship', because now it will be two people, of the same number of parties, who will control the Congress.
These four deputies, together with Gloria Margarita Santos Aguilar, coordinator of the PRD, were the only ones who voted against the Board of Directors, headed by the panist Salvador López Brito and Luis Antonio Cárdenas Fonseca, as vice president.
Corvera Quevedo, explained that with these reforms in an institutional way constitutes the political marginality on the part of the PRI and PAN.
He explained that in the coming days they will speak with the representatives of the PAN and PRI to modify the scheme, starting from a political will, and if they do not have it, then they will be overwhelmed again by both parties and with it the opposition coalition is over.
"So far (the opposition coalition), it's what I'm looking at, because we're excluded, from any decision, they don't even have the political deference to consider the opinion of the rest."
Felipe Manzanarez Rodríguez, from Convergencia, mentioned that with the PRD, PT and Patrón, there are coincidences and that is why they are forming a plural group.
It cannot be allowed, he continued, that the Congress, having been monopolic, is now "duopolic."
"It's not the spirit to reforming the law that there's eternal bipathidism in Congress."
To begin with, this group is initiating with 5 legislators, and they are conversing with other comrades to be more, the convergent abounded.
Ezequiel Reynoso Esparza, of the PT, explained that currently the Congress became a 'loot' between two, in allusion to the PAN and PRI.
"Before it was just a game, now they're handing it out between two."
Patrón Montalvo, asked to respect the personality of the deputy, be given his place at the negotiating tables.
"Not for coupular agreements, it is left out to 5 that we are probably going to make 9 deputies, right now to the five who see us, separated into different fractions, we have decided to unify to find a place at the negotiating table, that is the issue that in the reform that they made that they agreed, we are out and we do not agree with that reform."
$200 thousand left for "the supplier"
Pay Mazatlán City Council another check to the questioned owner of an electrical equipment company.
One more check for just over 200 thousand pesos in favor of Lorena Guadalupe Camacho Palazuelos was issued by the City Council.
With this, the amount that has been paid in this second semester to the owner of the company of electrical equipment, amounts to 1 million 200 thousand pesos.
According to the transparency page of the Municipality, this last check number 9258447 was issued last November 16, for the amount of 200 thousand 144.56 pesos, under the concept of "liquidation of accounts payable".
The Treasurer Luis Miguel López Moreno had anticipated that more money would come out of the Commune in favor of this supplier, which was observed by the Superior Audit of the State for selling at a higher price.
What has not been reported is the total that is still owed and, therefore, how many checks are missing to be issued in favor of Camacho Palazuelos.
“You haven’t bought anything, you’re just paying you what’s owed, just like the rest of the vendors,” he said in an interview.
The last checks "reappeared" in the last quarter of the year: September 24, two on October 15, October 19, October 29 and the last one, on November 16.
Although the provider has not yet been published in the Official Journal of the State, as marked by the Law when a company is questioned by the ASE, in order to prevent other governments from using their services, the Treasurer assured that they have not reused their services.
The above, after on July 25, Northwest published a review of the checks issued by the City Council to its suppliers, in which it was revealed that, from March 2008 to July 9, 2010, in the triennium of Mayor Jorge Abel López Sánchez, Camacho Palazuelos paid the Municipality more than 10 million pesos, through 42 checks.
He also transacted with Jumapam, where he collected checks for more than 3 million pesos.
Urging to serve tourist sites
Lamentas tourist businessmen a foreign tourist accident due to poor conditions of the Glorieta Sánchez Taboada in Mazatlán.
After the fall of a foreign tourist in a collapse of the Glorieta Sánchez Taboada, the tourism sector of Mazatlan asked the authorities to give attention to the areas and sites that are in poor condition and that project a bad image of the port.
Salvador Barraza Rubio, president of the Association of Merchants of the Golden Zone, recalled that on different occasions they have denounced taking care of the image of the destination and reversing how bad tourists find with new activities or routes.
He said that the bad condition of the streets and sidewalks has affected because tourists come for a walk and see the mess and turn around the nearby businesses.
"Politicians don't know what it's like to close a company one day and they have no idea of the money that is lost, or else they didn't do the works in this way so long and without proper planning."
Hotelier José Ramón Manguart Sánchez said that the fall of the tourist exposed the lack of prevention and maintenance by the City Council.
"The municipality will have to assume its responsibility and prevent it from happening again, how to do so by giving preventive maintenance and not falling solely into the corrective," he said.
"We look forward to enjoying a clean city, because not only because it's a tourist area must be maintained, but as inhabitants who deserve a clean place to live and the City Council must change its strategy to keep the city well."
There are other sites that also look sloppy and incomplete, he said, such as the Shield and the Venadito of Mazatlan.
"Today, in order for the tourist to come, it does not depend solely on the promotion, there has to be a good product, a city that has cleaning on its beaches, on its avenues, permanent maintenance in what are the tourist attractions," he said.
"There has been no due attention and this type of fact highlights something that is noticeable by everyone, maintenance is necessary."
Raúl Llera Martínez, undersecretary of Tourism Promotion, said that it is urgent the rescue of tourist sites in Mazatlan, because they are not used correctly and project a bad image.
He talked about a series of places that must be put online together with the municipality and be a priority to have the city up to date, clean, orderly and that is attractive because it is of tourist vocation.
“Fortunately Mazatlan offers many historical and cultural sites and places that will have to be ordered first, cleaned up and put us online to make them attractive in order to add them in the inventory we have to promote to the destination.”
Asks for IP results from Congress
Eduardo Dávalos, president of the CCE, proposed that deputies assume the great responsibility of being an example of seeing for a better Sinaloa for the various challenges it faces.
A Legislature of decent work and free of agreements between parties and of the State Executive claims the business sector of Mazatlan, because there is a convulsed Sinaloa that faces great problems.
Since yesterday, the 60 legislature was installed, composed of seven political forces, 19 deputies of the PRI, 13 of the PAN, three of the New Alliance Party, two of the PRD, one of the PT, one of Convergencia and one more of the PVEM, which adds a total of 40 popular representatives.
Among the list of expectations of the new members of the State Congress is that they come to carry out a clean management and commitment to society and with the different sectors that require responsible legislative work and that the same patterns of partisanship or paternalism are not repeated.
Eduardo Dávalos Zamora, president of the Business Coordinating Council, proposed that the newly released 40 deputies of the State Congress assume the great responsibility of being an example of seeing for a better Sinaloa for the various challenges it faces.
"We hope that the deputies really exercise their right, free of agreements between the parties because it is customary that they make arrangements to look after their own interests and become carnival comparsas of the rulers and the leaders of the parliamentary fractions."
Hence, he spoke of certifying the commitment to establish the necessary bridges that will carry out the Ruler in turn.
"Let's occupy that they come to do a decent job and above all committed to society and the sectors because it was the Sinaloans who gave them the vote of confidence in the last elections."
José Ramón Manguart Sánchez, president of the Three Islands Hotel Association, considered that one of the great challenges of the 40 popular representatives is to push and join forces in search of the economic growth of the state, because today the economic situation is very complicated.
“Most importantly, they boost economic growth, generate incentives for investment and infrastructure, and that will somehow encourage job creation.”
Regarding tourism activity, he demanded that the new legislators give him the weight he has within the state budget to strengthen the State Secretary of Tourism.
"I understand that it is the second Secretariat in the state with the lowest budget and is incongruous as an important economic activity and generating currency and investment and that it directly competes with agro industry."
In addition, it is necessary to labell resources for detonating tourism projects that help to give security to the state and in the update to the Alcohol Law that they have not been able to resolve in previous legislatures.
You're looking for a magician king
Dolls, cars and bicycles, among other toys, are the yearning of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family.
For his parents, the next King’s Day it will be difficult to please his illusions, since his home is made up of nothing more and nothing less than 12 people and a goat.
However, Adelaide, Elizabeth, Jesús, José Alfredo, María Guadalupe, Isaías, José Juan and Miguel do not lose hope that Melchor, Gaspar and Baltazar will give them the toys and clothing they so desire.
The family inhabits a humble house in Colonia Salvador Allende and they live up to date because the father does not have a fixed job.
"Well, sometimes we look hard because my husband doesn't have chamba, but we sell mandarina and potato ... already in the afternoon I go out with the 'plebes' to sell to the street," said Cayetana Valenzuela Andrade, mother of the little ones.
Adelaide, 4, wants a doll or a bicycle, while her 3-year-old sister Isabel would like a Barbie doll.
For his part, José Alfredo, 10, has long aspired to have a 'monster' car and for his little brother Jesus, 8 months old, Cayetana suggests clothes carved for a year.
"He said (José Alfredo), 'I'd like this monster,' but I said 'we're going to see son,' because I was very expensive," Cayetana recalled with sadness.
For older children who were in school, clothing and footwear would be ideal.
María Guadalupe, 11, is size 12; Isaiah, 10 years old, is size 10, while Michael, 8 years old, wears size eight.
José Juan, 14, lacks tennis, so a pair of size five and a half or six would be of great help.
If you want to help to realize the dream of the children of the Coronado Valenzuela family you can participate in the campaign You are looking for a Wizard King, donating a toy or cash at the Northwest reception.
Fourteen suspected drug traffickers arrested
On Wednesday in Estrie, a dozen persecutions have been carried out within the framework of the dismantling of a cannabis production and sale network.
Fourteen people have been detained who will face various accusations.
Persecutions have occurred in Sherbrooke, Orford and Stoke, mainly in the places where the drug was produced.
"About 750 cannabis plants, four kilograms of hashish, money and production equipment have been seized," said Louis-Philippe Ruel, Quebec Security spokesman.
In total, eleven men and three women have been arrested, including the network’s two alleged leaders, two Sherbrooke men.
Detainees had to appear at Sherbrooke courthouse in the hours following his arrest.
They could face accusations of cannabis production, possession in order to traffic, plot and theft of services, among others.
These persecutions have occurred after a long investigation that has lasted about a year.
"It has been the information provided by the public that has allowed us to carry out the arrests," says Mr. Ruel.
Around seventy officers from the Estrie Quebec Security investigation module from the Sherbrooke Police Service and the Memphrémagog Police Department participated in this police operation.
Clandestine migration can be fought without problems
The conservators’ bill, which according to the government, should solve the problem of pateras laden with clandestine migrants, will surely face great opposition, since the three opposition parties have announced on Wednesday that they could not support it.
"I base myself mainly on the Charter."
"I meet here with a party that has created the granite that you tread," said liberal leader Michael Ignatieff, surrounded by deputies following the weekly caucus meeting.
The liberals argue that the bill in its current form, which would make it possible to detain up to a year anyone who comes from a ship identified as transporting clandestine immigrants, violates the Canadian Charter of Rights and Liberties.
The leader of the Quebec Bloc, Gilles Duceppe, also indicated that he would not be able to support the bill because it targets the wrong target.
"We are against and, it seems, all the opposition parties are against the project, so we'll see what the government does," Duceppe said.
NPD immigration criticism Olivia Chow has rejected criticism that her party isn’t steadfast enough with clandestine immigrants.
"Neo-Democrats agree that measures will be taken against clandestine paters and that's what we want with another bill, the C-35," Chow said.
“We don’t support the conservative bill because it attacks refugees and jails children for a minimum of one year,” Chow said.
Immigration Minister Jason Kenney has indicted parties of the position of playing politicians and ignoring the will of Canadians.
“The vast majority of Canadians tell deputies not to tolerate this type of clandestine migration, these criminal operations against Canada and that they treat the country as if it were a doormat.”
“They expect Canada to take firm action,” Minister Kenney said.
The government has not announced a credible vote on its bill, which could lead the country to elections as a result of its rejection.
However, a conservative has informed the QMI Agency that the Government was going to request the holding of a vote so that each deputy who is going to oppose will later explain himself to their voters.
The new unity doesn’t achieve unanimity
Although the possible establishment of a permanent anti-corruption and collusion unit by Jean Charest had been announced, many observers continue to call for the establishment of a public consultation commission on the issue.
The Minister of Public Security, Robert Dutil, supports that this idea has not been raised two days ago: Quebec would have consulted with the Department of Investigation of New York has been in question since January last year.
The metropolis has had a permanent anti-corruption unit since 1870.
Consequently, Tuesday’s announcement caught more than one by surprise.
It can be said that, during a meeting of the Permanent Council on Quebec Police Services on 29 October, this measure was never debated despite the presence of Robert Dutil.
That was enough for the critic of the Quebec party dealing with the question of public safety, Stéphane Bergeron, calling this announcement "a rabbit taken out of the hat" and as "a last-minute sloppy solution."
Minister Dutil, for his part, has responded to those who fear that political power has too much influence over possible future unity.
“You’ll see the results when we announce it,” he simply says.
"We're aware of this problem."
For CSQ President Réjean Parent, there’s nothing that can replace the launch of a public poll.
"The more Mr. Charest stumbles to avoid the creation of this commission of inquiry, the more people share our opinion, that is, he has something to hide," he explains.
"The only way to have a commission of inquiry is with the resignation of Jean Charest," explains Yves Boisvert, professor of ethics and governance at the National School of Public Administration.
To get out of this impasse, he suggests to Quebec to grant a special mandate to the general intervener to conduct a survey on collusion and corruption.
"We must be able to extend the mandate of the general intervener," he concludes.
Likewise, it considers that Quebec could grant the intervener the same competences as to a commissioner, thus allowing him to force people to testify.
The creation of a permanent crew is not the only budding project in the Charest Government.
Quebec also requests Ottawa to amend the Criminal Code to better suit this type of offence.
This request seems to come from the prosecutor of Operation Marteau, who has informed the Minister of Justice Jean-Marc Fournier that the case law is not explicit in such investigations.
The Federal Minister of Public Security, Vic Toews, has confirmed for his part that his Justice counterpart, Rob Nicholson, is currently inclined to make certain amendments in relation to this issue.
During Operation Spring 2001 related to biker groups, even if the Criminal Code had not been adapted, 131 persons were arrested.
Subsequently, Ottawa promoted the adoption of the Gangsterism Act.
Rivers under extreme surveillance
Several waters are being closely monitored due to heavy rainfall that has been recorded in several regions of the province since Tuesday.
On Wednesday afternoon there was a landslide in Shawinigan in Mauricie.
As a preventive measure, two houses have been evacuated.
At night, there was another landslide in Saint-Lin-Laurentides, in Lanaudière.
A road sank.
From Tuesday to Wednesday night, several regions registered heavy rainfall, between 40 and 70 millimeters of rain.
In addition, an additional 20 to 40 millimeters are expected from now until Thursday night, depending on the region.
Sectors north of the river and the Outaouais valley were the most affected.
They could receive up to 100 millimeters of rain.
Since these precipitations add to the melting of snow that had already accumulated in certain sectors, it is feared that various rivers will overflow.
On Wednesday night, Quebec’s civil security unit issued alerts to the Ouareau and Achigan rivers in Lanaudière.
The rivers of the north, in Saint-Jérôme, in the Laurentides, and of the Chêne, in Lotbinière, have already caused flooding in the past and are under surveillance.
In the Lanaudière region, heavy rainfall has caused some lakes in Sainte-Julienne, Sainte-Marcelline-de-Kildare and Rawdon.
In Saint-Calixte, heavy rains have dragged a temporary bridge.
As a preventive measure, ten residences have been evacuated.
In Outaouais, the overflow of certain lakes has caused the closure of several roads, in particular in the municipality of Mansfield-et-Pontefract.
The civil security unit remains on alert and closely monitors the situation, according to Annik Bouchard, a security adviser.
Quebec’s flood forecasting center on Wednesday issued announcements communicating the heavy floods of several rivers, notably the Maskinongé, Assomption, Ouareau, Achigan, Batiscan and Loup.
They partially blame Dupont
Quebec radio presenter Stéphane Dupont has suffered a certain setback before the Canadian Council of Broadcasting Standards (CCNR) for controversial statements in relation to social assistance and Haitians, as well as for their rude language on radio.
The CCNR considers that Mr. Dupont and his CHOI 98.1 FM team had the right to express their opinion, but certain statements contained “inaccurate information on social assistance, unduly negative representation of Haitians and rude language that should not be used in emissions broadcast during the day”.
The Council also indicated that discussions on suicide, including the dissemination of the name of two people who had committed suicide, did not violate the codes on broadcasting.
CCNR had received a number of complaints in connection with comments made on broadcasts between 2008 and 2010.
The first decision came after a complaint by the Common Front of people receiving social assistance from Quebec.
The group complained that the amounts mentioned in the broadcast were not correct.
Presenters must communicate reliable data and not lead to deceit.
Regarding the suicide, the presented Stéphane Dupont had stated that suicide was an act of cowardice.
Most members of the Committee have concluded that the message conveyed was that suicide should not be seen as a solution to the problems of life.
"The majority of the Committee considers that the episodes called into "Dupont le midi" have dealt with a sensitive issue in an equitable and appropriate manner."
Consequently, in this respect any fault has been dismissed.
The latest litigation concerned the statements made after the earthquake in Haiti.
At that time, Stéphane Dupont and Jean-René Dufort had starred in a media confrontation that had provoked numerous reactions.
CHOI’s presenter stated that he hadn’t made any donations to help Haitians because he couldn’t know for sure if those in need were actually going to receive the money.
The Committee considers that these comments are not abusive, but that their cumulative effect constitutes an unduly-negative representation of Haitians.
"I've served my three-day suspension up there."
"My file has been clean for a year and a half," he said.
In all decisions, the Committee denounces the issuance of rude language.
In any case, the CHOI-FM radio network has collaborated "even more than required".
The consequences will be minimal for the station, which must, among other things, announce the decisions once in the hours of greatest audience within three days.
Kung-Fu King in Montreal
Algerian Montrelese of Algerian origin Nacereddine Zemmal has had an extremely peculiar route.
The athlete, who excels in "kung-fu "full contact"), kickboxing and and thai box, rises to the tatamis and rings of the world in search of new challenges.
"I started practicing martial arts like kung-fu and kickboxing 12 years ago in Algeria," said Zemmal, who has lived in the metropolis since 2008 to advance the professional level.
“In my case, I’m looking for fights everywhere because, for those who don’t enjound the on-floor game involving mixed martial arts, there are few competitions in North America.”
The athlete, who works as a security agent and as an employee of the UPS company, has recently given bad news to his rivals, winning two gold medals at the World Karate and Kickboxing Association (WKA), held October 23-30 in Edinburgh, Scotland.
“I’ve won the kickboxing full contact and thai box categories,” he explained.
"I am currently preparing to participate in the San Shou World Cup in China."
"They'll invite athletes to finish in the top four in the last world championship in each category."
"In 2009, I won the bronze medal in Toronto."
"In 2005, in Vietnam, I finished in eighth place."
"In 2007, in China, I finished the tournament in fifth place, but reached third place in Toronto in 2009," he added.
"What's interesting is that I've been making progress in every competition."
"At this step, I'll be the first this time or next year!"
The "san shou" or "sanda" is a discipline native to China in which it is allowed to strike with fists and feet, as well as throw adversaries to the ground.
Clashes are originally held on a platform and, less frequently, in a ring.
The most famous fighter in this type of combat is the Vietnamese-American Cung Le, widely recognized in the world of martial arts.
Although Nacer Zemmal must only maintain a competition in his career, he will undoubtedly attend the great tournament "King of Sanda", the Super Bowl of kung-fu.
"This competition is China's most noticeable and prestigious," explains the sympathetic boy.
“It’s like the equivalent of Lumpinee Stadium (Thailand’s largest stadium).”
"It's been an amazing experience."
Zemmal has won his first match, in the semi-finals of his category (80 kg), but lost in the final.
"The one who has beaten me is a real professional," he says.
"There are four competitors per category (80 kg, 85 kg, 90 kg and 90 kg)."
"The winners of the semi-finals face each other in the final of their category."
"Next, the champions of each category meet to crown a single champion, the king of kung-fu."
450 million in contracts without tenders
Hydro-Québec has awarded direct contracts worth 450 million dollars to engineering companies for the Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert hydroelectric project.
In the center of this avalanche of more than one hundred contracts is a consortium formed by the companies BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor, which has obtained without a previous tender the most important contract, the management of the project, for a sum of 295 million.
The consortium, called Énergie gérance, was created in 2004 following Hydro-Québec’s decision to entrust the private sector with its responsibility to manage this important 5,000 million-dollar hydroelectric project.
At the beginning of 2005, the Énergie gérance consortium obtained the lucrative contract without a public tender.
For the Quebec Party, recourse to consortiums of companies poses a serious ethical problem that threatens to eliminate competition and cause a rise in prices.
“It’s a new method of collusion and eliminating competition.”
"That is, the people, instead of doing it secretly, now they do it openly," Quebec Party MP Sylvain Simard told a news conference yesterday.
Earlier in the morning, Mr. Simard raised the issue in the National Assembly because, he says, he does not understand how the public interest can benefit in this way.
The Minister of Natural Resources, Nathalie Normandeau, responded in the afternoon, firmly denying that there has been collusion with this contract.
"I'm starting to get tired," he protested, arguing that the PQ had acted "dishonestly" and "undergetted."
He pointed out that "the minimum" would be for the PQ to apologise.
Consortium formula is a common practice throughout the world.
Normally, it is an alliance with a time limit that allows two or several companies to pool their knowledge to respond to a tender for a complex project.
The Eastmain project presents a high level of complexity, taking into account the magnitude of the works.
The works have already begun and will last until 2012.
In the whole case, the contract has been granted without competition; it has been a negotiation.
In addition, consortium formula has ceased to be the exception to the standard in Quebec, even for relatively simple projects.
As Le Devoir published last week, the formula of consortiums in the technical advisory sector is undergoing an investigation in the Government.
A quick look at Quebec’s business register identifies more than 200 consortiums, some of which seem permanent.
In this way, Dessau works in partnership with most of its main competitors, such as CIMA+, Génivar, SNC-Lavalin, Roche et Tecsult, and others.
Each combination, and there are dozens of them, leads to a single legal person, i.e. Dessau.
Regarding the Énergie gérance consortium, Minister Normandeau stressed that "the territory of James Bay responds to a particular labour dynamic and Hydro-Québec responds specifically to it since the 1970's".
The great head of the Eastmain project, Réal Laporte, of the Société d'énergie de la Baie James (SEBJ), has given an explanation that has highlighted the fact that the project management contract has been granted directly.
"If you respond to a tender and there are few participants, your prices are going to be higher."
"You have two options: you can come into contact with the actors in the sector and tell them: we are going to negotiate a satisfactory agreement with a good price."
"However, the idea is that what matters is not the price, but the competition of the people," said Mr Laporte.
The contract awarded to the Énergie gérance consortium is part of a long list of contracts of more than 50,000 dollars obtained by the Quebec Party.
Contracts were awarded by Hydro-Québec or SEBJ for Eastmain-1-A-Sarcelle-Rupert project.
Following the verification, Le Devoir has identified 109 technical advisory contracts granted between 2002 and March 2010 to companies or their subsidiaries, in particular geotechnical laboratories.
SNC-Lavalin has obtained 22 contracts, Génivar got 16, Tecsult 14 and Dessau 10, regardless of his participation in Énergie gérance.
All contracts were negotiated.
In total, they amounted to 449,783,344 dollars, including Énergie gérance’s contract of 295,131,564 dollars.
Sylvain Simard has hinted that the four consortium-forming companies (BPR, Dessau, Groupe SM and Axor) are “well-known of the Quebec Liberal Party.”
Minister Normandeau has dropped that the PQ is also linked to these companies, which "finance", before specifying that it is more about its leaders.
Barometer of responsible consumption. "green" label does not inspire confidence
More eco-friendly but deeply skeptical.
The Quebec consumer hardly relies on the actors of sustainable development, as well as fair trade brands or ecological certificates to guide their choices.
According to the Barometer of responsible consumption, whose first results have been published yesterday, the situation is even worse. The consumer is immersed in a media universe where they swarm messages that defend a more responsible consumption, but he regrets the lack of reliable information and considers that it even slows its commitment.
Even if the consumption of so-called "socially responsible" products has increased in 2010, Quebecers seem to give very little credit to those who try to sell them.
Two-thirds of them say, in fact, that they do not trust the environmental commitment of the companies or the environmental advertising of them, reveals the measurement tool developed by the Consumer Observatory responsible for the University of Sherbrooke.
Another conclusion: half of consumers question the reliability of official certificates carried by so-called fair products, as well as the credibility of products and brands bearing labels indicating that they are “eco-responsible,” the Barometer notes.
Similarly, only 14.6 per cent of Quebecers take politicians’ speeches on this issue seriously.
In any case, information on responsible consumption seems essential to stimulate this sector of consumption, currently supported by about a third of Quebec consumers.
In fact, the gaps and the doubtful nature of the information cause 46% of consumers to dismiss this option.
It is the second obstacle identified by the Barometer, after the highest price of these products.
In addition, a third of Quebecers also say they are wary of the alleged yields of organic products or services that they are trying to sell.
On the other hand, almost half of consumers changed their habits in 2010 and, in particular, changed the brand of one or more products to respect their convictions.
61% have given priority to purchases in stores in their neighborhood and have preferred to buy cultured food products in their region, the Barometer says.
More green and responsible (the index of responsible consumption has reached 64 points over a maximum of one hundred this year), the Homo consumus of Quebec loves to occupy the position of the king of recycling that tries to reduce its impact on the environment worrying about the protection of wildlife.
However, according to the Barometer, that social awareness seems to be of doors (of their car) outside: in 2010, only 28% of Quebecers indicated that they have used public transport or shared a car to defend their environmental principles.
In addition, 39.1% of the participants of this extensive behavioral survey, which has just completed its first edition, support the use of the bicycle and walking trips.
A loan for the State to recover eXcentris
The Government of Quebec, which they have asked to commit financially to the recovery of the eXcentris cinema complex by the Société Angus and the Parallèle, agrees to collaborate, but only through a loan.
The Société de développement Angus and the Parallèle, who were waiting for a donation of seven million dollars from the Ministry of Culture to re-purchase Daniel Langlois the eXcentris film complex on Saint-Laurent boulevard and return their true cinéphile vocation, they have not obtained what they asked for.
The Government has agreed to get involved in the matter, but in its own conditions.
Instead of the expected donation, the Government of Quebec proposes a loan whose amount would, in fact, be lower than the initial expectations; this could constitute a viable solution under advantageous conditions, at least in the opinion of certain public employees.
Quebec had already rejected a first action plan and a 12.8 million request last spring, but since then requests have been revised downwards.
The president of the Société de développement des entreprises culturelles (SODEC), François Macerola, who leads the eXcentris file, submitted a proposal and an action plan to Minister St-Pierre.
He has sent him one last report a dozen days ago.
However, SODEC received a counterproposal from the cabinet on Tuesday.
El Parallèle and Angus have until December 31 to respond to it.
In any case, it seems that the hypothesis of a donation from the State must be completely rejected.
Let us remember that the Association of owners of cinemas and film complexes in Quebec opposed a direct investment of the State to support the recovery of eXcentris, arguing that the Ministry of Culture should not finance real estate projects, since it has not done so with other cinemas.
On the other hand, various representatives of the sector do demand considerable financial assistance.
Daniel Langlois, the owner of it, has carried for ten years the burden of the signature cinema in its premises of the complex, after financing its own construction, which could justify a derogation by the Treasury Council.
The lack of theaters for independent films is evident in Montreal and eXcentris had been able to retain his clientele.
The company Angus and Parallèle, led by Christian Yaccarini, promote a private financing campaign, hoping to gather 700,000 dollars to make their way towards acquisition.
Since the spring of 2009, only one of these theaters, the Parallèle, continues to screen films on eXcentris.
The objective of the future buyers is to reopen the three theaters on February 27, screening Xavier Beauvois "Des hommes et des dieux".
However, Christian Yaccarini has not contacted Le Devoir again and we do not know what he and his group of the downward proposals they have received.
The veil of Canada is lifted complex...
The new U.S. diplomatic cables released by WikiLeaks unveil what the U.S. views as the complex of Canadians with regard to Americans, but at the same time reflect the latter’s sense of superiority towards Canada.
U.S. diplomats are shocked that Canadian voters don’t think about Canadian-U.S. relations when they’re going to vote and believe President Obama’s visit has helped the conservative government get its budget approved in 2009!
The four new communications between the U.S. embassy in Ottawa and Washington that have been made public yesterday, offer rather juicy details than truly substantial content.
A cable of September 22, 2008, that is, during the middle of the federal election campaign, relates the extent to which the United States does not enter into the issues debated by political leaders.
This provoked great surprise the American ambassador sent to Ottawa, David Wilkins.
Mr Wilkins offers some explanations for this situation.
“Perhaps political leaders are conditioned by an inferiority complex practically inherent in Canadians in dealing with daily domestic issues, rather than leaving such an election to the United States (as during the 1988 free trade campaign),” he writes.
Consequently, he concludes, the United States is "the proverbial elephant of this choice: omnipresent, but potentially too dangerous to talk about it."
The ambassador has even more problems in understanding this "deafening silence" because the two countries maintain intense political and economic relations.
"Logically, he thinks, the ability of a candidate, of a party and, even more, of the head of a party to manage these essential relations should be a key factor in which voters should think when voting."
You can also observe this feeling of importance the cable sent on January 22, 2009 by Terry Breese, chargé d'affaires in Ottawa, to prepare President Barack Obama for his first visit to Ottawa.
“Their travel will contribute to ensuring the government’s survival during the vote of confidence in early February on the federal budget, through which Canada will for the first time deficit in the last decade due to a recovery plan of between 30,000 and 40,000 million dollars,” writes Mr. Breese.
Nowhere is it mentioned that the government had to extend Parliament before Christmas to avoid replacing the coalition or that the opposition parties conditioned their survival to the quality of the proposed recovery plan.
Another January 2008 communication signed by Ambassador Wilkins alerts Washington about the increasingly negative image transmitted by the drama series disseminated at the U.S. CBC.
The ambassador is concerned about Border's broadcast, Little Mosque on the Prairie, Intelligence and H2O, in which unsubtle references are made to Maher Arar, the "Non-Fly-List" or even the kidnappings for the CIA to carry out strong interrogations.
Another cable says that, during their first bilateral meeting, Hillary Clinton asked her French counterpart Bernard Kouchner if Europe would be willing to host some Guantanamo prisoners.
Mr Kouchner answered yes, but it depended "on each case."
At the end of the meeting, they wrote "has delivered a document to the Secretary of State regarding Omar Khadr, a 15-year-old Muslim of Canadian origin."
"Secretary agreed to review the file."
Lastly, we can mention that the encouragement in relation to the WikiLeaks website has been warmed.
Tom Flanagan, a former adviser very close to Stephen Harper, stated on CBC’s radio that the person responsible for this massive leak should be “murdered.”
Mike Huckabee echoes in the United States.
A global plan to renovate lighting
Yesterday, the U.N
This ambitious plan is framed in the set of large specific projects that the UN expects to be adopted in Cancun to compensate for the lack of binding reduction commitments in industrialized countries.
According to the plan presented yesterday, if incandescent bulbs, which still monopolize between 50 and 70% of the market, were replaced by fluoccompact bulbs or, even better, by electroluminescent diode bulbs (DEL or "LED", much of the 1,800 million tons (Gt) of GHG emitted on the planet to illuminate humans would be avoided.
Enlightenment is responsible for 8% of global emissions.
It is the equivalent of 70% of the emissions from the planetary car fleet.
In Copenhagen last year, last year, large emitting countries, such as the United States, China, Europe and others, including Canada, committed to voluntary reduction plans that had to reduce global GHG emissions from 53 Gt to 49 Gt.
However, 5 Gt of this amount should still be decreased to stabilize, before 2020, the increase in temperatures by two degrees Celsius to avoid getting out of control.
In incandescent bulbs, 95% of energy dissipates in the form of heat and the rest, i.e. 5%, produces light.
In the case of fluocompact bulbs, energy expenditure is reduced by 75% and with the new LED bulbs, energy consumption is still reduced twice more.
Fluocompact bulbs last an average of between 6,000 and 10,000 hours.
The new LED bulbs, such as those launched by the manufacturer Philips this week (a company that, in addition, has participated in the UN study), have a life expectancy of 25,000 hours and consume 80% less energy than an incandescent bulb, which allows us to make us an idea of the savings that can be achieved with the replacement plan that would be applied in one hundred countries.
These new bulbs can also work with rheostates and emit very little heat.
On top of that, they don’t contain any mercury.
A number of specialists have expressed reservations about fluocompact bulbs, since they contain mercury that should be disposed of as hazardous wastes, which is not always possible in developing countries, unless manufacturers place instructions.
However, according to the report, the main source of mercury emission on the planet is not fluoccompact bulbs, but coal-fired thermal power plants, which emit a large amount of mercury to produce electricity that is wasted with incandescent bulbs.
In the U.S. alone, these plants emit 25,000 tons of mercury per year.
The report details the advantages that this international programme would offer to each country.
For example, Indonesia would thus save one billion dollars a year in electricity and eight million tons of CO2, that is, the equivalent of 3.5 thermal power plants whose construction would cost it 2.5 billion.
Côte d'Ivoire eagerly awaits the announcement of election results
U.N. Secretary-General Ban Ki-moon has called for "immediate" publication of the results of the presidential elections in Côte d'Ivoire, as the deadlines set have expired.
"The Secretary-General stresses the need for the independent electoral commission to complete its work and announce the provisional results immediately," a U.N. spokesman said.
"Call on all Ivory Coast parties and institutions to demonstrate their sense of responsibility and to refrain from any initiative until the provisional results are announced."
Despite the pressure exerted by some foreign governments, the electoral commission has lefted to pass the deadline of Wednesday at midnight without the results being published, three days since Sunday’s scrutiny.
About forty minutes before the end of the deadline, the CEI president had told television that the commission "continued working" to announce the results.
On Wednesday there have been requests in the world that the CIS finally proclaim the name of the winner of the second round between the outgoing Laurent Gbagbo and his opponent Alassane Ouattara.
However, Gbagbo’s side counts on the annulment by the Constitutional Council of “fraudulent” votes in the north, in the hands of the old New Forces (FN) rebellion since the failed coup d’état in September 2002.
Pascal Affi N'Guessan, director of Côte d'Ivoire's president's campaign, has claimed that FN voters' intimidation maneuvers in the north of the country had deterred supporters of the outgoing president from voting.
The entire election could be annulled if the Constitutional Council accepts complaints lodged by Gbagbo.
However, Gbagbo’s adversaries consider that the country’s highest court is not neutral, since it is led by Paul Yao N’Dré, Gbagbo’s close political ally.
Alassane Ouattara, a man from the north who denies any relationship with the former rebels, has judged "unacceptable" the delay in the publication of the results and has asked Gbagbo to respect them, but has not gone so far as to claim victory.
His allies claim that Gbagbo blocks the publication of the results because he knows that he has lost and that filing a complaint with the Constitutional Council having prevented the electoral commission from disseminating the results would be illegal.
The second round of the presidential elections, on Sunday, should have put the icing on the process of reunification and reconciliation of a country divided into two for years.
The United Nations on Wednesday warned Côte d’Ivoire’s political class that they could be held responsible for possible acts of violence.
Laurent Gbagbo had finished in the first round, on 31 October, with 38% of the votes compared to 32% of Alassane Ouattara, who publicly received the support of former President Henri Konan Bédié (25% in the first round).
Unemployment Rate Remains Stable in France in Third Quarter
According to the International Labour Organization (ILO), the unemployment rate in France has remained stable at 9.3 per cent on the mainland (9.7 per cent taking into account overseas departments), as indicated by data corrected with provisional seasonal variations (SVCs) published Thursday by the Insee.
The number of unemployed remains at 2,631,000 in mainland France.
Data for the second quarter have been confirmed, 9.3% of the working population in the mainland and 9.7% with DOMS.
In one year, the unemployment rate has registered an increase of 0.1 points.
In general, 3.4 million people in metropolitan France did not work in the third quarter, but want to work, whether or not they are available in the two weeks to work and whether or not they look for a job.
The situation of young people present in the labour market has continued to worsen, with a further increase in the unemployment rate to 24.2% in mainland France (+0.9 points in three months, +0.5 points in a year), 25% including DOMs.
During the third quarter, 637,000 young people between the ages of 15 and 24 were unemployed by the ILO.
For its part, there has been a slight increase in unemployment among the elderly, to 6.4% in mainland France (+0.2 points in both three months and a year), that is, 482,000 people over 50 years of age, a figure that amounts to 6.6% with the DOMS.
Unemployment stability has only been recorded among people aged between 25 and 49, with 8.3% in mainland France (-0.1 points in three months, +0.1 points in a year) and 8.8% throughout France.
The Insee has not published the monthly unemployment rate since 2006, after a controversy about its calculation methods.
Eurostat, the European Union’s statistics agency, continues to do so and on Tuesday announced a rate of 9.8% for France (including DOM) in October, after 9.9% in September.
Alexandre Guérini Investigated and Detained
Alexandre Guérini, 53, director of waste treatment companies and brother of President PS of the General Council of Bouches-du-Rhône, is being investigated and has been imprisoned in Luynes prison, near Aix-en-Provence, in the framework of an investigation into allegedly fraudulent public contracts.
Alexandre Guérini is being investigated for abuse of social property, embezzlement of funds and public property, cover-up, active corruption, inflict trafficking and possession of ammunition, according to his lawyer.
"Alexandre Guérini is going to fight and answer all the questions that the magistrate raises, not ghosts."
“It’s easy to pursue him for a multitude of reprehensible acts, it’s another thing to prove them,” his lawyer, Florence Rault, told AFP ahead of his client’s appearance.
Mr. Guérini, brother of the leader of the PS in the department, Jean-Noël Guérini, wanted to transmit through his lawyers to the judge, before his appearance, a document to be explained.
On Wednesday night they began to investigate and also imprisoned one of their people nearby, Philippe Rapezzi.
According to his lawyer, Mr. Lucien Simon, all his client has done has been to take care of the landfinders he must manage.
With regard to the allocation of contracts or to the destination of funds related to their implementation, he does not know anything about it, according to Mr. Simon.
In the framework of this file, Daniel Pinna, former director general of the services of the urban agglomeration of Aubagne, has also been being investigated since Wednesday for embezzlement and complicity, but he has been left on probation and is prohibited from meeting with the protagonists of the case.
"My client has been able to explain all the facts they have reproached him."
"The investigating magistrate has taken into account the explanations and released him," his lawyer, Mr. Yann Arnoux, explained.
On Tuesday night, four other people were questioned and released, under judicial control in the case of two of them, according to the deputy prosecutor of the Republic, Michel Raffin.
Michel Karabadjakian, Deputy Director General of Marseille Provence Métropole (MPM), has been charged with “trafficking and passive corruption.”
Two Aubagne agglomeration officials, Michèle Duval and Christophe Bringuier, have been questioned for “abduction and embezzlement of public goods by a person engaged in public service” and “complicity in the embezzlement of public funds,” respectively.
Finally, Alexandre Guérini's partner, Jeannie Peretti, is being investigated for "cover-up of the abuse of social property."
People who have confronted Mr. Guérini included Stéphane Brunengo-Girard, recently jailed in a parallel case of fraudulent public contracts affecting Corsica.
Investigators suspect that their societies have served to launch the capital of great banditism.
The investigation is being carried out within the framework of several judicial instructions initiated against X in April 2009 after the receipt of anonymous communications, of course fraud in the allocation of public contracts, particularly in the waste sector.
A source close to the research points out that the research focuses especially on the conditions of assignment and operation of a landfill in La Ciotat, which collects Aubagne waste, as well as other cities in the urban community of Marseille (MPM).
Clotilde Reiss Subject: Elisha has exaggerated Syria's mediation
For more than ten months, between July 2009 and May 2010, the fate of Clotilde Reiss, the young French researcher detained in Iran for "espionage", has had the public in France, has alerted diplomacy and has accentuated the deterioration of relations between Paris and Tehran.
All under the watchful eye of U.S. diplomats.
U.S. documents obtained by WikiLeaks and consulted by Le Monde reveal certain unknown aspects of this crisis.
They describe how the Elysee has explained its strategy to the Americans to the point of giving them advice to manage their own “hostage” crises in Iran (a small group of hikers).
They show French officials tried to convince Washington that the release of the young Frenchwoman did not occur in exchange for the release of Majid Kakavand, an Iranian detained in France and wanted by the U.S
Finally, they reveal the stark reality of how the French presidency tried to manipulate the media to give the idea that Syrian President Bashar Al-Assad had played an essential role in releasing Clotilde Reiss.
He didn't actually do anything.
A diplomatic telegram drafted on 12 August 2009 in Paris is entitled "Iran: French comments on how to handle hostage-taking cases".
Elysseum’s advisor for strategic affairs, François Richier, accompanied by Quai d’Orsay’s director for North Africa and the Middle East, Patrice Paoli, talks to U.S. diplomat Kathleen Allegrone.
They explain the French method.
They advise Americans to do the following to repatriate their own forcibly held citizens in Iran.
"Make noise," they say, "continuously release" into the media the words "fundamental human rights," "innocence," "immediate release."
All to embarrass the Iranian regime, careful of its image.
"When the Iranians ask you to stop, do it even more frequently."
Because, once the attempts to negotiate by discreet channels are exhausted, "the silence will not solve anything", the Iranians are "experts in dilatory manoeuvres".
Mr. Richier adds that the detention of foreigners is "a common Iranian tactic: the taking of hostages for political blackmail."
He announces that Clotilde Reiss’ release of prison (which will occur on August 16, the day he will take refuge in the French embassy) “will require the payment to the Iranians of a large amount of money” (230,000 euros of reward).
In addition, he points out that the Elysee anticipates other hostage crises, a phenomenon that "will increase".
The Iranians could "in the near future stop another of the nearly 1,800 French citizens living in Iran," he says.
28 August 2009.
Clotilde Reiss is released from prison after twelve days, but is blocked in Iran.
The following information is disseminated: Iranian Majid Kakavand, whose extradition is requested by the United States for illicit trafficking of sensitive electronic material to Iran, has been released in Paris by decision of a judge.
The U.S. Embassy, like numerous observers, are hesitant about this strange coincidence.
For Washington, which close to Iran’s clandestine networks for the acquisition of material for the nuclear program, the release of Majid Khakavand is bad news.
Has France accepted an exchange?
Releasing the Iranian in exchange for French?
During a new conversation with Kathleen Allegrone on Sept. 4, François Richier guarantees it didn’t.
The Elysee, he says, not only "has been greatly surprised by the judge's decision", but has tried to oppose it.
"There have been intense negotiations between the French presidency and the Minister of Justice" (at the time, Michèle Alliot-Marie), but "the French government cannot annul the decision," he said.
Mr. Richier also wants to demonstrate that Majid Kakavand remains under the "close" surveillance of French information services.
The Elysée’s adviser marks a phone number, hangs up and then informs the U.S. diplomat that the Iranian “is currently in buildings in Paris managed by the Iranian embassy.”
During Clotilde Reiss' detention in Iran, the Elysseum does not scale in efforts, it uses statements and "leaks" organized in the media to leen that Syrian President Bashar Al-Assad plays an "important" and "influential" role of "mediator."
The Americans observe this activity with a certain irony and verify that the manipulation brings its fruits, since several press articles convey the official French version.
A telegram dated August 25, 2009 mentions "the media 'blitz' of French officials".
To what is due such "gratitude" towards Mr. Assad, the embassy in Paris wonders.
“Despite their statements full of praise, French officials privately acknowledge that they only have a vague idea of what the Syrians have actually done.”
Next, the document addresses the reasons that have led the Elyssian to give rise to this fiction.
The great communication effort "aims to validate Sarkozy's policy of openness towards Syria," he says.
A policy initiated in 2008, which "in the opinion of numerous critical voices in France and abroad, had just been dismissed as premature and undeserved."
By "exaggerating the Syrian role," Paris tries "to show to Syria the complaiments it can reap if it plays a constructive role in the region."
The goal is also to "sow the seed of discord between Syria and Iran."
The U.S. embassy knows this from a good source.
On August 11, François Richier confides him: "of course, we don't know if the Syrians have done anything or not."
"But anyway, we've thanked him."
"At least, this should cause confusion among the Iranians."
Clotilde Reiss returns to France on May 16, 2010.
Nicolas Sarkozy receives her at the Elysee.
In a statement, he thanked the presidents of Brazil, Senegal and Sirius for playing an “active role in favor of the liberation of our compatriot.”
Starting at midnight, young Koreans can't play online
The South Korean government will adopt a so-called "Cinderella" law that will prevent teenagers from participating in online games after midnight blocking their access, government officials said.
In December, a bill will be submitted to Parliament to ask online gaming companies to block access to such games after midnight for young people under the age of 16, according to representatives of the Ministries of Culture and Family.
"Many children play all night and then find it difficult to study at school and lead a normal life during the day."
"We believe that this law is necessary to protect your health and your right to sleep," Jo Rin, the bill's ministry official, told AFP.
The online games will be available again at six in the morning.
Businesses will have one year to prepare for law enforcement, he says.
The government also intends to ask companies to limit daily access time to these games when the parents of the young people request it, he added.
In addition, it will make it available to those who request it free computer programs that allow to limit Internet connection time.
South Korea is one of the most “connected” countries and newspapers regularly give news about various facts linked to intensive use of the Internet.
The country is also the undisputed champion of world online gaming competitions.
In November, South Korea won four gold medals at the World Cyber Games, where it faced 58 other countries.
World Cup 2018-2022: FIFA designates organizing countries
In the midst of the storm caused by suspicions of corruption, FIFA’s executive committee on Thursday designates the organizing countries of the 2018 and 2022 World Cups, a clearly political choice where influence struggles and lobbying can come to prevail over technical criteria.
One thing is clear, the 2018 edition of the FIFA competition will be held in Europe, traditional bastion of the discipline (four candidates: England, Russia, Spain-Portugal and Netherlands-Belgium).
The 2022 edition will be much more exotic, given that the International Federation hopes to conquer new territories (Qatar, Australia), after a premiere on the African continent in 2010, or to continue its implementation in the United States or in the Far East (Japan, South Korea).
The list of personalities that were part of the delegations (David Cameron, Prince William, José Luis Rodríguez Zapatero, José Socrates, Bill Clinton, the emir of Qatar, the Belgian and Dutch heads of government, Zidane, Beckham, Cruyff) attested to the economic and political interests that are at stake in a World Cup.
Transactions behind the scenes, therefore, may last until the last minute.
Moreover, it is difficult to establish a hierarchy among candidates.
According to FIFA’s evaluation report, English, Spanish-Portuguese (2018) and American (2022) candidates have better arguments than their competitors.
However, these considerations won't have too much weight for the 22 members of the executive committee when voting.
The Sunday Times, responsible for the revelations that led to the suspension for corruption of two members of the CE (Reynald Temarii and Amos Adamu), could have thus ruined the opportunities of their country, the cradle of football, which only hosted the World Cup once, in 1966.
The BBC added firewood to the bonfire on Monday by releasing a documentary that has exposed three influential EC members Ricardo Teixeira, Nicolas Leoz and Issa Hayatou.
Sufficient to displease FIFA and embarrass English officials, who have jumped into an intense lobbying in recent weeks with David Cameron in the lead.
The Head of Government hopes to redibute the successful coup by his predecessor, Tony Blair, who had achieved in 2005 the organization of the JJ.OO. 2012.
Will this intense activity be enough to regain England’s brightness before the EC?
It is not clear, since the duo Spain-Portugal also has numerous strengths thanks to their stadiums, already ready, their experience (World Cup of 82 in Spain, JJ.OO in Barcelona in 1992, European Cup in 2004 in Portugal) and their football culture.
However, the bleak economic and financial prospects of both countries could bring down their ambitions.
The possible reprisals against the English and the doubts about the solvency of Spain and Portugal the only thing they manage is to give points to Russia, become an outsider of weight despite the gaps in his candidacy (stadiums, hotels, transport).
However, Prime Minister Vladimir Putin decided not to move to Zurich and has denounced an "unfair competence in the preparation of the vote", in a direct allusion to the accusations of the English press against the members of the EC.
"They're bringing out their dirty rags, trying to compromise them," Putin said.
By 2022, the U.S., which has the experience of 1994, seems the most logical favorites.
The world’s leading economic powerhouse and the headquarters of FIFA’s main sponsors, the country seems to take advantage of Japan and South Korea, which have separately developed an excellent project.
However, these new candidacies seem premature for two countries that have organized the championship together in 2002.
Another risk: the concession of the 2026 World Cup to China, fervently desired by FIFA, which would cause Asia to run out of possibilities by 2022.
Together with Qatar, which has presented its project based on futuristic stadiums, and Australia, FIFA would continue its expansion towards new horizons, but both countries present more risks than certainties.
Australia, like Japan and Korea, could also be harmed by the weakness of paid TV rights (depending on the time difference) by European countries, the backbone of the war for FIFA.
2nd Term: The Test
De Gaulle had really hesitated.
Mitterrand had hinted that he was hesitant.
Valéry Giscard d'Estaing and Jacques Chirac had no doubt.
Nicolas Sarkozy even less.
One mandate in the Elisseus leads to another.
Therefore, the current head of state will once again be a candidate in 2012.
However, proof of this does not combine well with the mystery often associated with the presidential function.
Therefore, we must always pretend that one doubts.
To one side and the other.
In 2006, although they did not believe it, Jacques Chirac's friends claimed that he did not rule out a third term.
On November 15, in a serious tone, Nicolas Sarkozy indicated that he would make his decision "sometime in autumn 2011".
Therefore, the fact that it deals with UMP deputies on Tuesday night in the Elisee, the possibility of "two mandates" has provoked a stir.
Is this the official announcement of your candidacy?
Not less.
The president only indicated that the 2008 constitutional reform limited the number of five-year terms to two.
It simply conveyed the facts, not an advertisement.
It’s not the first time he’s talked about the presidential elections, with a joking tone, in meetings of this kind.
"At the worst, or better, of cases, you'll still spend seven and a half years with me," he said in July 2009.
On another occasion, in May 2010: "Don't think I don't wonder what's coming next."
His claims yesterday do not bring him closer to the candidacy, nor less.
They arouse comments for two reasons.
Accelerating the race for candidacy on the PS, on the one hand.
But it is not the fact that Ségolène Royal has climbed onto the fore that leads Nicolas Sarkozy to sincere.
On the contrary, the fact that his candidacy in 2012 is evident is what leads many people of the PS to claim that the battle is organized earlier than expected.
On the other hand, bad surveys.
For the first time, right-wing voters prefer François Fillon’s candidacy to Nicolas Sarkozy (46% versus 44%, according to BVA-L’Express).
"We have a candidate and in the UMP there is no discussion on the issue," the Prime Minister's environment reacted immediately and prudently.
“If he thinks he can lose, he won’t be a candidate,” Jean-Pierre Raffarin says.
“He’ll always think he can win,” corrects Alain Juppé, resigned to the evidence.
Euro: ECB is forced to maintain its acquisitions of state letters
The last meeting of the European Central Bank (ECB) Board of Governors of the year should make it possible to clarify the modalities for exiting the crisis or, at the very least, announcing the interruption of a large number of unconventional monetary policy operations that should end at the end of the year.
Due to the European sovereign debt crisis, Frankfurt’s issuing institute certainly cannot afford to be so peremptory if it does not want to risk aggravating a more than complicated situation today in European bond markets.
The political cost would be equally high.
Yesterday, during a press conference, French Finance Minister Christine Lagarde has backed the active role played by the ECB in favor of solving the debt crisis of European states.
He did not want to say if he considered that the central bank should do more, for example increasing its program to purchase state obligations.
"I reserve my opinion for my fellow Europeans"...
On the eve, in Brussels, during his hearing before the Committee on Economic and Monetary Affairs of the European Parliament, the president of the ECB, Jean-Claude Trichet, was not more eloquent in terms of his intentions, indicating only that the program of purchase of State Letters in the secondary market "continued" and that "we will see what we decided" during the meeting on Thursday.
The only thing that is clear is that the ECB is going to keep its main invariable rate at 1%, the lowest level in its history since May 2009.
In practice, the ECB’s acquisition of Treasury obligations doubled last week to about 1.350 million euros.
In addition, according to indiscreeted market operators, yesterday it would again have bought Irish and Portuguese securities.
In total, the central banks of the eurosystem have acquired securities for an amount of 67,000 million euros since May 2010.
Among market operators, there is no doubt in the slightest that purchases will follow (see page 29).
Economists also don’t envisage any significant course changes.
Although loan operations more than three months have already disappeared from the ECB’s arsenal, it still makes unlimited liquidity allocations to beneficial conditions with durations ranging from one week to three months.
Economists are expecting this device to continue today for a few months.
"I don't think he's going to significantly modify his program."
“If not, it would run the risk of provoking new tensions in the short term,” says Michael Schubert, an economist at Commerzbank.
"It seems more likely that there will be a progressive exit announced during the first quarter of 2011," said Norbert Bräms, an economist in Sal.
Oppenheim.
With regard to interventions in the public debt market, "the ECB should maintain a purchase volume of such obligations in order for the market to remain stable", adds Norbert Bräms.
Bearing in mind the importance of European bank exposures to euro area sovereign debt markets, the issuing institute has no choice but to maintain its presence on the market.
"It must compensate at any price the lack of investors in the market and act in such a way as to prevent a marked increase in yields paid by the most fragile states," said an important bond manager.
Because, if interest rates turn through the clouds, states will have even more trouble reducing their indebtedness, or even to stabilize it.
This is the price of protecting the euro.
In Italy, Berlusconi’s government weathers the storm
After Italy started the week in a disturbing way, the situation improved slightly yesterday with the reflux of 10-year bond rates.
A recovery from precarious calm.
After leaving a meeting with the Minister of Economy, the regional presidents have said that they listened to Giulio Tremonti to describe the situation as "indecipherable" and that an "unpredictable" weekend was expected, statements subsequently denied by the interested party.
In any case, on Tuesday the difference with German long-term rates had reached the record figure of 210 basis points and the price of hedge contracts against the risk of bankruptcy (CRQ) had skyrocketed to 263 points, causing a stir in Rome.
In the Council of Ministers, Silvio Berlusconi commented that, in Spain, the difference with German rates was higher than 400 basis points.
But his right arm, Gianni Letta, expressed "deep concern" about "turbulencies in the markets" and a possible contagion from Ireland.
"The good news is that Italy, the euro area's third-largest economy, isn't like Greece or Ireland."
"The bad news is that it's like Portugal," explains Irwin Stelzer, an economist at the Hudson Institute.
In reality, its problem is the lack of growth and the stagnation of income associated with it, which causes the State to run the risk of not being able to meet its financing needs.
The latest forecasts of Brussels have meant a jug of cold water for the government.
It is estimated that Italian growth will reach 1.1% in 2011 and 1.4% in 2012, while the budget that is being reviewed in the Senate is based on figures of 1.3% and 2%, respectively.
Under these conditions, according to the European Commission, the public deficit will account for 4.3% of GDP next year (compared to 3.9% planned by the government) and 3.5% in 2012 (instead of 2.7%).
Hence the rumour that new budgetary provisions can be applied after the holidays, for an amount of 7,000 million euros.
"If necessary, new austerity measures must be adopted," Economic Affairs Commissioner Olli Rehn said.
At the moment, Italy has the support of international institutions.
"The foundations of its economy are in order and its accounts in good condition, as we have been saying for a long time," said Arrigo Sadun, the Italian administrator at the International Monetary Fond responsible for Italy at the IMF.
"There is no immediate problem, either in the short or long term, because despite its considerable debt (119.2% of GDP in 2011, NDLR), the stabilization efforts requested from Italy are less than those required from other countries," comments Pier Carlo Padoan, chief economist of the OECD.
There remains the problem of political instability.
On December 14, Silvio Berlusconi will face a no-confidence motion in the Chamber of Deputies, after which elections could be anticipated at the end of March.
In such a case, it would open a period unconducive to decision-making in the event of a serious crisis.
Europeans reflect on strengthening anti-crisis mechanisms
Christine Lagarde has already repeated it yesterday during a press conference in Paris: "I believe that we have already showned on Sunday night and for the day that Europeans are clearly supportive, determined, committed to the defense of their currency and their monetary zone."
Although it was relative, the relaxation registered yesterday in the rates of the obligations of the European States has boosted governments and the ECB.
However, we cannot yet talk about a principle of normalization.
Rating agency Standard & Poor's announced it could lower Portugal's debt rating, the most frequent target of speculation today.
"Like in May, there is a real lack of political clarity and transparency, which weighs significantly on banking systems," said Exane BNP Paribas economists.
Especially because "the political pace is much slower than the pace of markets."
Aware of this hiatus and the dangers it hides, European countries are intensifying debates to strengthen existing crisis exit mechanisms.
Informal consultations between finance ministers that are most directly affected and, in general, with their G-20 counterparts are on the right track.
And this also taking into account the meetings of the Eurogroup and the Ecofin at the beginning of next week.
The ECC meets today.
On the agenda, the type of unconventional measures to support euro area countries in the market collimator (see above).
Regarding the European Financial Stability Fund (EFSF), its chief executive, Klaus Regling, has started a tour of Asia in Singapore on Tuesday to inform investors in the Far East about the developments of the crisis in Europe.
Although the aim of these visits is not to raise funds, it certainly prepares the ground.
EFSF title issuances are expected to take place next month.
Yesterday, an official U.S. representative has confided to Reuters that the U.S. would support a potential increase in funds mobilized through an additional direct commitment from the IMF.
With regard to front-line countries, Portugal and Spain, they are preparing to adopt structural reforms, in particular with regard to the organisation of the labour market.
These reforms add to austerity measures with closer effect.
Lisbon and Madrid want to move on a different path than Greece and Ireland followed.
Both want to avoid at all costs requesting international aid before implementing their own budgetary corrections and deciding the structural reforms that they consider essential to increase confidence on a lasting basis.
The capitalist partners of the European funds, finally, consider the options available to accelerate the process of normalization of public liability markets.
Passing through Paris, on November 24, Axel Weber was the first to launch the idea that, if the 750,000 million euros mobilized by Europe and the International Monetary Fund were not enough, the Union would not hesitate to take out the wallet again.
The economists at Barclays Capital, a front-line player in the European public debt market, consider that an amount of around an additional 100,000 million euros would be sufficient, "preferably" in the form of direct liquidity injection.
There is the instrument for this: the European financial stabilisation mechanism, currently endowed with 60,000 million euros.
A piece of advice that would undoubtedly be able to examine European capitals in detail.
Especially if market conditions get worse.
Ice snow: 24 apartments on orange alert
Snowfall continues to lead to circulation difficulties, especially in the secondary network in Brittany, and can affect various economic activities.
Currently, twenty-four departments are on orange alert due to the risk of snowfall and ice formation.
Eure, Eure-et-Loir, Deux-Sèvres and Vienne have joined the twenty departments that were already on orange alert, eighteenth since Wednesday at noon and two, Dordogne and Gironde, joined the group at night.
According to Météo France, the heavy snowfalls affect northern Gironde and Charentes, although it also snows slightly in Île-de-France.
Layers of snow of between 2 and 5 cm are expected, punctually between 7 and 10 cm, in the affected departments, and between 1 and 3 cm, or even up to 5 cm, in the Parisian region.
The affected departments are Charente, Charente-Maritime, Côtes d'Armor, Finistère, Gironde, Dordogne, Poitou and those of the regions of Lower Normandy, Île-de-France and Lemosín.
The snowfalls are strong enough to cause circulation difficulties and affect some economic activities, warns Météo France.
In the Côtes d'Armor, access to several departmental roads has been banned from heavy-duty vehicles of more than 7.5 tonnes, including the Guingamp-Lannion and Saint-Brieuc-Loudéac axles.
According to the gendarmes, circulation in Brittany's secondary network is complicated, so they recommend that it not be driven except in case of necessity and the movement of certain TER vehicles during the day has been prohibited.
During Thursday, school transportation has been banned in Picardia.
No specific measure had been foreseen on Wednesday night to limit the movement of heavy-duty vehicles.
The prefecture "urges all drivers to drive with the greatest prudence on the roads and to adapt their driving to the situation."
The Directorate-General for Civil Aviation (DGAC) asked the airlines to annul 25% of their flights at Roissy’s Parisian airport and 10 percent in Orly on Thursday because of planned snowfall.
The DGAC advises air passengers to contact their airline.
In Britain, Gatwick Airport, south of London, and Edinburgh in Scotland are closed until Thursday morning.
Grand Paris Metro: first financial drafts
Since the debate on the modernization of transport on the Île-de-France has reached its peak, it rains billions.
At least in the debate.
In particular, we are talking about the 23,000 million for the Grand Paris subway that must build the Société du Grand Paris.
To start this SGP, the State has announced an allocation of funds of 4,000 million euros.
At the theoretical level, this sum will be what will subsequently allow the SGP to obtain loans in the markets to finance a part of its double loop.
Today, at last, we have the first accounts.
The 2010 rectificing finance law examined in the National Assembly’s finance commission this morning contains the devices planned to find the first part of that money.
There are two modes: the creation of a special equipment tax and the increase of the tax on offices on the Isle of France.
The first, as we read in article 15 of the text of the bill, is "for the benefit of the Société du Grand Paris public institution".
In addition, it is fixed in numbers: "The product of this tax is 117 million euros per year."
The second is a modification of a tax on offices that already exists "to modernize it, increase its product and allocate this increase to the Société du Grand Paris".
How much would you get with this method?
Who knows.
There are no figures in the text.
But it's far from 4,000 million.
"It is specified that the fiscal resources are added to the 4,000 million euros of the allocation of funds that will be mobilized by the State and that they will be granted to the Société du Grand Paris as the project progresses," we can read in the explanatory statement.
SGP doesn’t be surprised by this system.
"From the moment (the two taxes, ndlr) is created, we can cover the studies for three years and then receive the 4,000 million later, as we explained."
“It will be recorded in the budget and will be used according to needs.”
The GSP estimates that it will start receiving 4,000 million in 2013 for the 2014 budget.
This device, in which the 4,000 million promised does not appear in credit, can provoke a slightly lit parliamentary debate.
The first public session will be on December 7.
Adventing hackers and diligent spies
The WikiLeaks issue has a dual value.
On the one hand, it unveils an apparent scandal, a scandal that only seems scandal in the face of hypocrisy that governs relations between states, citizens and the press.
On the other hand, it announces profound changes on an international scale and foresees a future dominated by regression.
But let's go step by step.
The first aspect of WikiLeaks is the confirmation of the fact that each file created by a secret service (regardless of the nation to which it belongs) is made up exclusively of press clippings.
The "extraordinary" American revelations about Berlusconi's sexual customs only reflect what has been written for months in any newspaper (except those belonging to Berlusconi) and Khaddafi's sinistently ridiculing profile has long been giving ideas to cabaret artists to create their sketches.
The norm according to which secret files should only be made up of news of public knowledge is essential for the dynamics of the secret services, and not only in this century.
If you visit a bookstore dedicated to esoteric publications, you will see that each work repeats (about the Grail, the mystery of Rennes-le-Château, the Templars or the Rosicrucians) exactly what had been written in the previous works.
This is not only because the authors of occult texts do not want to do unpublished research (or do not know where to look for news about the non-existent), but because those dedicated to occultism not only trust what they already know, which reconfirms what they had already learned.
This is Dan Brown's mechanism of success.
The same applies to secret files.
The informant is lazy, just as he is lazy (or has little craving) the head of the secret services (if it could not be, I know, editor of the Libération), who only considers true what he can recognize.
Top secret information about Berlusconi sending Rome’s U.S. embassy to the State Department was the same one published on the Newsweek the previous week.
So why have the revelations of these files caused such a stir?
On the one hand, they only say what every cultivated person already knows, i.e
Any viewer who sees investigative films knows perfectly well, we just seem that we don’t know by hypocrisy.
In any case, repeating it publicly violates the duty of hypocrisy and gives a bad image of American diplomacy.
Secondly, the idea that any hacker can consult the most secretive secrets of the most powerful country in the world significantly affects the prestige of the State Department.
Therefore, the scandal affects both the victims and the "executions".
But let's get into the true nature of what's happened.
In the past, in Orwell’s time, he could conceive himself at full power as a Big Brother who controlled every gesture of his subjects.
Orwell’s prophecy has come true since, being able to control each movement thanks to the phone, every transaction made, hotel visited, highway taken, the citizen thus became a complete victim of the eye of power.
But when it is shown, as it happens now, that not even the crypts of the secrets of power cannot escape the control of a hacker, the control relationship ceases to be unidirectional and becomes circular.
Power controls every citizen, but every citizen (at least the hacker, chosen as the avenger of the citizens) can know all the secrets of power.
How can a power that can no longer preserve its own secrets endure?
It is true, as Georg Simmel already said, that a real secret is an empty secret (and an empty secret can never be revealed); it is also true that all knowledge about the character of Berlusconi or Merkel is actually an empty secret, without secrecy, since it proceeds from the public sphere; but to reveal, as WikiLeaks has done, that Hillary Clinton’s secrets were empty secrets means to remove all power.
WikiLeaks hasn’t harmed Sarkozy or Merkel, but yes, and a lot, Clinton and Obama.
What will be the consequences of this wound caused to such a powerful power?
It is clear that, in the future, States will no longer be able to network any reserved information, since it would be how to publish it on a poster in the middle of the street.
But it’s also clear that, with today’s technologies, you can’t expect to hold confidential conversations over the phone.
There is nothing easier to discover whether and when a Head of State has travelled by plane and contacted one of the counterparts.
How can private and confidential relationships be maintained in the future?
I know perfectly well that, at the moment, my foresight is a matter of sci-fiction and, therefore, novice, but I can imagine government agents who move discreetly in errands with uncontrollable itineraries, carrying messages that have been learned by heart or, at most, hiding some data written in the heels of their shoes.
That information is then preserved, in a single copy, in locked drawers: after all, Watergate’s espionage attempt was less successful than WikiLeaks.
In the past, I’ve written that technology currently advances like a crab, that is, backwards.
A century after the wireless telegrapher has revolutionized communications, the Internet has re-created a telegrapher with cables (telephones).
(analogical) videotapes had allowed film researchers to study a step-by-step film, moving forward and backward, uncovering all the secrets of montage, while now (digital) CDs only make it possible to advance or go back from chapter to chapter, i.e. to enormous proportions.
With high-speed trains, we can go from Rome to Milan in three hours, while by plane, with the journeys it entails, we need three and a half hours.
Therefore, it is nothing out of the ordinary that politics and communication techniques take us back to horse carriages.
One last comment.
In the past, the press was trying to understand what was secretly plotted in embassies.
At present, it is embassies that ask for confidential information from the press.
Climate: France’s slaps in its funding pledges
A year after the Copenhagen Agreement, it’s time to examine whether the rapidly unlocked (fast-starking or early) pledges made by developed countries have been complied.
The French slaps have been unveiled.
In the Copenhagen Agreement, developed countries pledged to provide US$ 30,000 million between 2010 and 2012.
The European Union pledged to contribute 7.200 million euros in three years and France 1,260 million euros (i.e. 420 million a year).
During the opening of COP16, the EU has tabled its best letters to convince its partners that their commitment to fast start financing is true: a report detailing the measures of its Member States, a press conference, a major complementary event, the distribution of brochures...
Transparent transparency was appreciable and appreciated for what is really worth it: developing countries and civil society would want all developed countries to do the same and with the same level of precision.
The EU announces that it has liberated 2010 2.200 million euros, which puts it on the right path towards fulfilling its commitment of 7.200 million in 2012.
But, if we study the situation in detail, things are more complicated.
According to the Copenhagen Agreement, the money must be "new and additional" to ensure that a complementary effort is made, without harming official development aid.
Unfortunately, no agreement has been reached on what "new and additional" means.
When the European Commission proposed harmonisation, some Member States, including France, have blocked the procedure.
Nowadays, everyone chooses the reference that best suits them.
For example, the European Commission has taken 2009 as a reference: international funding is maintained and 150 million in three years of fast start financing.
Other countries have chosen similar benchmarks in terms of the year or level of official development assistance.
On the contrary, France considers the financing of "all new projects" in 2010 to be "new and additional".
In other words, there is no reference to the costs of previous years or the total level of official development assistance.
In particular, the total amount of France's assistance to developing countries may remain stable (or even decrease), while pretending to respect its commitment to "new and additional" funds...
Another crucial element, the distribution between donations and loans.
Once again, there is no rule and everyone chooses the calculation method.
Somes, like France, consider that each euro borrowed is equivalent to one euro donated.
Evidently, such a thing is difficult to justify: effort is not comparable.
Although the use of loans may be justified for certain investments that are profitable (often, projects related to the reduction of emissions that are based on more effective technologies), it is falacious to present a euro that is to be recovered as equivalent to one euro being donated ... At most, only the cost of the loan should be computed for its commitments for the loan that grants the loan (i.e. the equivalent of an interest rate).
But France does not find such a reasoning useful: it "respects" its commitment of 420 million euros making a 91.5% of loans (389.8 million euros) and 8.5% of donations (36.1 million euros); consult the EU document to see it by its own eyes.
It is the only country in the Union that uses the loan so frequently.
Germany uses it in 46%, while Denmark, the Netherlands or Slovakia, among others, respect their commitments with 100% donations.
In other words, when the majority of the European Union contributes one euro to fulfill its commitment, France only contributes ten cents and considers that the effort is comparable.
A nice trick!
In short, in its fast start financing, France makes a double abuse: on the one hand, in relation to actions in time and, on the other, in relation to the real financial effort.
Spending only 36.1 million euros, he manages to give the impression that he spends 420 million "new and additional", as he had promised.
The manipulation of the Ministry of Finance (because it seems that it is Bercy who has dealt with arbitration) could have a double negative impact, both within the EU (why were the other Member States going to continue donating when they can simply lend?) and in the negotiations, since the developing countries are not silly...
The pre-financing chapter is relatively short-term minutia in relation to all climate negotiations.
But it could be a grain of extra sand in the immense machinery that is the UNFCCC.
Let's hope it doesn't.
Whatever it may be, it’s essential to learn from this first year of early funding.
In the first place, France could announce a change in its calculation method for the years 2011 and 2012, with the intention of respecting its commitments and in the same way as the other countries of the European Union.
But, especially, France could advance discussions on gambling in relation to long-term financing (another essential chapter that is currently being discussed): we will not get rid of the question of "additional character" or the method of counting loans.
France could change its position to become as progressive in relation to this issue as it is to others.
Therefore, we have the hope of the new Minister of the Environment, Mrs Kosciusko-Morizet, who will arrive in Cancun next week.
A surprise announcement on France's fast startups and its vision of "additional character" and the use of loans would contribute to improving the level of international confidence needed to advance negotiations.
Warning of pesticides ingested by children
A 10-year-old boy ingests 81 different chemicals on a day, according to a study published Wednesday by the Générations Futures (the ancient movement for rights and respect for future generations).
The association, which warns that its results "do not have a significant statistical value taking into account the limited number of samples analyzed, but they are illustrative of the problem treated", acquired in supermarkets in Oise and Paris between July and September the foods usually consumed by a child of about ten years in four meals and a snack a day.
These meals taken as a model, which respect the official recommendations (five parts of fresh fruits and vegetables, three of dairy products and 1.5 liters of water a day), as well as treats, were examined in laboratories in search of chemicals, pesticides, heavy metals and other pollutants.
The balance of the dish is overwhelming: 81 chemicals, of which 42 classified as possible or probable carcinogens and five substances classified as safe carcinogens, as well as 37 substances susceptible to acting as endocrine (PE) disruptors, according to studies from various institutions such as the International Cancer Research Center.
"Although, in virtually all cases, the legal limits for each of the chemicals are not exceeded individually, our study clearly shows that consumer exposure to possibly carcinogenic pollutants and/or PE is troubling," the association said.
For breakfast, butter and tea with milk alone contain more than a dozen possible carcinogenic residues and three considered as safe carcinogens, as well as about twenty residues that can impair the hormonal system.
The apple, imported from Brazil, intended as a snack for the school, contains traces of six chemicals, including a fungicide banned in France.
The same applies to green beans from Kenya, included in food, in which the study has detected traces of a potent insecticide banned in the EU.
In addition, Russian steak, canned tuna, and even the loaf of bread or gum were seasoned with pesticides and other chemicals.
In faucet water, analyses uncovered the presence of nitrates and chloroform.
At supper, salmon slice was the most “nutritious” with 34 chemical residues detected and even analysis of the plastic dish used to heat food in the microwave was not completely clean.
The ultimate risk generated by these "pollutant cocktails" for the consumer is "probably underestimated," the study says.
The European Ecology-Les Verts Alliance (EE-LV), for its part, has proposed "in the face of the gravity of the situation" to apply a moratorium on the use of pesticides to curb "the increase in diseases associated with civilization".
In July, a study by the European Food Safety Agency (EASA) indicated the presence of residues of 365 different pesticides identified in fruit and vegetables consumed in Europe, of which 76 in cereals.
